{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in updating react diagnostics; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"updating\n     1\t# Updating, streaming and zoom\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can change a chart's options and data, feed it a live stream, set and follow its zoom, listen to its events and remove it, and you know the rules that keep every one of these calls predictable.\n     4\t\n     5\tA chart function returns a handle with six members:\n     6\t\n     7\t```ts\n     8\tconst chart = line('#sessions', options);\n     9\t\n    10\tchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                               // change options\n    11\tchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 }); // add rows\n    12\tchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);                           // set the visible x range\n    13\tconst off = chart.on('hover', point => console.log(point));        // listen; off() stops\n    14\tchart.diagnostics;                                                 // what is wrong, right now\n    15\tchart.destroy();                                                   // remove the chart\n    16\t```\n    17\t\n    18\tThere is no `resize()`: the chart follows its container by itself.\n    19\t\n    20\t## The rules every call follows\n    21\t\n    22\t1. **Calls apply in the order you make them.** `update({ data })` followed by `append(rows)` appends to the new data.\n    23\t2. **Checking is immediate.** Each call reads and checks its input before it returns. `chart.diagnostics` is up to date as soon as the call returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call.\n    24\t3. **Drawing waits for the next frame.** All the calls made before the next animation frame are drawn once, together. Ten updates in a row cost one drawing.\n    25\t4. **Everything switches together.** Marks, labels, the tooltip, keyboard navigation and the accessibility text move to the new state in the same frame. No frame shows half of one state and half of another.\n    26\t\n    27\t## update: change options\n    28\t\n    29\t`update(patch)` takes any options of the chart's type. For each key:\n    30\t\n    31\t| In the patch | Effect |\n    32\t|---|---|\n    33\t| Present with a value | Replaces the old value whole. There is no deep merge: `y: { field: 'revenue' }` replaces the whole `y`, label and unit included. |\n    34\t| Present as `undefined` | Returns that option to its default. |\n    35\t| Absent | Stays as it is. |\n    36\t\n    37\tA new `data` replaces the data, including anything appended. Field options that only change presentation (`domain`, `format`, `tickFormat`, `title`, colours) do not make the chart read the rows again.\n    38\t\n    39\t[example: update](../examples/update.html)\n    40\t\n    41\t## append and keep\n    42\t\n    43\t`append(rows, { keep })` adds rows at the end. It is the fast path for live data: earlier rows are not read again.\n    44\t\n    45\t- `keep` is the number of distinct x values to retain; older ones are dropped. Without it, every row is kept.\n    46\t- Rows should be at or after the last x. Rows before it are still accepted, through the slower full update, with warning [AX151](diagnostics.md#ax151).\n    47\t- When the view is zoomed to the latest edge, it follows new data. Zoomed elsewhere, it stays where it is.\n    48\t- Appended rows are checked like any others, and a `strict` chart throws on a bad one.\n    49\t- Data arriving does not speak: announcements happen only when the reader moves, and keyboard focus stays on the same point until that point leaves the window.\n    50\t\n    51\t[example: stream](../examples/stream.html)\n    52\t\n    53\t### Pause and resume\n    54\t\n    55\tOnce a chart has been appended to, its toolbar shows a Pause control. While paused, new rows are taken and checked but not shown, and the view does not move, so a reader can study a moment of a live chart. Resume shows everything that arrived. Pause is in the chart's own toolbar for the reader; there is no method for it.\n    56\t\n    57\t## zoom\n    58\t\n    59\t`zoom([from, to])` sets the visible x range, in the same kind of values as your x field (date strings, numbers, `Date` objects). `zoom(null)` shows everything. A range that cannot be read is reported and ignored; `zoom` never throws.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe reader can zoom too, on date, time and number x axes: with the Zoom in and Zoom out buttons (and, once zoomed, Pan left, Pan right and Reset zoom), by dragging across the plot, with `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift with the arrows while the plot has focus, with the wheel while the plot has focus (never with Ctrl or Meta held, so browser zoom keeps working), and with pinch and two-finger pan when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. A one-finger drag always scrolls the page. Every one of these works with single clicks or keys alone; no gesture is required.\n    62\t\n    63\tEvery change of the visible range, by your call or by the reader, fires the `zoom` event.\n    64\t\n    65\t[example: zoom](../examples/zoom.html)\n    66\t\n    67\t## on: events\n    68\t\n    69\t`on(event, listener)` returns a function that removes the listener.\n    70\t\n    71\t| Event | Payload | When |\n    72\t|---|---|---|\n    73\t| `hover` | A point, or `null` when the pointer leaves | The datum under the pointer, or the keyboard's point, changes. |\n    74\t| `select` | A point | The reader clicks a point or presses Enter or Space on it. |\n    75\t| `zoom` | `[from, to]`, or `null` for the whole range | The visible range changes. |\n    76\t\n    77\tIn event payloads, date and time x values currently arrive as epoch milliseconds (a calendar date as its UTC midnight), whatever form your data used; `new Date(value)` reads either form, as the zoom example does.\n    78\t\n    79\tA point is `{ series, x, y, row, index }`: the series name (the slice label on a donut), the x value, the value (`null` for no data), your own row object (with typed columns, an object built from the columns at that index), and the row's index in your data.\n    80\t\n    81\t## diagnostics\n    82\t\n    83\t`chart.diagnostics` lists the diagnostics for the current options and data. It is current as soon as a create, `update` or `append` returns. Diagnostics found later, while drawing (contrast, labels that stop fitting after a resize, a formatter that throws), are added when found. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md).\n    84\t\n    85\t## destroy\n    86\t\n    87\t`destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects every observer and listener, removes what the chart added to its element, and makes later calls do nothing. Call it when the chart's element leaves your page. A chart whose element leaves the page without `destroy()` lets go of the page's listeners until the element returns.\n    88\t\n    89\t## Strict mode\n    90\t\n    91\tWith `strict: true`, any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call throws from that call, and the chart keeps its last valid state. The thrown error is named `AxyloStrictError`, its message starts with the first diagnostic, and its `diagnostics` property has all of them. A rejected create leaves nothing behind in the page.\n    92\t\n    93\tHints never throw. Neither do diagnostics that can only arise later, while drawing: those are reported when found.\n    94\t\n    95\t[example: strict](../examples/strict.html)\n    96\t\n    97\tStrict mode suits tests and development builds, where a bad row should stop you. In production a chart that shows a gap and tells the reader is usually better than one that throws.\nreact\n     1\t# React\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can use Axylo's React components, choose between props and the imperative handle, keep re-renders cheap, handle events and errors, and render on the server.\n     4\t\n     5\t`@axylo/react` works with React 18 and 19. It is a thin layer: each component creates an ordinary Axylo chart in an empty `<div>` and turns prop changes into `update` calls.\n     6\t\n     7\t```sh\n     8\tnpm install axylo @axylo/react\n     9\t```\n    10\t\n    11\t## The components\n    12\t\n    13\t`Line`, `Area`, `Bar`, `Scatter` and `Donut` take the same options as `line()`, `area()`, `bar()`, `scatter()` and `donut()`, as props. A few props belong to the component itself:\n    14\t\n    15\t| Prop | What it does |\n    16\t|---|---|\n    17\t| `id`, `className`, `style`, `data-*` | Attributes of the container `<div>`. The container always has the class `axylo`. |\n    18\t| `ref` | Receives `{ chart }`: the chart's handle, or `null` before mount, after unmount and on the server. |\n    19\t| `onHover`, `onSelect` | The `hover` and `select` events. |\n    20\t| `onZoom` | The `zoom` event. Not on `Donut`. |\n    21\t\n    22\t`aria-*` props are not passed to the container: the chart names itself through `title` or `labelledBy`. An `aria-label` prop is reported as an option the chart does not know ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)).\n    23\t\n    24\t[example: react-line](../examples/react-line.tsx)\n    25\t\n    26\t## Props and what they cost\n    27\t\n    28\tOn each render the component compares the new props with the ones it last applied, and calls `chart.update` with only what changed.\n    29\t\n    30\t- `data` is compared by identity. A new array, even with the same rows, is read again. Keep data in state, memoise it with `useMemo`, or build it outside the component. In development, the same content arriving as a new array three renders running prints a hint, `[axylo/react] … memoize it`, to the console.\n    31\t- Every other prop is compared by content, so an inline `x={{ field: 'day', type: 'date' }}` or `reference={[{ y: 90, label: 'Target' }]}` costs nothing when it has not changed. Do not change an option object in place: a value changed under the same reference is not seen.\n    32\t- A prop you remove is sent as `undefined`, which returns that option to its default.\n    33\t- Event handlers are replaced without recreating the chart, so inline arrow functions are fine. `onDiagnostic` too.\n    34\t\n    35\t## Props or the handle\n    36\t\n    37\tProps describe what the chart shows. The handle is for what props cannot express: `append` for live data, `zoom` from your own controls, and reading `diagnostics`.\n    38\t\n    39\t[example: react-stream](../examples/react-stream.tsx)\n    40\t\n    41\tWhen `data` is a prop, a new `data` replaces whatever was appended, so a streaming component keeps its first rows stable (in state, as above) and appends through the ref.\n    42\t\n    43\t## Lifecycle\n    44\t\n    45\tThe chart is created when the component mounts and destroyed when it unmounts. It is correct under StrictMode's setup, cleanup and setup again. A chart hidden by Suspense is not destroyed: when it is shown again it is the same chart, with its appended rows and zoom.\n    46\t\n    47\tThe component creates the chart during React's layout phase, so a parent's layout effect or callback ref already finds `ref.current.chart` with the committed options, and calls made there apply in the order React committed them.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Errors and error boundaries\n    50\t\n    51\tA chart does not throw for bad data: it draws what it can, shows the reader what it could not, and reports diagnostics. Only a `strict` chart throws, when a create or an update finds an error or a warning. In a component that happens during React's commit, so the nearest error boundary catches it like any render error, with the `AxyloStrictError` and its `diagnostics`.\n    52\t\n    53\tTo log diagnostics without throwing, pass `onDiagnostic`.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Server rendering\n    56\t\n    57\tThe component files are marked `'use client'`. On the server a component renders only its empty container, `<div class=\"axylo\"></div>` with your `id`, `className`, `style` and `data-*` attributes, and never reaches the chart code, which needs a browser. The chart is created after hydration. Give the container its size with CSS, so the page does not move when the chart appears: the chart fills the container's width, and with no height set its plot is 16:9 of the width, between 160 and 480 px.\n    58\t\n    59\tTo check data on the server, use `validate()` from `axylo`, which runs without a DOM. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\ndiagnostics\n     1\t# Diagnostics\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can read an Axylo diagnostic, collect diagnostics in your own code, make a chart throw on them, and check a chart's data in Node or in a test before anything is drawn. Every code has its own page, listed at the end.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Reading one\n     6\t\n     7\tWhen Axylo cannot draw something as given, it prints one line to the console:\n     8\t\n     9\t```text\n    10\t[axylo AX103] \"Conversion\": Field \"rate\" has 1 non-finite value at row 1 (NaN). Check the calculation that produced it or send null for no data. Shown as gaps.\n    11\t```\n    12\t\n    13\t| Part | Here |\n    14\t|---|---|\n    15\t| Code | `AX103`. Stable across releases; each has a page. |\n    16\t| Chart | `\"Conversion\"`: the chart's title, or its element id when it has none. |\n    17\t| What and where | The field, how many values, and the first rows (up to ten), numbered in your own data as you passed it. |\n    18\t| Fix | What to change. |\n    19\t| Outcome | What the chart did instead: here, gaps. |\n    20\t\n    21\t## Levels and who is told\n    22\t\n    23\t| Level | Means | Console | The reader |\n    24\t|---|---|---|---|\n    25\t| Error | The chart, or a series, cannot be drawn. | Every build, once per code per page | The chart shows its error state, or says which series could not be drawn |\n    26\t| Warning | Something is not drawn as given. | Every build, once per code per page | When values were left out or changed, a note in the figure: \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" |\n    27\t| Hint | Advice for you; the chart is drawn as given. | Development builds only | Usually nothing. Two hints go with a visible change: a plot too small to draw shows its summary instead ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), and hidden labels get a note ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)) |\n    28\t\n    29\tErrors and warnings print with `console.warn`, hints with `console.info`. The note to the reader is in every build, inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, so a production chart is never silently wrong.\n    30\t\n    31\t## Collecting them in your code\n    32\t\n    33\tEach diagnostic is an object:\n    34\t\n    35\t```ts\n    36\tinterface Diagnostic {\n    37\t  code: `AX${number}`;           // 'AX103'\n    38\t  level: 'error' | 'warning' | 'hint';\n    39\t  message: string;               // what is wrong, where, and how to fix it\n    40\t  chart: string;                 // the chart's title, else its element id\n    41\t  field?: string;                // when it is about a field\n    42\t  rows?: readonly number[];      // first rows, in your data, at most 10\n    43\t  count?: number;                // how many values or rows in all\n    44\t}\n    45\t```\n    46\t\n    47\t`chart.diagnostics` lists them for the current options and data, up to date as soon as a create, `update` or `append` returns. `onDiagnostic` is called once for each diagnostic the chart newly finds, in every build and at every level, so it is the place to send them to your error tracking. A listener that throws is reported once and otherwise ignored; it cannot break the chart.\n    48\t\n    49\t[example: diagnostics-listener](../examples/diagnostics-listener.html)\n    50\t\n    51\t## When they are found\n    52\t\n    53\tMost diagnostics are found while a call checks its input: the create, `update` or `append` call. Those are in `chart.diagnostics` when the call returns. A few can only be found later, while drawing: low contrast after the page's CSS changes ([AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141)), a plot too small ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), labels that stop fitting after a resize ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)), more bars than pixels ([AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132)), a formatter that throws ([AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160)), and a label element that does not exist. They are reported when found, through the same console line and `onDiagnostic`.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Strict mode\n    56\t\n    57\t`strict: true` makes the chart throw on any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. The chart keeps its last valid state; a rejected create leaves nothing in the page. The error is named `AxyloStrictError`, and its `diagnostics` property has every diagnostic of the rejected call. Hints never throw, and neither do diagnostics found later while drawing. See the example in [Updating](updating.md#strict-mode).\n    58\t\n    59\t## validate() in Node and in tests\n    60\t\n    61\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as creating a chart (options, data, view, name) without a DOM, and returns the diagnostics a created chart would report from its call. It takes the same options as the chart type, so it type-checks the same way. Importing `axylo` in Node does not touch the DOM.\n    62\t\n    63\tUse it on a server, in a build step, or in a coding agent's loop:\n    64\t\n    65\t[source: examples/node/validate.ts](../examples/node/validate.ts)\n    66\t\n    67\tOr in a unit test, so that a change in the data a query returns fails the build instead of a dashboard:\n    68\t\n    69\t[source: examples/node/chart-data.test.ts](../examples/node/chart-data.test.ts)\n    70\t\n    71\tBoth files are run by this documentation's own tests. `validate()` cannot find what only a page can show: the diagnostics listed under \"When they are found\" need a browser.\n    72\t\n    73\t## Every code\n    74\t\n    75\t[table: diagnostic codes](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in theming accessibility conformance; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in theming accessibility conformance; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"theming\n     1\t# Theming\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can change a chart's colours and type with CSS custom properties, give a series the same colour on every chart, and know what Axylo does in dark mode, in forced-colours mode and in print, and why its default palette looks the way it does.\n     4\t\n     5\tThere is no theme object and no palette option. A chart takes its font and text colour from the page, as any element does, and everything else from CSS custom properties that you can set on the chart, on any ancestor, or on `:root`.\n     6\t\n     7\t## The custom properties\n     8\t\n     9\tEach colour property falls back to a default from Axylo's stylesheet, with one value for light charts and one for dark. The defaults below are read from the built stylesheet when this site is built.\n    10\t\n    11\t[table: theme tokens](../data/tokens.mjs)\n    12\t\n    13\tValues are any CSS colour, including `var()` references; a chart follows a variable when it changes, without a call. Colours of marks should keep at least 3:1 against the background behind the chart, and text 4.5:1. When the series colours fall below 3:1 against the background the chart actually sits on, Axylo raises [AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141).\n    14\t\n    15\t[example: theme-tokens](../examples/theme-tokens.html)\n    16\t\n    17\t## One colour per series name\n    18\t\n    19\tThe palette assigns colours by position: the first series gets the first colour. On a dashboard where charts show different subsets, \"Referral\" would change colour from chart to chart. `colors` pins a colour to a series name, so the same series looks the same everywhere:\n    20\t\n    21\t```ts\n    22\tconst colors = { Direct: 'var(--brand-direct)', Referral: 'var(--brand-referral)' };\n    23\tline('#a', { ...options, colors });\n    24\t```\n    25\t\n    26\tSeries not named in `colors` take palette colours. A value the browser does not accept as a colour is replaced by the series' palette colour.\n    27\t\n    28\t[example: theme-colors](../examples/theme-colors.html)\n    29\t\n    30\t## Dark mode\n    31\t\n    32\tEach chart decides on its own whether it is light or dark, from the page around it:\n    33\t\n    34\t1. If `--axylo-background` is set, from that colour's lightness.\n    35\t2. Otherwise from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`: `light` or `dark`; for `light dark`, the scheme the browser is using there.\n    36\t3. Otherwise from the inherited text colour: light text means a dark page.\n    37\t\n    38\tSo a page that sets `color-scheme`, a page whose theme switch only changes classes and colours, and a light-only page on a device set to dark mode (which stays light) all get the right defaults. The chart repaints within a frame when the colour scheme, forced colours or the contrast preference change, and when the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes.\n    39\t\n    40\t[example: theme-toggle](../examples/theme-toggle.html)\n    41\t\n    42\t## Forced colours, high contrast and print\n    43\t\n    44\tIn forced-colours mode (Windows High Contrast and similar) every paint becomes a system colour, the colours you set included, and they come back when the mode ends. Series are then told apart by their markers and dashes. Under `prefers-contrast: more`, and in forced colours, lines also differ by dash.\n    45\t\n    46\tMarks are drawn as SVG by default, so they stay sharp at any zoom and in print. There is no print stylesheet in 1.0, and printing has not been tested; the toolbar prints as it appears on screen.\n    47\t\n    48\t## The default palette\n    49\t\n    50\t[table: palette](../../../packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts)\n    51\t\n    52\tEight colours, in the order blue, orange, green, purple, red, cyan, magenta, slate, with the same family in the same slot in light and dark charts. They were fitted to measurable limits, not chosen by eye:\n    53\t\n    54\t- each colour is at least 3:1 against the default light and dark backgrounds;\n    55\t- every pair stays apart (OKLab difference of at least 0.10) under normal vision and under simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia, and the first four, which most charts use, further apart still;\n    56\t- they stay clear of the focus, reference and muted colours;\n    57\t- neighbouring colours stay apart as translucent and solid fills.\n    58\t\n    59\tColour is never the only cue: lines and areas also carry marker shapes, adjacent fills are separated by background, and the legend and tooltip repeat the cues. The palette is a proposal that meets those limits; whether it is pleasant to look at is still open. The reasoning, the measurements and what was tried are in [decision 0003](../../../docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md).\n    60\t\n    61\t## Internal properties\n    62\t\n    63\tThe stylesheet also uses a few custom properties that are not for authors. They are listed so that you can recognise them in the developer tools; setting them is unsupported.\n    64\t\n    65\t[table: internal tokens](../data/tokens.mjs)\naccessibility\n     1\t# Accessibility\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know what a reader using a screen reader, a keyboard, magnification, high contrast or with a colour-vision deficiency gets from an Axylo chart, which keys do what, what you as the author must supply, and what has not been verified.\n     4\t\n     5\tAccessibility is on by default and cannot be forgotten: there is no module to import and no option to turn on.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What each reader gets\n     8\t\n     9\t| Reader | What the chart provides |\n    10\t|---|---|\n    11\t| Screen reader | The chart is a named group (or, when compact, a named image) with a generated summary: chart type, series, the x range with its time zone, the value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any values left out. The plot is one native slider-like control whose value text reads the point in focus: its x, every visible series' value with its unit, and \"Point 4 of 120\". \"View as table\" opens the data as an HTML table with a caption, column headers with units, and row headers. Changes of state (loading, empty, error) are announced once, politely; arriving data is not announced. |\n    12\t| Keyboard | Two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot and the toolbar. Every point is reachable in order, the series can be switched, the ends are one key away, and Tab always leaves the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1. |\n    13\t| Low vision | Text is real HTML at 12 px or more, with 4.5:1 contrast; marks, axes and focus indicators 3:1. Text follows browser zoom and user text-spacing settings. Labels never overlap; when one cannot be shown, the chart says so. The tooltip can be hovered, stays inside the window, and Escape closes it. |\n    14\t| Colour-vision deficiency | Eight colours kept apart under simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia, and never colour alone: each line has its own marker shape, repeated in the legend and tooltip; adjacent fills are separated by background. |\n    15\t| High contrast | Forced-colours mode uses system colours, and lines also differ by dash; so they do under `prefers-contrast: more`. |\n    16\t| Reduced motion | Nothing is animated on first render. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions at all. |\n    17\t| Touch and single pointer | Every control is at least 24 × 24 px. Zoom works by single clicks on buttons as well as by dragging. A one-finger drag scrolls the page. |\n    18\t\n    19\tThe legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state. When a series is hidden, its entry keeps full-contrast text, struck through.\n    20\t\n    21\t## The keyboard map\n    22\t\n    23\tThe plot is a single native range input laid over the chart. Its keys:\n    24\t\n    25\t| Key | In the plot |\n    26\t|---|---|\n    27\t| Left, Right | Previous or next x, reading every visible series there. Scatter: the previous or next point in x, then y order. Donut: the previous or next slice. |\n    28\t| Home, End | The first or last point. |\n    29\t| Page Up, Page Down | A larger step. |\n    30\t| Up, Down | Narrow to one series and move to the previous or next series. Donut: the next or previous slice. |\n    31\t| Escape | Close the tooltip; pressed again, return from one series to all. Otherwise left to the page, so a chart in a dialog does not swallow it. |\n    32\t| Enter, Space | Select the point: the chart fires its `select` event. |\n    33\t| `+`, `-`, `0` | Zoom in, zoom out, show everything (date, time and number x). |\n    34\t| Shift with Left or Right | Pan the zoomed view. |\n    35\t| Wheel | Zoom, only while the plot has focus and never with Ctrl or Meta held. |\n    36\t\n    37\tThe toolbar (legend toggles, Zoom in, Zoom out, Pan left, Pan right, Reset zoom, Pause, View as table; each appears when it applies) is one tab stop. Inside it the arrow keys move between controls, Home and End go to the first and last, and Enter or Space presses the control. The open table adds the table's scroll region and its pager (previous page, next page and a \"Jump to\" field) to the tab order.\n    38\t\n    39\tThis pattern was chosen by reasoning and by inspecting Chrome's accessibility tree, not by testing with screen readers; see [decision 0001](../../../docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md) and the limits below. The plot's accessible description gives a short version of this map.\n    40\t\n    41\t## What you must supply\n    42\t\n    43\tAxylo can describe the data; only you know what it means.\n    44\t\n    45\t- **A meaningful name.** Give every chart a `title`, or, when the card around it already has a visible heading, `labelledBy` with that heading's id. A generated name (\"Line chart of sessions by day\") is a fallback and raises [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140). A title can be hidden visually with `title: { text, visible: false }`, and its heading level set with `level` (2 to 6, default 3) to fit your page's outline.\n    46\t- **Series names.** For wide rows, give each field a `label`; for long rows, the series field's values are the names. They are read for every point.\n    47\t- **Units.** Set `unit` (and `label`) on value fields, so \"412\" is read as \"412 ms\".\n    48\t- **Context.** `description` adds what the data cannot say (\"The spike on 12 March was the payment provider outage.\") to the generated summary. It never removes the time zone or the statement of values left out.\n    49\t- **The page's structure.** Headings, landmarks and the order of content around the chart are yours.\n    50\t- **Text in other languages.** Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. When the page's language is not English, built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"`.\n    51\t\n    52\t[example: a11y-card](../examples/a11y-card.html)\n    53\t\n    54\t## Limits\n    55\t\n    56\tAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast and text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence planned for that in 1.0 is a rehearsal by the developer on five screen-reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made.\n    57\t\n    58\tAs of this documentation:\n    59\t\n    60\t- No screen reader has been run against these charts yet. The plot's pattern ([decision 0001](../../../docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md)) and the rules for the tooltip and the focused point's text ([decision 0004](../../../docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md)) are unrehearsed.\n    61\t- Only Chrome on desktop has been tested. Firefox, Safari (macOS and iOS) and phones have not been verified.\n    62\t- The tooltip's placement, the pan buttons, zoom on category axes and the table's overlay are being finished, and may differ from this page until they are.\n    63\t- Right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored.\n    64\t- Built-in text ships in English only; other languages go through `messages`.\n    65\t\n    66\t## Conformance\n    67\t\n    68\tThe WCAG 2.2 AA conformance table is produced separately, from test evidence: see [Conformance table](conformance.md).\nconformance\n     1\t# Conformance table\n     2\t\n     3\t**Placeholder. This page is not the conformance table.**\n     4\t\n     5\tThe table that lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion, the test that covers it and what the author must supply (criterion A9 of the specification) is produced separately from the acceptance tests' evidence, and published with each release. It has not been produced yet, and nothing on this page may be read as a claim of conformance.\n     6\t\n     7\tWhen it exists, it will also state the limits of that evidence in the specification's own terms: automated checks and the developer's rehearsal only, no usability testing with disabled users, and the browsers, screen readers and devices that have not been verified.\n     8\t\n     9\tUntil then, [Accessibility](accessibility.md) describes what the charts do and what has not been verified.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in index getting-started data; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in index getting-started data; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"index\n     1\t# Axylo\n     2\t\n     3\tAxylo draws the everyday charts of web apps and dashboards: line, area, bar, scatter and donut, and a compact variant for KPI tiles. You pass rows and name the fields; the chart that comes back is faithful to the data, readable at any width and in light or dark pages, and works with a keyboard and a screen reader. When the data is wrong, Axylo says so, to you in the console and to the reader on the chart, instead of drawing a guess.\n     4\t\n     5\tThis page says what you get, what Axylo leaves out on purpose, and where to read next.\n     6\t\n     7\t[example: bar-target](../examples/bar-target.ts)\n     8\t\n     9\tThat chart is one call with a title, the rows and two field names, plus a target line. Its value axis starts at zero, its long label fits, its colours follow this page's scheme, and it has a legend-free toolbar with \"View as table\". Try Tab, then the arrow keys.\n    10\t\n    11\t## What you get without asking\n    12\t\n    13\t| You get | Where to read more |\n    14\t|---|---|\n    15\t| A title and an accessible name, and a generated summary of the data (range, minimum, maximum, last value, time zone) | [Accessibility](accessibility.md) |\n    16\t| Axes with units, a legend, a tooltip that appears wherever the pointer is, and \"View as table\" | [Getting started](getting-started.md) |\n    17\t| Keyboard access: one tab stop for the plot, one for the controls | [Accessibility](accessibility.md#the-keyboard-map) |\n    18\t| Series told apart by colour and by marker or dash, in every mode | [Theming](theming.md) |\n    19\t| Light and dark taken from the page, forced colours, reduced motion | [Theming](theming.md#dark-mode) |\n    20\t| Sizing from the container, in both directions, with no call from you | [Getting started](getting-started.md#what-you-get-without-asking) |\n    21\t| Gaps for missing values, never a line drawn through them | [Your data](data.md#missing-values) |\n    22\t| Calendar dates that read the same in every time zone | [Your data](data.md#three-kinds-of-time) |\n    23\t| A coded diagnostic with the field, the rows and the fix for anything that cannot be drawn as given | [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md) |\n    24\t\n    25\t## What Axylo does not try to do\n    26\t\n    27\tAxylo has few chart types, each with its own rules for labels, bad data and accessibility. It does not aim for many chart types, deep visual customisation, or exploratory analysis in notebooks. There are no maps, financial charts or network diagrams, and no log scale or second y axis in 1.0. [Stability and releases](stability.md#what-is-not-in-10) lists what is left for later and why.\n    28\t\n    29\tThere is one way to do each thing: one data model, one method per kind of change, and CSS for every colour. There is no `width`, `height`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colour from CSS, and the first render is never animated.\n    30\t\n    31\t## Where to go next\n    32\t\n    33\t- [Getting started](getting-started.md): install, the first chart, and what to check when it looks wrong.\n    34\t- [Your data](data.md): rows and columns, dates and times, missing values, units.\n    35\t- One page per chart type: [line](line.md), [area](area.md), [bar](bar.md), [scatter](scatter.md), [donut](donut.md), [compact](compact.md).\n    36\t- [Updating, streaming and zoom](updating.md), and the [React components](react.md).\n    37\t- [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md): every code, with a page each.\n    38\t- [Options reference](options.md): every option of every chart type.\n    39\t- Coming from another library: [Chart.js](migrate-chartjs.md) or [Recharts](migrate-recharts.md).\n    40\t\n    41\t## About this documentation\n    42\t\n    43\tThe live examples on these pages run the library's built files (the development flavour) from this repository. A test type-checks every example and renders it in Chrome, and fails on any console error or on a diagnostic the example does not say it raises. The library is a pre-release (package version 0.0.0) and some areas are still being finished: the tooltip's placement, the pan buttons, zoom on category axes and the table overlay. Pages that describe those areas follow the specification and say where the built library may still differ.\ngetting-started\n     1\t# Getting started\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can install Axylo, draw a first chart from rows in npm, script-tag or React code, and know the three things to check when a chart looks wrong.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Install\n     6\t\n     7\tWith npm and a bundler:\n     8\t\n     9\t```sh\n    10\tnpm install axylo\n    11\t```\n    12\t\n    13\tFor React, add the adapter. It works with React 18 and 19.\n    14\t\n    15\t```sh\n    16\tnpm install axylo @axylo/react\n    17\t```\n    18\t\n    19\tWith a script tag, load one file from the package's `dist` folder: `axylo.iife.js` is the development build (it prints hints), `axylo.iife.min.js` the production build. Both define one global, `Axylo`. The snippet below loads them from jsDelivr, which serves published npm packages; until Axylo is published there, copy the file from the package to your site instead.\n    20\t\n    21\t```html\n    22\t<script src=\"/js/axylo.iife.min.js\"></script>\n    23\t```\n    24\t\n    25\tThe package has no runtime dependencies. It ships two flavours of the module build, chosen by the `development` export condition: a bundler that resolves that condition gets the development build, which also prints hints; otherwise you get the production build, which prints errors and warnings only. The styles are applied by the library itself without adding a `<style>` element, so there is no CSS file to import. A standalone copy, `axylo/axylo.css`, ships for pages that prefer to load the stylesheet themselves. Whether a page with a strict Content Security Policy needs it has not been tested yet.\n    26\t\n    27\tTypeScript 5.4 or later gives you checked field names; plain JavaScript works the same without them.\n    28\t\n    29\t## The first chart\n    30\t\n    31\tEach of these is complete: the import, the rows, and one call (or one component). They are kept in the repository as files that the package's distribution tests install and render, so what you see here is what is checked.\n    32\t\n    33\tWith npm:\n    34\t\n    35\t[source: examples/first-chart/npm.ts](../../../examples/first-chart/npm.ts)\n    36\t\n    37\tWith a script tag:\n    38\t\n    39\t[source: examples/first-chart/script.html](../../../examples/first-chart/script.html)\n    40\t\n    41\tIn React:\n    42\t\n    43\t[source: examples/first-chart/react.tsx](../../../examples/first-chart/react.tsx)\n    44\t\n    45\tThe same kind of chart, running on this page. Point at it, press Tab to reach the plot and use the arrow keys, or open the table.\n    46\t\n    47\t[example: first-chart](../examples/first-chart.ts)\n    48\t\n    49\tA script-tag page needs nothing more than this:\n    50\t\n    51\t[example: script-tag](../examples/script-tag.html)\n    52\t\n    53\tThe only required options are `data` and the fields: `x` and `y` (and `series` for long rows), or `label` and `value` for a donut. `title` is not required, but a chart without one gets a generated name and warning [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140).\n    54\t\n    55\t## What you get without asking\n    56\t\n    57\t| Feature | What it does |\n    58\t|---|---|\n    59\t| Title and accessible name | `title` is the visible heading and the chart's name for assistive technology. Without it, the name is generated (\"Line chart of sessions by day\") and AX140 is raised. In a dashboard card that already has a heading, use `labelledBy` with the heading's id instead. |\n    60\t| Summary | A generated description: chart type, series, the x range with its time zone, the value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. `description` adds to it. |\n    61\t| Table view | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from your rows, 100 rows per page, with a \"Jump to\" field. |\n    62\t| Keyboard | The plot is one tab stop: arrow keys move through the points and read each one. The legend, zoom, pause and table controls are a second tab stop. See [the keyboard map](accessibility.md#the-keyboard-map). |\n    63\t| Tooltip | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is at that x for every visible series; Escape closes it. |\n    64\t| Colour and more than colour | Up to eight series get eight colours that work in light and dark pages, and each line also carries its own marker shape, repeated in the legend and tooltip. |\n    65\t| Page theme | Font, text colour and light or dark come from the page. The chart repaints when the page switches scheme. |\n    66\t| Size | The chart fills its container's width; with no height set, the plot is 16:9, between 160 and 480 px tall. It follows the container as it resizes, with no call from you. |\n    67\t| States | No rows shows \"No data to show.\" inside the plot, never an empty frame or an invented axis. `state` sets loading, empty and error messages of your own. |\n    68\t| Diagnostics | Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported with a code, the field, the rows and the fix; when values were left out, the reader is told too. |\n    69\t\n    70\tThe four states, each a chart with no rows:\n    71\t\n    72\t[example: states](../examples/states.ts)\n    73\t\n    74\t## When it looks wrong: three things to check\n    75\t\n    76\t**1. Read the diagnostics.** Open the browser console. Every problem Axylo found is printed once per code as `[axylo AX…] \"Chart title\": what is wrong, where, and how to fix it`. The same list is on the chart as `chart.diagnostics`, current as soon as the call returns. Each code has a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). Hints, which are advice rather than problems, print in the development build only.\n    77\t\n    78\t**2. Check which kind of x Axylo read.** A date off by one day almost always means the x values are instants where calendar dates were meant: `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'` is a moment, which is the evening of the 23rd in New York. If the tooltip heading names a time zone, Axylo read instants. Send `'2026-02-24'`, or declare `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. If the x labels are your raw strings in input order, Axylo read categories. [The three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time) explains the rule.\n    79\t\n    80\t**3. Check the values.** Numbers that arrive as strings, a missing day, two rows for the same day, a misspelled field: each has its own code, and `validate()` finds them without drawing, in Node or in a test:\n    81\t\n    82\t```ts\n    83\timport { validate } from 'axylo';\n    84\t\n    85\tconst problems = validate('line', { data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'sessions', series: 'channel' });\n    86\tfor (const d of problems) console.log(d.code, d.level, d.message);\n    87\t```\n    88\t\n    89\t[Your data](data.md) covers each case.\n    90\t\n    91\t## Next\n    92\t\n    93\tRead [Your data](data.md) next: it explains the three shapes rows can take, how dates are read, and what happens to missing values.\ndata\n     1\t# Your data\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can pass the same table as long rows, wide rows or typed columns; say which kind of time your x values are; control what happens to missing values; and give values a unit, a format and a percent scale.\n     4\t\n     5\t## One table, three shapes\n     6\t\n     7\tTake seven numbers: sessions per day for two channels.\n     8\t\n     9\t| day | Direct | Referral |\n    10\t|---|---|---|\n    11\t| 2026-03-02 | 3,120 | 410 |\n    12\t| 2026-03-03 | 3,380 | 455 |\n    13\t| 2026-03-04 | 3,290 | 390 |\n    14\t| 2026-03-05 | 3,510 | 520 |\n    15\t\n    16\tAxylo accepts this table in three shapes. All three produce the same chart, the same table view and the same diagnostics; row numbers in diagnostics and events always refer to your own data in the shape you gave it.\n    17\t\n    18\t### Long rows\n    19\t\n    20\tOne row per x and series, as most SQL queries and APIs return it. `series` names the field that says which series a row belongs to.\n    21\t\n    22\t[example: data-long](../examples/data-long.ts)\n    23\t\n    24\t### Wide rows\n    25\t\n    26\tOne row per x, one field per series. `y` is a list of fields, and there is no `series`. Passing both is a type error, and at run time it raises [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150).\n    27\t\n    28\t[example: data-wide](../examples/data-wide.ts)\n    29\t\n    30\t### Typed columns\n    31\t\n    32\tOne array per field under `columns`, all of the same length; different lengths are [AX100](diagnostics.md#ax100). This is the shape for large data. In a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array`, `NaN` means \"no value\" and raises nothing.\n    33\t\n    34\t[example: data-columns](../examples/data-columns.ts)\n    35\t\n    36\t## Fields\n    37\t\n    38\tA field is a key of your rows, written as a string, or an object when it needs more:\n    39\t\n    40\t```ts\n    41\tx: 'day'\n    42\ty: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'visits' }\n    43\t```\n    44\t\n    45\tThe object form adds a label (the axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements), a unit, formats, a scale, a missing-value rule and author bounds. The [options reference](options.md#value-fields) lists them all.\n    46\t\n    47\tWith TypeScript, field names are checked against your row type. A misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, or wide `y` together with `series` does not compile. With untyped rows (from `fetch().json()`, say) the same mistakes are reported at run time: a field that no row has is [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), and the message lists the fields that exist and the closest match.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Three kinds of time\n    50\t\n    51\tDates are where charts most often go wrong without anyone noticing. Axylo keeps three kinds apart and never mixes them silently.\n    52\t\n    53\t| Kind | Looks like | Means | Shown |\n    54\t|---|---|---|---|\n    55\t| Calendar date | `'2026-02-24'`, `'2026-02'`, `'2026'` | A day, month or year, wherever you are | The same in every time zone |\n    56\t| Instant | Epoch milliseconds with `type: 'time'`, a `Date`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00Z'`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00+01:00'` | One moment, the same everywhere | In `timeZone` (default: the viewer's zone), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the summary |\n    57\t| Wall-clock time | `'2026-02-24T09:30'` (no offset) | What a clock showed, zone unknown | As written, with hint [AX112](diagnostics.md#ax112) |\n    58\t\n    59\t### The off-by-one-day trap\n    60\t\n    61\tA daily metric is a calendar date. Many back ends send it as an instant at UTC midnight: `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'`, or the epoch milliseconds of that moment. For a viewer in New York that moment is the evening of 23 February, so every daily value appears one day early. In Berlin it is still the 24th. The library is not wrong to show the instant in the viewer's zone; the data said \"this moment\", not \"this day\".\n    62\t\n    63\tThe example below has the same five values sent both ways. On this page your browser's time zone decides how the first chart reads: west of UTC, its first point is on the 23rd.\n    64\t\n    65\t[example: dates](../examples/dates.ts)\n    66\t\n    67\tAxylo notices instants that all fall at the same time of day, whole days apart, and raises hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). The fix is one of:\n    68\t\n    69\t- Send calendar dates: `'2026-02-24'`. `new Date(ms).toISOString().slice(0, 10)` turns a UTC midnight into one.\n    70\t- Keep the values and declare `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Axylo then reads each instant (string, number or `Date`) as the calendar day it falls on in UTC.\n    71\t- If they really are instants, and you want everyone to see UTC, set `x: { field: 'day', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' }`.\n    72\t\n    73\t### How Axylo decides\n    74\t\n    75\tWhen `type` is not given, Axylo looks at every value of the x field and takes the kind most of them have:\n    76\t\n    77\t- `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM` or `YYYY` strings are calendar dates.\n    78\t- Date-time strings with `Z` or an offset, and `Date` objects, are instants.\n    79\t- Date-time strings without an offset are wall-clock times.\n    80\t- Numbers are numbers. A bare number is never taken for an instant: epoch milliseconds need `type: 'time'`, and numbers that look like timestamps raise hint [AX109](diagnostics.md#ax109).\n    81\t- Anything else is a category, kept in the order it first appears.\n    82\t\n    83\tA value that does not match the chosen kind (a day that does not exist, a different format) is [AX107](diagnostics.md#ax107): that row is not drawn, and the reader is told.\n    84\t\n    85\t`timeZone` takes `'local'` (the default, the viewer's zone), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone such as `'Europe/Paris'`. It applies to instants only. A zone the browser does not know is [AX152](diagnostics.md#ax152), and the viewer's zone is used.\n    86\t\n    87\tCalendar periods other than years and months (quarters, ISO weeks) are categories in 1.0: write them as you want them read (`'2025 Q1'`) and set `ordered: true` on the x field so that their labels may be thinned when they do not fit.\n    88\t\n    89\t## Missing values\n    90\t\n    91\tAxylo never draws a missing value as zero and never connects a line across one, unless you ask.\n    92\t\n    93\t| In your data | Drawn | Reported |\n    94\t|---|---|---|\n    95\t| `null`, or `''` | A gap | Nothing: this is how to say \"no data\" |\n    96\t| `NaN` in a typed column | A gap | Nothing |\n    97\t| The field absent from a row (`undefined`) | A gap | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n    98\t| `NaN` or `Infinity` in rows | A gap | Warning [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103), and the reader's note |\n    99\t| No row at all for a step of a regular axis (a missing day) | A gap, as if the row held `null` | Hint [AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105) |\n   100\t| `0` | Zero | Nothing |\n   101\t\n   102\tA value standing alone between two gaps is drawn as a point, so it does not vanish. On a bar chart a missing value gets a small \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". When a warning means values were left out, the chart shows a short note to the reader (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\") inside the figure, in every build.\n   103\t\n   104\tFor counts, where a day without a row means nothing happened, declare it: `missing: 'zero'` turns null and absent values of that field into zeros.\n   105\t\n   106\t[example: missing](../examples/missing.ts)\n   107\t\n   108\tHere are the values an application sends by mistake, all at once. The note under the chart is what the reader sees; the console has one line per code.\n   109\t\n   110\t[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\n   111\t\n   112\t## Numbers sent as strings\n   113\t\n   114\tSome database drivers return counts and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). Axylo treats two cases differently:\n   115\t\n   116\t- A whole field of plain decimal strings (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) is unambiguous: it is parsed and drawn, and hint [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104) asks you to say so with `parse: 'number'`. With that, there is no hint, and TypeScript accepts the string field on a value axis.\n   117\t- A string among numbers, or a string that is not a plain decimal (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`), is a bug upstream. It is not drawn, and it is warning [AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106).\n   118\t\n   119\t[example: numeric-strings](../examples/numeric-strings.ts)\n   120\t\n   121\t## Order and duplicates\n   122\t\n   123\tRows can come in any order. Line, area and scatter charts are drawn in x order; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n   124\t\n   125\t[example: unsorted](../examples/unsorted.ts)\n   126\t\n   127\tOn a line, area or bar chart one x has one value per series. Two rows with the same x in the same series are error [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108), and that series is not drawn: Axylo does not know whether you meant the sum, the last or the average. The same applies to two donut slices with the same label. On a scatter chart repeated x values, and repeated points, are ordinary data.\n   128\t\n   129\t## Units, formats and percentages\n   130\t\n   131\t| Option on a value field | What it does |\n   132\t|---|---|\n   133\t| `label` | The axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. Default: the field name. |\n   134\t| `unit` | Follows every value in tooltip, table and announcements, and is part of the axis title. |\n   135\t| `format` | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements: `Intl.NumberFormat` options, or a function. Default: the page locale's grouping, the full integer part and up to six significant fraction digits (`123,456.75`). |\n   136\t| `tickFormat` | How axis ticks are written; may be shorter than `format`. Default: compact when it saves space (`50K`). |\n   137\t| `scale: 'percent'` | The values are percentages from 0 to 100. |\n   138\t| `scale: 'ratio'` | The values are fractions from 0 to 1, shown as percentages. |\n   139\t\n   140\tA percent or ratio scale is declared, never guessed from a label. Its axis ends at full scale (100%) only when the largest value is at least half of full scale and not above it; otherwise it ends at the first tick at or above the data, so 4% is not drawn as a sliver of 100%. Numbers and dates are formatted for the `locale` option if given, else the document's language (`<html lang>`), else the browser's. A formatter function that throws is warning [AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160), and the default format is used for that value.\n   141\t\n   142\t[example: units](../examples/units.ts)\n   143\t\n   144\tBar and area value axes always include zero, since a bar's length is its value. A `domain` that leaves zero out is widened, with warning [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). Line and scatter axes fit the data.\n   145\t\n   146\t## What Axylo reads, keeps and copies\n   147\t\n   148\t- **Rows** are read when the chart is created or updated and never changed. Their values are copied into Axylo's own typed arrays. Events hand back your own row object (`point.row`), and every diagnostic names rows by their index in your array.\n   149\t- **Typed columns** of values (`Float64Array`, `Float32Array`) are borrowed: Axylo reads them in place and does not copy them, unless it must change a value (`Infinity`, or `missing: 'zero'`). Do not change a borrowed array in place afterwards; to change the data, pass new arrays with `update({ data })`.\n   150\t- **Appended rows** are read once and added to the chart's own storage; `keep` limits how much is kept. See [Updating, streaming and zoom](updating.md#append-and-keep).\n   151\t\n   152\t## Checking data before you draw\n   153\t\n   154\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as a chart, without a page, and returns the diagnostics. Use it in Node, in a unit test, or in a coding agent's loop before rendering. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
