{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in options migrate-chartjs migrate-recharts stability; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"options\n     1\t# Options reference\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can look up any option of any chart type, any field option, and every member of the chart handle, with its type, its default and what it does. The groups follow the declarations in `packages/axylo/src/public.ts`, which are the source of truth. A test checks that every key in those declarations is on this page and that nothing on this page is missing from them.\n     4\t\n     5\t`Row` is the type of one of your rows. For typed columns it is inferred from the columns.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Which options apply where\n     8\t\n     9\t| Group | line | area | bar | scatter | donut |\n    10\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    11\t| [Common options](#common-options) | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes |\n    12\t| [Encoding](#encoding-line-area-bar) (`x`, `y`, `series`) | yes | yes | yes | – | – |\n    13\t| [Cartesian options](#cartesian-options) (`zoom`, `reference`) | yes | yes | yes | yes | – |\n    14\t| [`compact`](#compact) | yes | yes | yes | – | – |\n    15\t| [`stacked`](#area-options) | – | yes | – | – | – |\n    16\t| [`layout`, `horizontal`](#bar-options) | – | – | yes | – | – |\n    17\t| [Scatter encoding](#scatter-options) | – | – | – | yes | – |\n    18\t| [Donut encoding](#donut-options) (`label`, `value`) | – | – | – | – | yes |\n    19\t\n    20\tAn option that does not apply to a chart type does not compile, and at run time is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150).\n    21\t\n    22\t## Common options\n    23\t\n    24\tOn every chart type.\n    25\t\n    26\t<!-- options: CommonOptions -->\n    27\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    28\t|---|---|---|---|\n    29\t| `data` | `readonly Row[] \\| { columns }` | required | The rows, or typed columns of equal length. See [Your data](data.md). |\n    30\t| `title` | `string \\| Title` | none | Visible heading and accessible name. Without it (and without `labelledBy`) the name is generated, with AX140. |\n    31\t| `labelledBy` | `string` | none | Id of an existing heading that names the chart, for cards that already show a title. |\n    32\t| `subtitle` | `string` | none | A line under the title. |\n    33\t| `description` | `string` | none | Added to the generated summary. The time zone and any omitted values are always stated. |\n    34\t| `state` | `'ready' \\| 'loading' \\| { error: string } \\| { empty: string }` | `'ready'` | Shows a loading, error or empty message inside the plot instead of the data. |\n    35\t| `legend` | `'auto' \\| false \\| 'top' \\| 'right' \\| 'bottom'` | `'auto'` | Where the legend goes, or `false` for none. `'auto'` places it under the plot. A chart with one series has no legend entries. |\n    36\t| `tooltip` | `boolean` | `true` | The pointer tooltip. Keyboard reading is not affected. |\n    37\t| `table` | `boolean` | `true` | The \"View as table\" control. |\n    38\t| `colors` | `Record<string, string>` | none | A CSS colour per series name (per slice label on a donut), the same on every chart. |\n    39\t| `messages` | `Partial<Messages>` | English | Replacements for built-in text. See [Messages](#messages). |\n    40\t| `locale` | `string` | the document's language | Locale for numbers and dates. |\n    41\t| `strict` | `boolean` | `false` | Throw on any error or warning found while checking a call, from that call. |\n    42\t| `onDiagnostic` | `(diagnostic: Diagnostic) => void` | none | Called once for each diagnostic the chart newly finds, in every build. |\n    43\t\n    44\t### Title\n    45\t\n    46\tWhen `title` is an object:\n    47\t\n    48\t<!-- options: Title -->\n    49\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    50\t|---|---|---|---|\n    51\t| `text` | `string` | required | The title. |\n    52\t| `visible` | `boolean` | `true` | `false` keeps it as the accessible name but hides it visually. |\n    53\t| `level` | `2 \\| 3 \\| 4 \\| 5 \\| 6` | `3` | The heading level, to fit the page's outline. |\n    54\t\n    55\t## Encoding (line, area, bar)\n    56\t\n    57\tLong rows name one `y` and a `series`; wide rows give `y` as a list and no `series`. The two forms are exclusive.\n    58\t\n    59\t<!-- options: Encoding -->\n    60\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    61\t|---|---|---|---|\n    62\t| `x` | `XKey<Row> \\| XField` | required | The x field. See [X fields](#x-fields). |\n    63\t| `y` | `ValueField \\| readonly ValueField[]` | required | The value field, or one field per series. See [Value fields](#value-fields). |\n    64\t| `series` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | none | The field whose value names each row's series (long rows only). |\n    65\t\n    66\t## Cartesian options\n    67\t\n    68\tOn line, area, bar and scatter charts.\n    69\t\n    70\t<!-- options: CartesianOptions -->\n    71\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    72\t|---|---|---|---|\n    73\t| `zoom` | `boolean \\| 'touch'` | `true` on date, time and number x | Zoom and pan. `'touch'` also takes pinch and two-finger drag on touch screens. Category x is not zoomable. |\n    74\t| `reference` | `readonly Reference[]` | none | Reference lines and bands. See [Reference](#reference). |\n    75\t\n    76\t### Reference\n    77\t\n    78\t<!-- options: Reference -->\n    79\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    80\t|---|---|---|---|\n    81\t| `x` | `XValue \\| readonly [XValue, XValue]` | none | A vertical line at an x value, or a band between two. |\n    82\t| `y` | `number \\| readonly [number, number]` | none | A horizontal line at a value, or a band between two. |\n    83\t| `label` | `string` | required | Shown beside the line or band, and listed in the summary and the table caption. |\n    84\t\n    85\t## Compact\n    86\t\n    87\t<!-- options: CompactOption -->\n    88\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    89\t|---|---|---|---|\n    90\t| `compact` | `boolean` | `false` | Line, area and bar only. Marks only, as a static image with a text alternative. See [Compact](compact.md). |\n    91\t\n    92\t## Area options\n    93\t\n    94\t<!-- options: AreaOptions -->\n    95\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    96\t|---|---|---|---|\n    97\t| `stacked` | `boolean` | `false` | Stack the series; the tooltip adds the total. |\n    98\t\n    99\t## Bar options\n   100\t\n   101\t<!-- options: BarOptions -->\n   102\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   103\t|---|---|---|---|\n   104\t| `layout` | `'grouped' \\| 'stacked'` | `'grouped'` | Several series side by side, or stacked. |\n   105\t| `horizontal` | `boolean` | `false` | Bars from the left, categories down the side. |\n   106\t\n   107\t## Scatter options\n   108\t\n   109\tScatter takes one `y` (never a list) and an x that is a number, an instant or a date.\n   110\t\n   111\t<!-- options: ScatterOptions -->\n   112\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   113\t|---|---|---|---|\n   114\t| `x` | `XKey<Row> \\| ScatterXField` | required | The x field: like an [X field](#x-fields), with `type` limited to `'number'`, `'time'` or `'date'`, and no `ordered`. |\n   115\t| `y` | `ValueField` | required | The value field. |\n   116\t| `series` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | none | The field that groups points into series. |\n   117\t\n   118\t## Donut options\n   119\t\n   120\t<!-- options: DonutOptions -->\n   121\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   122\t|---|---|---|---|\n   123\t| `label` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | required | The field that names each slice. |\n   124\t| `value` | `ValueField` | required | The value of each slice. Not negative. |\n   125\t\n   126\t## Value fields\n   127\t\n   128\tA value field is a key of `Row` whose values are numbers (or `null`), or an object. A key whose values are strings is accepted only in the object form with `parse: 'number'`.\n   129\t\n   130\t<!-- options: ValueField -->\n   131\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   132\t|---|---|---|---|\n   133\t| `field` | `NumberKey<Row>`, or `StringKey<Row>` with `parse` | required | The key of the row. |\n   134\t| `parse` | `'number'` | none | The field holds numbers as strings (Postgres `bigint`, BigQuery); parse them. |\n   135\t| `label` | `string` | the field name | Axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n   136\t| `unit` | `string` | none | Follows values in tooltip, table and announcements: `'ms'`, `'USD'`. |\n   137\t| `scale` | `'percent' \\| 'ratio'` | none | Percentage data: `'percent'` is 0–100, `'ratio'` is 0–1. Never inferred. |\n   138\t| `format` | `Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| (value: number) => string` | locale grouping, up to 6 significant fraction digits | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n   139\t| `tickFormat` | `Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| (value: number) => string` | compact when it saves space | How axis ticks are written. |\n   140\t| `missing` | `'gap' \\| 'zero'` | `'gap'` | What a missing value means. `'zero'` is for counts where absence means none. |\n   141\t| `domain` | `readonly [number, number]` | from the data | Author bounds. On bar and area, widened to include zero (AX110). |\n   142\t\n   143\t## X fields\n   144\t\n   145\tAn x field is a key of `Row` whose values are numbers, strings or `Date`s, or an object.\n   146\t\n   147\t<!-- options: XField -->\n   148\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   149\t|---|---|---|---|\n   150\t| `field` | `XKey<Row>` | required | The key of the row. |\n   151\t| `type` | `'number' \\| 'time' \\| 'date' \\| 'category'` | inferred | `'date'`: calendar dates; `'time'`: instants; numbers are never inferred to be instants. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time). |\n   152\t| `ordered` | `boolean` | `false` | Category axes: the categories have a natural order, so labels may be thinned when they do not fit. |\n   153\t| `timeZone` | `'local' \\| 'UTC' \\| string` | `'local'` | For instants only: the zone they are shown in. `'local'` is the viewer's. |\n   154\t| `label` | `string` | the field name | Axis title (a date, time or category axis is titled only when you give one). |\n   155\t| `unit` | `string` | none | Follows x values in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n   156\t| `format` | `Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions \\| Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| (value: XValue) => string` | from the data | How x values are written in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n   157\t| `tickFormat` | same as `format` | from the data | How x ticks are written. |\n   158\t| `domain` | `readonly [XValue, XValue]` | from the data | Author bounds for x. |\n   159\t\n   160\tFor scatter charts the x field takes the same keys except `ordered`:\n   161\t\n   162\t<!-- options: ScatterXField -->\n   163\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   164\t|---|---|---|---|\n   165\t| `field` | `XKey<Row>` | required | As above. |\n   166\t| `type` | `'number' \\| 'time' \\| 'date'` | inferred | No `'category'`: a scatter relates two measures. |\n   167\t| `timeZone`, `label`, `unit`, `format`, `tickFormat`, `domain` | as above | as above | As on an x field. |\n   168\t\n   169\t## The chart handle\n   170\t\n   171\tEvery chart function returns a `Chart<Row, Options>`.\n   172\t\n   173\t<!-- options: Chart -->\n   174\t| Member | Type | Meaning |\n   175\t|---|---|---|\n   176\t| `update` | `(patch: Partial<Options>) => void` | Change options: a present key replaces, `undefined` restores the default, an absent key stays. See [Updating](updating.md#update-change-options). |\n   177\t| `append` | `(rows: readonly Row[], options?: { keep?: number }) => void` | Add rows at the end. |\n   178\t| `zoom` | `(domain: readonly [XValue, XValue] \\| null) => void` | Set the visible x range; `null` shows everything. |\n   179\t| `on` | `(event, listener) => () => void` | Listen to `hover`, `select` or `zoom`; returns a function that removes the listener. |\n   180\t| `diagnostics` | `readonly Diagnostic[]` | Diagnostics for the current options and data, current as soon as a call returns. |\n   181\t| `destroy` | `() => void` | Remove the chart and everything it attached. Later calls do nothing. |\n   182\t\n   183\t### append options\n   184\t\n   185\t<!-- options: AppendOptions -->\n   186\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   187\t|---|---|---|---|\n   188\t| `keep` | `number` | all rows | The number of distinct x values to retain; older ones are dropped. |\n   189\t\n   190\t### Events\n   191\t\n   192\t<!-- options: ChartEvents -->\n   193\t| Event | Payload | Meaning |\n   194\t|---|---|---|\n   195\t| `hover` | `Point<Row> \\| null` | The datum under the pointer or the keyboard's point; `null` when it leaves. |\n   196\t| `select` | `Point<Row>` | The reader clicked a point, or pressed Enter or Space on it. |\n   197\t| `zoom` | `readonly [XValue, XValue] \\| null` | The visible range changed; `null` for the whole range. |\n   198\t\n   199\t<!-- options: Point -->\n   200\t| Point key | Type | Meaning |\n   201\t|---|---|---|\n   202\t| `series` | `string` | The series name; on a donut, the slice label. |\n   203\t| `x` | `XValue` | The x value. |\n   204\t| `y` | `number \\| null` | The value; `null` for no data. |\n   205\t| `row` | `Row \\| null` | Your row. With typed columns, an object built from the columns at that index. |\n   206\t| `index` | `number` | The row's index in your data. |\n   207\t\n   208\t### Diagnostic\n   209\t\n   210\t<!-- options: Diagnostic -->\n   211\t| Key | Type | Meaning |\n   212\t|---|---|---|\n   213\t| `code` | `` `AX${number}` `` | Stable code, with a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). |\n   214\t| `level` | `'error' \\| 'warning' \\| 'hint'` | See [levels](diagnostics.md#levels-and-who-is-told). |\n   215\t| `message` | `string` | What is wrong, where, and how to fix it. |\n   216\t| `chart` | `string` | The chart's title, else its element id. |\n   217\t| `field` | `string` | The field, when it is about one. |\n   218\t| `rows` | `readonly number[]` | The first offending rows in your data, at most ten. |\n   219\t| `count` | `number` | How many values or rows in all. |\n   220\t\n   221\t## validate\n   222\t\n   223\t`validate(type, options)` takes `'line'`, `'area'`, `'bar'`, `'scatter'` or `'donut'` and that type's options, runs the checks a created chart runs from its call without a DOM, and returns `Diagnostic[]`. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n   224\t\n   225\t## Messages\n   226\t\n   227\tEvery built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Placeholders in braces are filled in by the chart; an override should keep the ones it needs. The defaults below are read from the library when this site is built.\n   228\t\n   229\t[table: messages](../../../packages/axylo/src/messages/index.ts)\n   230\t\n   231\t## React components\n   232\t\n   233\t`Line`, `Area`, `Bar`, `Scatter` and `Donut` from `@axylo/react` take the chart type's options as props, plus these. See [React](react.md).\n   234\t\n   235\t<!-- options: ReactProps -->\n   236\t| Prop | Type | Meaning |\n   237\t|---|---|---|\n   238\t| `id` | `string` | Attribute of the container. |\n   239\t| `className` | `string` | Added to the container's class `axylo`. |\n   240\t| `style` | `CSSProperties` | Style of the container. |\n   241\t| `data-*` | `string \\| number \\| boolean` | Data attributes of the container. |\n   242\t| `ref` | `Ref<ChartRef>` | Receives `{ chart }`. |\n   243\t| `onHover` | `(point: Point<Row> \\| null) => void` | The `hover` event. |\n   244\t| `onSelect` | `(point: Point<Row>) => void` | The `select` event. |\n   245\t| `onZoom` | `(domain: readonly [XValue, XValue] \\| null) => void` | The `zoom` event. Not on `Donut`. |\n   246\t\n   247\t<!-- options: ChartRef -->\n   248\t| `ChartRef` key | Type | Meaning |\n   249\t|---|---|---|\n   250\t| `chart` | `Chart<Row, Options> \\| null` | The chart's handle; `null` before mount, after unmount and on the server. |\n   251\t\n   252\t## Options that do not exist\n   253\t\n   254\tThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colours from [CSS](theming.md), and motion from the reader's settings: the first render is not animated, and nothing moves under reduced motion. Each of these names, like any unknown key, is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150), which suggests the closest known option.\nmigrate-chartjs\n     1\t# Migrating from Chart.js\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can translate a Chart.js configuration into Axylo for the three everyday tasks below, map Chart.js concepts to Axylo's, and know what Axylo deliberately does differently or does not have.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe Chart.js code on this page is quoted, not retyped, from the configurations used in this project's comparison of ten charting libraries (`research/harness/src/libs/chartjs.js`). They were written against **Chart.js 4.6.1** with **chartjs-adapter-date-fns 3.0.0** and **date-fns 4.4.0**, and each is the least configuration that library needed for the task; comments mark what was required beyond the data. The Axylo versions run live on this page, on the same data.\n     6\t\n     7\t## A multi-series time line\n     8\t\n     9\tChart.js takes parallel arrays (`labels` and one `data` array per dataset), needs a date adapter before a time axis works, and needs `maintainAspectRatio: false` to fill a container of fixed height.\n    10\t\n    11\t[source: Chart.js, task \"trend\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/chartjs.js#L1-L16)\n    12\t\n    13\tIn Axylo the same table is rows and field names. The fixture's x values are epoch milliseconds of UTC midnights, which are calendar days: `type: 'date'` reads them that way, so each value shows on its own day in every time zone. Chart.js shows the same values as local instants, which west of UTC puts every point on the day before. The gap in \"Referral\" (seven `null`s) is a gap in both.\n    14\t\n    15\t[example: migrate-trend](../examples/migrate-trend.ts)\n    16\t\n    17\t## A bar chart with a target line\n    18\t\n    19\tChart.js has no reference line of its own. The usual ways are the separate `chartjs-plugin-annotation` package, or a second dataset of `type: 'line'` repeating the target. The comparison's configuration has neither, so only its bars are quoted here:\n    20\t\n    21\t[source: Chart.js, task \"bars\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/chartjs.js#L18-L25)\n    22\t\n    23\tIn Axylo the target is one option, `reference`, and it is labelled, included in the value axis, and listed in the chart's summary and table caption. Both libraries start these bars at zero; in Axylo a bar or area axis cannot be made to start elsewhere. The long label \"Customer Success & Onboarding\" is fitted by Axylo's remedies (wrapping, then rotating, then truncating with the full text in the tooltip and table), where Chart.js rotates all labels.\n    24\t\n    25\t[example: migrate-target](../examples/migrate-target.ts)\n    26\t\n    27\t## A live-updating series\n    28\t\n    29\tChart.js updates by mutating its arrays and calling `update()`. The comparison's stream task pushes one point and drops the oldest:\n    30\t\n    31\t[source: Chart.js, tasks \"scale\" and \"stream\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/chartjs.js#L45-L62)\n    32\t\n    33\tIn Axylo, `append` adds rows and `keep` bounds the window. A Pause control appears for the reader, the view follows the newest data while it is zoomed to the latest edge, and arriving data is not announced to screen readers.\n    34\t\n    35\t[example: migrate-stream](../examples/migrate-stream.ts)\n    36\t\n    37\tThe comparison also has a tuned Chart.js configuration for large data (`chartjs-tuned.js`: no parsing, the decimation plugin, no animation, no point markers). Axylo has no equivalent options: typed columns and the simplified drawing of dense lines are the defaults.\n    38\t\n    39\t## Concepts side by side\n    40\t\n    41\t| Chart.js | Axylo |\n    42\t|---|---|\n    43\t| `new Chart(canvas, { type: 'line', … })` | `line(target, options)`: one function per chart type; `target` is any element or a selector, not a canvas. |\n    44\t| `data.labels` and `datasets[].data` | `data` as rows, wide rows or typed columns, and field names: `x`, `y`, `series`. See [Your data](data.md). |\n    45\t| `datasets[].label` | The `series` field's values, or `label` on each `y` field. |\n    46\t| `options.plugins.title` | `title` (also the accessible name). |\n    47\t| `scales.x.type: 'time'` and a date adapter | Nothing for date strings; `type: 'time'` for epoch milliseconds, `type: 'date'` for calendar days. No adapter. |\n    48\t| `scales.y.title` | `label` and `unit` on the `y` field. |\n    49\t| `maintainAspectRatio`, `responsive`, canvas size | Nothing: the chart takes its container's size. |\n    50\t| `scales.y.beginAtZero` | Bars and areas always start at zero; lines fit the data. |\n    51\t| `spanGaps` | No equivalent: gaps are never bridged. `missing: 'zero'` for counts. |\n    52\t| `backgroundColor`, `borderColor` | The default palette, CSS custom properties, or `colors` by series name. See [Theming](theming.md). |\n    53\t| `plugins.legend.position` | `legend: 'top' \\| 'right' \\| 'bottom' \\| false`. |\n    54\t| `plugins.tooltip` (`mode`, `intersect`) | On by default, nearest x wherever the pointer is; `tooltip: false` turns it off. |\n    55\t| `chartjs-plugin-annotation` | `reference: [{ y, label }]` or `{ x: [from, to], label }`. |\n    56\t| Mutate `chart.data`, then `chart.update()` | `chart.update(patch)` for options and data, `chart.append(rows, { keep })` for new rows. |\n    57\t| `onClick`, `onHover` | `chart.on('select', …)`, `chart.on('hover', …)`. |\n    58\t| `animation` | No option: the first render is never animated. |\n    59\t| `parsing: false`, decimation | No options: typed columns and simplified drawing are the defaults. |\n    60\t| `chart.destroy()` | `chart.destroy()`. |\n    61\t\n    62\t## What is deliberately different\n    63\t\n    64\t- **Accessible without work.** A Chart.js chart is a canvas, and making it accessible is left to you. An Axylo chart has a name, a summary, keyboard access and a table view, with no option.\n    65\t- **Bad data is reported.** Chart.js plots `'40'` among numbers as 40 and says nothing. Axylo leaves it out, tells the reader, and prints a diagnostic with the field and row ([AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106)).\n    66\t- **Time zones are named.** Instants are shown in a zone that the tooltip heading and the summary name, and calendar dates are not shifted.\n    67\t- **Fewer chart types.** Line, area, bar, scatter, donut and the compact variant. No radar, polar area, bubble or mixed charts in 1.0.\n    68\t- **Less to configure.** No plugin API, no per-point styles, no custom tooltip renderer, no animation settings, no log scale or second y axis in 1.0.\n    69\t- **Your data is not watched.** Changing an array in place is not seen; call `update` or `append`. Typed columns are borrowed and must not be changed in place.\nmigrate-recharts\n     1\t# Migrating from Recharts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can translate a Recharts chart into an `@axylo/react` component for the three everyday tasks below, map Recharts concepts to Axylo's, and know what Axylo deliberately does differently or does not have.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe Recharts code on this page is quoted, not retyped, from the configurations used in this project's comparison of ten charting libraries (`research/harness/src/libs/recharts.jsx`). They were written against **Recharts 3.11.0** with **React 19.3.1**, and each is the least configuration that library needed for the task. Recharts has no default palette and no title component, so the comparison used the colours of Recharts' own examples. The `mount` helper renders the element into the harness's container. The Axylo versions run live on this page, on the same data.\n     6\t\n     7\t## A multi-series time line\n     8\t\n     9\tRecharts takes one row object per x with one key per series, one `<Line>` per series with its colour, and a tick formatter, because a numeric time axis prints raw epoch numbers without one.\n    10\t\n    11\t[source: Recharts, task \"trend\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/recharts.jsx#L1-L29)\n    12\t\n    13\tIn Axylo one component holds the whole chart. Long rows with a `series` field replace one `<Line>` per series; wide rows would work too, with `y` as a list. The palette, the title, the axis titles, the date formatting, the legend and the tooltip need no code. `type: 'date'` reads the fixture's UTC-midnight milliseconds as calendar days.\n    14\t\n    15\t[example: recharts-trend](../examples/recharts-trend.tsx)\n    16\t\n    17\t## A bar chart with a target line\n    18\t\n    19\tRecharts draws a target with its `ReferenceLine` component; the comparison's configuration did not use it, so only its bars are quoted here:\n    20\t\n    21\t[source: Recharts, task \"bars\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/recharts.jsx#L31-L46)\n    22\t\n    23\tIn Axylo the target is the `reference` prop, labelled, included in the value axis and listed in the summary and table caption. The long label is fitted instead of dropped (in the comparison, Recharts removed that label and left a bar with no name), and the chart is titled.\n    24\t\n    25\t[example: recharts-target](../examples/recharts-target.tsx)\n    26\t\n    27\t## A live-updating series\n    28\t\n    29\tThe comparison's Recharts stream re-renders with a new array for every point:\n    30\t\n    31\t[source: Recharts, tasks \"scale\" and \"stream\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/recharts.jsx#L81-L93)\n    32\t\n    33\tWith `@axylo/react` the first rows are state, created once, and new points go through the handle with `append`, so earlier rows are not read again. The reader gets a Pause control.\n    34\t\n    35\t[example: recharts-stream](../examples/recharts-stream.tsx)\n    36\t\n    37\tRe-rendering with a new `data` array works too, and is the right thing when the whole data changes; it is the slower path for a stream.\n    38\t\n    39\t## Concepts side by side\n    40\t\n    41\t| Recharts | `@axylo/react` |\n    42\t|---|---|\n    43\t| `<LineChart data>` with `<XAxis>`, `<YAxis>`, `<CartesianGrid>`, `<Tooltip>`, `<Legend>` and one `<Line>` per series | One `<Line data x y series />`. Axes, grid, tooltip and legend are on by default. |\n    44\t| `<ResponsiveContainer>` | Nothing: the chart takes its container's size. |\n    45\t| `dataKey` per series | Long rows with `series`, or `y={['direct', 'organic']}` for wide rows. |\n    46\t| `stroke`, `fill`, `<Cell fill>` | The default palette, CSS custom properties, or `colors` by series name. See [Theming](theming.md). |\n    47\t| `<XAxis type=\"number\" scale=\"time\" tickFormatter>` | Nothing for date strings; `type: 'time'` or `type: 'date'` on `x` for numbers. Dates and numbers are formatted for the page's locale. |\n    48\t| `<YAxis label>` | `label` and `unit` on the `y` field. |\n    49\t| No title component | `title`, which is also the accessible name. |\n    50\t| `<ReferenceLine y>`, `<ReferenceArea>` | `reference={[{ y, label }]}`, or `{ x: [from, to], label }`. |\n    51\t| `<Bar>` with `stackId` | `layout=\"stacked\"` on `<Bar>`; `stacked` on `<Area>`. |\n    52\t| `layout=\"vertical\"` on `<BarChart>` | `horizontal` on `<Bar>`. |\n    53\t| `<Pie innerRadius>` | `<Donut label value />`. |\n    54\t| `isAnimationActive={false}` | Nothing: the first render is never animated. |\n    55\t| `dot={false}` | Nothing: markers appear only when points are sparse. |\n    56\t| `onClick`, `onMouseMove` on the chart | `onSelect`, `onHover`. |\n    57\t| Re-render with a new array to stream | `ref` and `chart.append(rows, { keep })`. |\n    58\t\n    59\t## What is deliberately different\n    60\t\n    61\t- **One component, one options object.** The same options work outside React with `line()`, so a chart can move between a React page and any other page unchanged.\n    62\t- **Defaults that are finished.** A palette that works in light and dark, a title, labels that fit, formatted numbers and dates, all without code.\n    63\t- **Accessible by default, differently.** Recharts' keyboard navigation (on by default in the version tested) uses `role=\"application\"`. Axylo's plot is one native range control with a text value, plus a table view; see [Accessibility](accessibility.md).\n    64\t- **Bad data is reported.** A string among numbers is left out and reported with its row ([AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106)), where Recharts plots `'40'` as 40.\n    65\t- **No composition API.** No custom shapes, custom tooltip content, label render props or `syncId` for linked charts in 1.0. A chart is configured through options, not assembled from components.\n    66\t- **Fewer chart types.** Line, area, bar, scatter, donut and the compact variant. No radar, radial bar, treemap, funnel or composed charts in 1.0.\nstability\n     1\t# Stability and releases\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know what Axylo promises between releases, which browsers and tools it supports, under what licence it ships, and what is not in 1.0.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Status\n     6\t\n     7\tThis documentation describes Axylo 1.0, which has not been released. The library in this repository is a pre-release (package version 0.0.0) and has not been published to npm. Until 1.0 is tagged, anything may change.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Versions\n    10\t\n    11\tAxylo follows semantic versioning:\n    12\t\n    13\t| Release | May contain |\n    14\t|---|---|\n    15\t| Patch (1.0.1) | Fixes that do not change documented behaviour, except to make it match the documentation. |\n    16\t| Minor (1.1.0) | New options, chart features and diagnostics, and deprecations. Nothing documented stops working. |\n    17\t| Major (2.0.0) | Removals and other breaking changes, at most one major version a year. |\n    18\t\n    19\t**Deprecation.** A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and at least six months, and its documentation says what to use instead. The documented examples of the previous release must still compile and run against each release candidate.\n    20\t\n    21\t**What the promise covers.** The functions and types exported by `axylo` and `@axylo/react` (the declarations in `packages/axylo/src/public.ts`), the diagnostic codes and their levels, the CSS custom properties documented on [Theming](theming.md), and the keys of `messages`. Match diagnostics on `code`; messages are written for people.\n    22\t\n    23\t**What it does not cover.** The chart's internal DOM structure and class names, the custom properties listed as internal, the roles the plot uses to talk to assistive technology (chosen without screen-reader rehearsal, and changeable in a minor release if rehearsal shows a better pattern), and the exact pixels of the drawing.\n    24\t\n    25\t## Supported environments\n    26\t\n    27\t| Dimension | 1.0 |\n    28\t|---|---|\n    29\t| Browsers | The current and previous major versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on macOS and iOS. |\n    30\t| TypeScript | 5.4 and later. Plain JavaScript is fully supported; types are an aid, not a requirement. |\n    31\t| React | 18 and 19, through `@axylo/react`. |\n    32\t| Node | Importing `axylo` touches no DOM, and `validate()` runs there. Charts themselves need a browser. |\n    33\t| Text direction | Left-to-right pages. Right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored. |\n    34\t| Built-in text | English, replaceable through `messages`. |\n    35\t\n    36\tVerified so far: Chrome on Linux desktop only. Firefox, Safari, Edge, Windows, macOS and phones have not been tested yet; see [Accessibility](accessibility.md#limits).\n    37\t\n    38\t## Licence\n    39\t\n    40\tMIT, for everything: zoom, accessibility and every chart type included. There is no commercial edition and no feature behind a licence key. The library has no runtime dependencies; third-party notices, if any, are checked at each release.\n    41\t\n    42\t## What is not in 1.0\n    43\t\n    44\tNot planned for 1.0, with the reason it can wait:\n    45\t\n    46\t| Not in 1.0 | Why it can wait |\n    47\t|---|---|\n    48\t| More chart types (histogram first, then heatmap, box plot, candlestick, radar, treemap, sankey, gauge, maps) | Each must pass the same acceptance criteria as the five types in 1.0. |\n    49\t| Log and other non-linear scales | They need their own rules for zero and negative values. |\n    50\t| A second y axis | Often misleading; it needs a design that keeps the zero baseline honest. |\n    51\t| Annotations beyond reference lines and bands | Placing free text interacts with label fitting. |\n    52\t| Linked charts (shared cursor, brush) | Needs a small coordination API. |\n    53\t| Export to PNG, SVG or CSV, and print styling | CSV from the table view comes first. |\n    54\t| Server-side rendering to SVG | The drawing model makes it possible later. |\n    55\t| Right-to-left layout mirroring | Stated as a limit in 1.0. |\n    56\t| Quarters and ISO weeks as calendar periods | Treat them as ordered categories in 1.0. |\n    57\t| Nested field paths (`'user.country'`) | Flatten rows before passing them. |\n    58\t| Vue, Svelte and Angular adapters, a custom element | Thin wrappers over the same handle. |\n    59\t| A plugin or custom-mark API | Waits until two real uses show its shape. |\n    60\t| Translations of built-in text | `messages` makes them possible; only English ships. |\n    61\t| Workers, WebGL or WebGPU | Decided from measurements. |\n    62\t\n    63\tNot the audience for 1.0: analysts exploring data in notebooks, designers building bespoke graphics, and teams that need maps, financial charts or network diagrams.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in line area bar scatter donut compact; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in line area bar scatter donut compact; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"line\n     1\t# Line charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when to use a line chart, which options it takes, and how a line is picked, read by keyboard, zoomed and simplified.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tA line shows how a measure changed along an ordered x: sessions per day, latency per minute, revenue per month. Several series compare trends. The x can be calendar dates, instants, wall-clock times, numbers or categories.\n     8\t\n     9\tUse a [bar chart](bar.md) to compare a few categories, an [area chart](area.md) when the total and its parts matter, and a [compact line](compact.md) for a sparkline in a KPI tile.\n    10\t\n    11\t[example: line-time](../examples/line-time.ts)\n    12\t\n    13\t## Options\n    14\t\n    15\t```ts\n    16\timport { line } from 'axylo';\n    17\tline(target, { data, x, y, series, ...options });\n    18\t```\n    19\t\n    20\t| Option | On a line chart |\n    21\t|---|---|\n    22\t| `x` | Dates, instants, wall-clock times, numbers or categories. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time). |\n    23\t| `y` | One value field with `series` (long rows), or a list of fields (wide rows). |\n    24\t| `series` | The field that names each row's series, for long rows. |\n    25\t| `zoom` | On by default for date, time and number x. `'touch'` also takes pinch and two-finger pan. |\n    26\t| `reference` | Lines and bands: `{ y: 500, label: 'Objective' }`, `{ x: [from, to], label: 'Incident' }`. |\n    27\t| `compact` | Marks only, for a tile. See [Compact](compact.md). |\n    28\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    29\t\n    30\tNot on a line chart, and a type error if given: `stacked` (stacking lines hides how each part moves; a stacked area shows it), `layout` and `horizontal` (bars only), `label` and `value` (donut only). At run time an option that does not apply is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). The [options reference](options.md) has every option with its type and default.\n    31\t\n    32\t## How a line chart behaves\n    33\t\n    34\t| Behaviour | Line |\n    35\t|---|---|\n    36\t| Pointer | The nearest x wins, wherever the pointer is in the plot. The tooltip lists every visible series at that x. A series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\"; one whose value is missing reads \"No data\". |\n    37\t| Keyboard | By x, then series. Left and Right move along x and read every visible series at once; Up and Down narrow to one series and move between series; Escape returns to all series. |\n    38\t| Zoom | Date, time and number x: Zoom in and Zoom out buttons (Pan left, Pan right and Reset zoom once zoomed), dragging across the plot, the keys `+` `-` `0` and Shift with the arrows, the wheel while the plot has focus, and pinch with `zoom: 'touch'`. Category x is not zoomable in 1.0. |\n    39\t| Many points | When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified: in every device-pixel column, each run of valid samples keeps its minimum and maximum, and a gap is always kept. Nothing is smoothed. Tooltip, keyboard and table read the data, not the drawing, so every sample stays reachable. |\n    40\t| Markers | Each series carries its marker shape at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip. Every point gets a marker only when points are at least 12 px apart. A point alone between gaps always has one. |\n    41\t| Limit | Eight series have eight colours. Beyond eight, each series still has its own colour and dash or marker combination, with [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n    42\t| Order and duplicates | Drawn in x order whatever the input order. Two rows for the same x in one series are [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108), and that series is not drawn. |\n    43\t| Reference lines and bands | Drawn, labelled beside the line, included in the value domain, and listed in the summary and the table caption. |\n    44\t\n    45\tThe value axis of a line fits the data and need not start at zero, unlike a bar or area.\n    46\t\n    47\t## Edge case: values that cannot be drawn\n    48\t\n    49\tNull is a gap without comment. `NaN`, a stray string and an absent field are gaps too, each with its own diagnostic, and the reader is told how many values are not shown. A value alone between gaps is a point.\n    50\t\n    51\t[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\narea\n     1\t# Area charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when to use an area chart, how stacking works, and what Axylo does with mixed signs and missing parts.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tAn area shows a quantity over an ordered x where the filled height is the point: a total and how its parts changed. Stacked, the top edge is the total and each band is one part. A single unstacked area is a line with its volume filled in.\n     8\t\n     9\tUse a [line chart](line.md) to compare trends that do not add up, or when the parts cross over and must be read one by one. Overlapping unstacked areas hide each other; stack them or use lines.\n    10\t\n    11\t[example: area-stacked](../examples/area-stacked.ts)\n    12\t\n    13\t## Options\n    14\t\n    15\t```ts\n    16\timport { area } from 'axylo';\n    17\tarea(target, { data, x, y, series, stacked: true, ...options });\n    18\t```\n    19\t\n    20\t| Option | On an area chart |\n    21\t|---|---|\n    22\t| `x`, `y`, `series` | As on a [line chart](line.md#options): long rows with `series`, or wide rows with a list of `y` fields. |\n    23\t| `stacked` | `true` stacks the series. Default: `false`. |\n    24\t| `zoom`, `reference`, `compact` | As on a line chart. |\n    25\t| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110): an area's height is its value. |\n    26\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    27\t\n    28\tNot on an area chart: `layout` and `horizontal` (bars only), `label` and `value` (donut only).\n    29\t\n    30\t## How an area chart behaves\n    31\t\n    32\t| Behaviour | Area |\n    33\t|---|---|\n    34\t| Pointer | The nearest x, as on a line. The tooltip lists every visible series at that x and, when stacked, the stack total. |\n    35\t| Keyboard | By x, then series, as on a line. |\n    36\t| Zoom | As on a line: date, time and number x. |\n    37\t| Baseline | The value axis always includes zero. |\n    38\t| Stacking | Positive values stack upwards from zero and negative values downwards, each on its own side, and the axis covers both totals. Adjacent bands are separated by at least 1 px of background, so they can be told apart without colour. |\n    39\t| Many points | Simplified as a line is, on the cumulative sums, so the drawn top edge keeps every column's extremes. Values come from the data. |\n    40\t| Limit | Eight series, then [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n    41\t| Reference lines and bands | As on a line. |\n    42\t\n    43\t## Edge case: a missing part of a stack\n    44\t\n    45\tWhen one series has no value at an x, its band there has zero height, so the bands above it do not fall into the gap. The stack at that x is marked \"incomplete\" in the tooltip, the table and announcements, because the total there is not the whole.\n    46\t\n    47\t[example: area-incomplete](../examples/area-incomplete.ts)\nbar\n     1\t# Bar charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when to use a bar chart, how to group, stack and turn bars horizontal, and what Axylo does with long labels, missing bars and negative values.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tBars compare categories: satisfaction by team, tickets by product area, orders per region. A bar's length is its value, so the value axis always starts at zero. Bars over a date axis compare counts per period (orders per day).\n     8\t\n     9\tUse a [line chart](line.md) to show a trend over many points, and a [donut](donut.md) only for the shares of one whole.\n    10\t\n    11\t[example: bar-target](../examples/bar-target.ts)\n    12\t\n    13\t## Options\n    14\t\n    15\t```ts\n    16\timport { bar } from 'axylo';\n    17\tbar(target, { data, x, y, series, layout: 'grouped', horizontal: false, ...options });\n    18\t```\n    19\t\n    20\t| Option | On a bar chart |\n    21\t|---|---|\n    22\t| `x` | The categories (or dates, numbers). Categories keep the order of the data. `ordered: true` says they have a natural order (months, age bands), which allows thinning their labels. |\n    23\t| `y`, `series` | One value field with `series`, or a list of fields. Several series make groups. |\n    24\t| `layout` | `'grouped'` (default) puts the series side by side; `'stacked'` stacks them. |\n    25\t| `horizontal` | `true` draws the bars from the left, with the categories down the side. Default: `false`. |\n    26\t| `zoom` | On date, time and number x only. A category axis is not zoomable. |\n    27\t| `reference`, `compact` | As on a line chart. |\n    28\t| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). |\n    29\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    30\t\n    31\tNot on a bar chart: `stacked` (that is `layout: 'stacked'` here), `label` and `value` (donut only).\n    32\t\n    33\t## How a bar chart behaves\n    34\t\n    35\t| Behaviour | Bar |\n    36\t|---|---|\n    37\t| Pointer | The band under the pointer: the whole category, not only the bar. The tooltip lists every series in that category. |\n    38\t| Keyboard | By category, then series. |\n    39\t| Zoom | Only on date, time and number x. |\n    40\t| Baseline | Always zero. An author `domain` without zero is widened ([AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110)). |\n    41\t| Missing values | A missing bar gets a small \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. |\n    42\t| Stacking | Positive parts stack up from zero, negative parts down from it; the axis covers both totals. A missing part has zero height and marks its stack \"incomplete\". |\n    43\t| Long labels | Remedies in order: thinning (date, number and `ordered` category axes only), wrapping to three lines, rotating up to 45°, truncating in the middle with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, the chart says \"Some labels hidden. See table.\" and [AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143) suggests `horizontal: true`. Axylo never switches orientation by itself: that would change what the chart says at a glance. |\n    44\t| Many bars | Not simplified. More bars than pixels along the axis are drawn, with hint [AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132). |\n    45\t| Limit | Eight series, then [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n    46\t| Reference lines | Drawn and labelled, included in the domain, listed in the summary and table caption. |\n    47\t\n    48\t## Horizontal and grouped bars\n    49\t\n    50\tLong category names read best down the side. With two value fields the bars are grouped, and a missing value is a \"no data\" mark, not an empty slot that looks like zero.\n    51\t\n    52\t[example: bar-horizontal](../examples/bar-horizontal.ts)\n    53\t\n    54\t## Edge case: stacks with both signs\n    55\t\n    56\t[example: bar-stacked](../examples/bar-stacked.ts)\nscatter\n     1\t# Scatter charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when to use a scatter chart, which options it takes, and how its points are picked and read by keyboard.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tA scatter chart relates two measures: latency against payload size, rating against price. Each row is a point. An optional `series` groups the points by colour and marker.\n     8\t\n     9\tIts x is a number, an instant or a date, never a category. To compare categories, use a [bar chart](bar.md); to follow one measure over time, a [line](line.md).\n    10\t\n    11\t[example: scatter-basic](../examples/scatter-basic.ts)\n    12\t\n    13\t## Options\n    14\t\n    15\t```ts\n    16\timport { scatter } from 'axylo';\n    17\tscatter(target, { data, x, y, series, ...options });\n    18\t```\n    19\t\n    20\t| Option | On a scatter chart |\n    21\t|---|---|\n    22\t| `x` | A number, time or date field. `type` takes `'number'`, `'time'` or `'date'`; `ordered` does not apply. |\n    23\t| `y` | One value field. A list of fields is not accepted ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150) at run time). |\n    24\t| `series` | Optional. The field that names each point's group. |\n    25\t| `zoom` | Along x only in 1.0. |\n    26\t| `reference` | Lines and bands, as on a line chart. |\n    27\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    28\t\n    29\tNot on a scatter chart, and a type error if given: `compact` (a sparkline of points says nothing a tile can use), `stacked`, `layout`, `horizontal`, `label` and `value`.\n    30\t\n    31\t## How a scatter chart behaves\n    32\t\n    33\t| Behaviour | Scatter |\n    34\t|---|---|\n    35\t| Pointer | The nearest point within 24 px; when two are equally near, the later row wins. The tooltip shows the point's series, x and y, and lists coincident points, up to eight. |\n    36\t| Keyboard | By x, then y, through every visible point. |\n    37\t| Zoom | x only in 1.0; both axes later. |\n    38\t| Many points | Not simplified in 1.0. Above 50,000 points every point is still drawn, with hint [AX133](diagnostics.md#ax133). |\n    39\t| Duplicates | Points that share an x, or both coordinates, are ordinary data. |\n    40\t| Limit | Eight series, then [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n    41\t| Reference lines and bands | Drawn and included in the domain. |\n    42\t\n    43\t## Edge case: points on top of each other\n    44\t\n    45\tWhole-number ratings put many reviews on the same spot. They are all kept: the tooltip lists the coincident points, and the keyboard visits each one in turn.\n    46\t\n    47\t[example: scatter-ties](../examples/scatter-ties.ts)\ndonut\n     1\t# Donut charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when a donut is the right choice, which options it takes, and what it does with zero, missing, negative and too many slices.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tA donut shows the shares of one whole: sessions by browser, budget by item. It works for a handful of parts; with more than eight, Axylo groups the smallest. To compare amounts that are not parts of one whole, or many categories, use a [bar chart](bar.md).\n     8\t\n     9\t[example: donut-basic](../examples/donut-basic.ts)\n    10\t\n    11\t## Options\n    12\t\n    13\t```ts\n    14\timport { donut } from 'axylo';\n    15\tdonut(target, { data, label, value, ...options });\n    16\t```\n    17\t\n    18\t| Option | On a donut |\n    19\t|---|---|\n    20\t| `label` | The field that names each slice. Labels must be unique. |\n    21\t| `value` | The value field: a field name or a [value field object](options.md#value-fields) (`label`, `unit`, `scale`, `format`). Values must not be negative. |\n    22\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    23\t\n    24\tNot on a donut, and a type error if given: `x`, `y` and `series` (a donut has a label and a value), `zoom` and `reference` (there is no axis), `compact`, `stacked`, `layout`, `horizontal`.\n    25\t\n    26\t## How a donut behaves\n    27\t\n    28\t| Behaviour | Donut |\n    29\t|---|---|\n    30\t| Order | Slices keep the order of the data, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. |\n    31\t| Legend | Every slice, in slice order, with its value and percentage as text. |\n    32\t| Pointer | The slice under the pointer. The tooltip shows its label, value and percentage. |\n    33\t| Keyboard | Through the slices in slice order. |\n    34\t| Zero and missing | A zero slice is listed in the legend without an arc. A slice with no value is listed as \"No data\". |\n    35\t| Negative | A negative value is error [AX120](diagnostics.md#ax120): the donut shows its error state, since a negative share cannot be drawn. |\n    36\t| More than eight | The seven largest slices are drawn and the rest grouped as \"Other (n)\", with [AX121](diagnostics.md#ax121). The table still lists every category. |\n    37\t| All zero | The empty state: there is no whole to divide. |\n    38\t| Duplicates | Two slices with the same label are [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): the donut is not drawn. |\n    39\t| Separators | Adjacent slices are separated by at least 1 px of background. |\n    40\t\n    41\t## Edge case: many slices, a zero and a gap\n    42\t\n    43\t[example: donut-many](../examples/donut-many.ts)\ncompact\n     1\t# Compact charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can put a sparkline in a KPI tile with `compact: true`, know what it shows and leaves out, and give it a text alternative that a screen-reader user can rely on.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\t`compact: true` on a line, area or bar chart draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and its label, and the chart only says which way it is going.\n     8\t\n     9\t[example: compact-tiles](../examples/compact-tiles.html)\n    10\t\n    11\tA compact chart does not give access to every value; its summary is the alternative. When the reader needs every value, use the full chart.\n    12\t\n    13\t## What a compact chart is\n    14\t\n    15\t| Behaviour | Compact |\n    16\t|---|---|\n    17\t| For assistive technology | A static image (`role=\"img\"`) with an accessible name and the generated summary as its description: series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, and any values left out. |\n    18\t| Name | `labelledBy` with the tile heading's id (best: the heading is already there), or `title`. In a compact chart the title is not shown; it is the accessible name. Without either, the name is generated and [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140) is raised. |\n    19\t| Keyboard and pointer | No tab stop and no pointer behaviour, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. A page of forty tiles adds no tab stops. |\n    20\t| Size | Fills its container from 24 × 16 px up. With no height set, it is 32 px tall. |\n    21\t| Several series | Allowed, with lines told apart by dash as well as colour, since there is no legend; hint [AX131](diagnostics.md#ax131) suggests one series per tile. |\n    22\t| Fidelity | The rules of the full charts still hold: zero baseline for bar and area, gaps for missing values, diagnostics, simplified drawing of dense lines, sorting by x, no motion, and light or dark from the page. |\n    23\t| Reference lines | Not drawn. |\n    24\t\n    25\t## Options\n    26\t\n    27\t`compact` takes the options of its chart type ([line](line.md#options), [area](area.md#options), [bar](bar.md#options)). The ones that need axes or controls (`zoom`, `reference`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`) have no effect on a compact chart. Scatter and donut have no compact form: on them `compact` is a type error.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Edge case: a value left out\n    30\t\n    31\tWhen values were left out, the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner of the chart.\n    32\t\n    33\t[example: compact-dirty](../examples/compact-dirty.html)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in index getting-started data; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in index getting-started data; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"index\n     1\t# Axylo\n     2\t\n     3\tAxylo draws the everyday charts of web apps and dashboards: line, area, bar, scatter and donut, and a compact variant for KPI tiles. You pass rows and name the fields; the chart that comes back is faithful to the data, readable at any width and in light or dark pages, and works with a keyboard and a screen reader. When the data is wrong, Axylo says so, to you in the console and to the reader on the chart, instead of drawing a guess.\n     4\t\n     5\tThis page says what you get, what Axylo leaves out on purpose, and where to read next.\n     6\t\n     7\t[example: bar-target](../examples/bar-target.ts)\n     8\t\n     9\tThat chart is one call with a title, the rows and two field names, plus a target line. Its value axis starts at zero, its long label fits, its colours follow this page's scheme, and it has a legend-free toolbar with \"View as table\". Try Tab, then the arrow keys.\n    10\t\n    11\t## What you get without asking\n    12\t\n    13\t| You get | Where to read more |\n    14\t|---|---|\n    15\t| A title and an accessible name, and a generated summary of the data (range, minimum, maximum, last value, time zone) | [Accessibility](accessibility.md) |\n    16\t| Axes with units, a legend, a tooltip that appears wherever the pointer is, and \"View as table\" | [Getting started](getting-started.md) |\n    17\t| Keyboard access: one tab stop for the plot, one for the controls | [Accessibility](accessibility.md#the-keyboard-map) |\n    18\t| Series told apart by colour and by marker or dash, in every mode | [Theming](theming.md) |\n    19\t| Light and dark taken from the page, forced colours, reduced motion | [Theming](theming.md#dark-mode) |\n    20\t| Sizing from the container, in both directions, with no call from you | [Getting started](getting-started.md#what-you-get-without-asking) |\n    21\t| Gaps for missing values, never a line drawn through them | [Your data](data.md#missing-values) |\n    22\t| Calendar dates that read the same in every time zone | [Your data](data.md#three-kinds-of-time) |\n    23\t| A coded diagnostic with the field, the rows and the fix for anything that cannot be drawn as given | [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md) |\n    24\t\n    25\t## What Axylo does not try to do\n    26\t\n    27\tAxylo has few chart types, each with its own rules for labels, bad data and accessibility. It does not aim for many chart types, deep visual customisation, or exploratory analysis in notebooks. There are no maps, financial charts or network diagrams, and no log scale or second y axis in 1.0. [Stability and releases](stability.md#what-is-not-in-10) lists what is left for later and why.\n    28\t\n    29\tThere is one way to do each thing: one data model, one method per kind of change, and CSS for every colour. There is no `width`, `height`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colour from CSS, and the first render is never animated.\n    30\t\n    31\t## Where to go next\n    32\t\n    33\t- [Getting started](getting-started.md): install, the first chart, and what to check when it looks wrong.\n    34\t- [Your data](data.md): rows and columns, dates and times, missing values, units.\n    35\t- One page per chart type: [line](line.md), [area](area.md), [bar](bar.md), [scatter](scatter.md), [donut](donut.md), [compact](compact.md).\n    36\t- [Updating, streaming and zoom](updating.md), and the [React components](react.md).\n    37\t- [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md): every code, with a page each.\n    38\t- [Options reference](options.md): every option of every chart type.\n    39\t- Coming from another library: [Chart.js](migrate-chartjs.md) or [Recharts](migrate-recharts.md).\n    40\t\n    41\t## About this documentation\n    42\t\n    43\tThe live examples on these pages run the library's built files (the development flavour) from this repository. A test type-checks every example and renders it in Chrome, and fails on any console error or on a diagnostic the example does not say it raises. The library is a pre-release (package version 0.0.0) and some areas are still being finished: the tooltip's placement, the pan buttons, zoom on category axes and the table overlay. Pages that describe those areas follow the specification and say where the built library may still differ.\ngetting-started\n     1\t# Getting started\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can install Axylo, draw a first chart from rows in npm, script-tag or React code, and know the three things to check when a chart looks wrong.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Install\n     6\t\n     7\tWith npm and a bundler:\n     8\t\n     9\t```sh\n    10\tnpm install axylo\n    11\t```\n    12\t\n    13\tFor React, add the adapter. It works with React 18 and 19.\n    14\t\n    15\t```sh\n    16\tnpm install axylo @axylo/react\n    17\t```\n    18\t\n    19\tWith a script tag, load one file from the package's `dist` folder: `axylo.iife.js` is the development build (it prints hints), `axylo.iife.min.js` the production build. Both define one global, `Axylo`. The snippet below loads them from jsDelivr, which serves published npm packages; until Axylo is published there, copy the file from the package to your site instead.\n    20\t\n    21\t```html\n    22\t<script src=\"/js/axylo.iife.min.js\"></script>\n    23\t```\n    24\t\n    25\tThe package has no runtime dependencies. It ships two flavours of the module build, chosen by the `development` export condition: a bundler that resolves that condition gets the development build, which also prints hints; otherwise you get the production build, which prints errors and warnings only. The styles are applied by the library itself without adding a `<style>` element, so there is no CSS file to import. A standalone copy, `axylo/axylo.css`, ships for pages that prefer to load the stylesheet themselves. Whether a page with a strict Content Security Policy needs it has not been tested yet.\n    26\t\n    27\tTypeScript 5.4 or later gives you checked field names; plain JavaScript works the same without them.\n    28\t\n    29\t## The first chart\n    30\t\n    31\tEach of these is complete: the import, the rows, and one call (or one component). They are kept in the repository as files that the package's distribution tests install and render, so what you see here is what is checked.\n    32\t\n    33\tWith npm:\n    34\t\n    35\t[source: examples/first-chart/npm.ts](../../../examples/first-chart/npm.ts)\n    36\t\n    37\tWith a script tag:\n    38\t\n    39\t[source: examples/first-chart/script.html](../../../examples/first-chart/script.html)\n    40\t\n    41\tIn React:\n    42\t\n    43\t[source: examples/first-chart/react.tsx](../../../examples/first-chart/react.tsx)\n    44\t\n    45\tThe same kind of chart, running on this page. Point at it, press Tab to reach the plot and use the arrow keys, or open the table.\n    46\t\n    47\t[example: first-chart](../examples/first-chart.ts)\n    48\t\n    49\tA script-tag page needs nothing more than this:\n    50\t\n    51\t[example: script-tag](../examples/script-tag.html)\n    52\t\n    53\tThe only required options are `data` and the fields: `x` and `y` (and `series` for long rows), or `label` and `value` for a donut. `title` is not required, but a chart without one gets a generated name and warning [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140).\n    54\t\n    55\t## What you get without asking\n    56\t\n    57\t| Feature | What it does |\n    58\t|---|---|\n    59\t| Title and accessible name | `title` is the visible heading and the chart's name for assistive technology. Without it, the name is generated (\"Line chart of sessions by day\") and AX140 is raised. In a dashboard card that already has a heading, use `labelledBy` with the heading's id instead. |\n    60\t| Summary | A generated description: chart type, series, the x range with its time zone, the value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. `description` adds to it. |\n    61\t| Table view | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from your rows, 100 rows per page, with a \"Jump to\" field. |\n    62\t| Keyboard | The plot is one tab stop: arrow keys move through the points and read each one. The legend, zoom, pause and table controls are a second tab stop. See [the keyboard map](accessibility.md#the-keyboard-map). |\n    63\t| Tooltip | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is at that x for every visible series; Escape closes it. |\n    64\t| Colour and more than colour | Up to eight series get eight colours that work in light and dark pages, and each line also carries its own marker shape, repeated in the legend and tooltip. |\n    65\t| Page theme | Font, text colour and light or dark come from the page. The chart repaints when the page switches scheme. |\n    66\t| Size | The chart fills its container's width; with no height set, the plot is 16:9, between 160 and 480 px tall. It follows the container as it resizes, with no call from you. |\n    67\t| States | No rows shows \"No data to show.\" inside the plot, never an empty frame or an invented axis. `state` sets loading, empty and error messages of your own. |\n    68\t| Diagnostics | Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported with a code, the field, the rows and the fix; when values were left out, the reader is told too. |\n    69\t\n    70\tThe four states, each a chart with no rows:\n    71\t\n    72\t[example: states](../examples/states.ts)\n    73\t\n    74\t## When it looks wrong: three things to check\n    75\t\n    76\t**1. Read the diagnostics.** Open the browser console. Every problem Axylo found is printed once per code as `[axylo AX…] \"Chart title\": what is wrong, where, and how to fix it`. The same list is on the chart as `chart.diagnostics`, current as soon as the call returns. Each code has a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). Hints, which are advice rather than problems, print in the development build only.\n    77\t\n    78\t**2. Check which kind of x Axylo read.** A date off by one day almost always means the x values are instants where calendar dates were meant: `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'` is a moment, which is the evening of the 23rd in New York. If the tooltip heading names a time zone, Axylo read instants. Send `'2026-02-24'`, or declare `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. If the x labels are your raw strings in input order, Axylo read categories. [The three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time) explains the rule.\n    79\t\n    80\t**3. Check the values.** Numbers that arrive as strings, a missing day, two rows for the same day, a misspelled field: each has its own code, and `validate()` finds them without drawing, in Node or in a test:\n    81\t\n    82\t```ts\n    83\timport { validate } from 'axylo';\n    84\t\n    85\tconst problems = validate('line', { data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'sessions', series: 'channel' });\n    86\tfor (const d of problems) console.log(d.code, d.level, d.message);\n    87\t```\n    88\t\n    89\t[Your data](data.md) covers each case.\n    90\t\n    91\t## Next\n    92\t\n    93\tRead [Your data](data.md) next: it explains the three shapes rows can take, how dates are read, and what happens to missing values.\ndata\n     1\t# Your data\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can pass the same table as long rows, wide rows or typed columns; say which kind of time your x values are; control what happens to missing values; and give values a unit, a format and a percent scale.\n     4\t\n     5\t## One table, three shapes\n     6\t\n     7\tTake seven numbers: sessions per day for two channels.\n     8\t\n     9\t| day | Direct | Referral |\n    10\t|---|---|---|\n    11\t| 2026-03-02 | 3,120 | 410 |\n    12\t| 2026-03-03 | 3,380 | 455 |\n    13\t| 2026-03-04 | 3,290 | 390 |\n    14\t| 2026-03-05 | 3,510 | 520 |\n    15\t\n    16\tAxylo accepts this table in three shapes. All three produce the same chart, the same table view and the same diagnostics; row numbers in diagnostics and events always refer to your own data in the shape you gave it.\n    17\t\n    18\t### Long rows\n    19\t\n    20\tOne row per x and series, as most SQL queries and APIs return it. `series` names the field that says which series a row belongs to.\n    21\t\n    22\t[example: data-long](../examples/data-long.ts)\n    23\t\n    24\t### Wide rows\n    25\t\n    26\tOne row per x, one field per series. `y` is a list of fields, and there is no `series`. Passing both is a type error, and at run time it raises [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150).\n    27\t\n    28\t[example: data-wide](../examples/data-wide.ts)\n    29\t\n    30\t### Typed columns\n    31\t\n    32\tOne array per field under `columns`, all of the same length; different lengths are [AX100](diagnostics.md#ax100). This is the shape for large data. In a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array`, `NaN` means \"no value\" and raises nothing.\n    33\t\n    34\t[example: data-columns](../examples/data-columns.ts)\n    35\t\n    36\t## Fields\n    37\t\n    38\tA field is a key of your rows, written as a string, or an object when it needs more:\n    39\t\n    40\t```ts\n    41\tx: 'day'\n    42\ty: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'visits' }\n    43\t```\n    44\t\n    45\tThe object form adds a label (the axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements), a unit, formats, a scale, a missing-value rule and author bounds. The [options reference](options.md#value-fields) lists them all.\n    46\t\n    47\tWith TypeScript, field names are checked against your row type. A misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, or wide `y` together with `series` does not compile. With untyped rows (from `fetch().json()`, say) the same mistakes are reported at run time: a field that no row has is [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), and the message lists the fields that exist and the closest match.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Three kinds of time\n    50\t\n    51\tDates are where charts most often go wrong without anyone noticing. Axylo keeps three kinds apart and never mixes them silently.\n    52\t\n    53\t| Kind | Looks like | Means | Shown |\n    54\t|---|---|---|---|\n    55\t| Calendar date | `'2026-02-24'`, `'2026-02'`, `'2026'` | A day, month or year, wherever you are | The same in every time zone |\n    56\t| Instant | Epoch milliseconds with `type: 'time'`, a `Date`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00Z'`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00+01:00'` | One moment, the same everywhere | In `timeZone` (default: the viewer's zone), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the summary |\n    57\t| Wall-clock time | `'2026-02-24T09:30'` (no offset) | What a clock showed, zone unknown | As written, with hint [AX112](diagnostics.md#ax112) |\n    58\t\n    59\t### The off-by-one-day trap\n    60\t\n    61\tA daily metric is a calendar date. Many back ends send it as an instant at UTC midnight: `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'`, or the epoch milliseconds of that moment. For a viewer in New York that moment is the evening of 23 February, so every daily value appears one day early. In Berlin it is still the 24th. The library is not wrong to show the instant in the viewer's zone; the data said \"this moment\", not \"this day\".\n    62\t\n    63\tThe example below has the same five values sent both ways. On this page your browser's time zone decides how the first chart reads: west of UTC, its first point is on the 23rd.\n    64\t\n    65\t[example: dates](../examples/dates.ts)\n    66\t\n    67\tAxylo notices instants that all fall at the same time of day, whole days apart, and raises hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). The fix is one of:\n    68\t\n    69\t- Send calendar dates: `'2026-02-24'`. `new Date(ms).toISOString().slice(0, 10)` turns a UTC midnight into one.\n    70\t- Keep the values and declare `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Axylo then reads each instant (string, number or `Date`) as the calendar day it falls on in UTC.\n    71\t- If they really are instants, and you want everyone to see UTC, set `x: { field: 'day', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' }`.\n    72\t\n    73\t### How Axylo decides\n    74\t\n    75\tWhen `type` is not given, Axylo looks at every value of the x field and takes the kind most of them have:\n    76\t\n    77\t- `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM` or `YYYY` strings are calendar dates.\n    78\t- Date-time strings with `Z` or an offset, and `Date` objects, are instants.\n    79\t- Date-time strings without an offset are wall-clock times.\n    80\t- Numbers are numbers. A bare number is never taken for an instant: epoch milliseconds need `type: 'time'`, and numbers that look like timestamps raise hint [AX109](diagnostics.md#ax109).\n    81\t- Anything else is a category, kept in the order it first appears.\n    82\t\n    83\tA value that does not match the chosen kind (a day that does not exist, a different format) is [AX107](diagnostics.md#ax107): that row is not drawn, and the reader is told.\n    84\t\n    85\t`timeZone` takes `'local'` (the default, the viewer's zone), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone such as `'Europe/Paris'`. It applies to instants only. A zone the browser does not know is [AX152](diagnostics.md#ax152), and the viewer's zone is used.\n    86\t\n    87\tCalendar periods other than years and months (quarters, ISO weeks) are categories in 1.0: write them as you want them read (`'2025 Q1'`) and set `ordered: true` on the x field so that their labels may be thinned when they do not fit.\n    88\t\n    89\t## Missing values\n    90\t\n    91\tAxylo never draws a missing value as zero and never connects a line across one, unless you ask.\n    92\t\n    93\t| In your data | Drawn | Reported |\n    94\t|---|---|---|\n    95\t| `null`, or `''` | A gap | Nothing: this is how to say \"no data\" |\n    96\t| `NaN` in a typed column | A gap | Nothing |\n    97\t| The field absent from a row (`undefined`) | A gap | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n    98\t| `NaN` or `Infinity` in rows | A gap | Warning [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103), and the reader's note |\n    99\t| No row at all for a step of a regular axis (a missing day) | A gap, as if the row held `null` | Hint [AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105) |\n   100\t| `0` | Zero | Nothing |\n   101\t\n   102\tA value standing alone between two gaps is drawn as a point, so it does not vanish. On a bar chart a missing value gets a small \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". When a warning means values were left out, the chart shows a short note to the reader (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\") inside the figure, in every build.\n   103\t\n   104\tFor counts, where a day without a row means nothing happened, declare it: `missing: 'zero'` turns null and absent values of that field into zeros.\n   105\t\n   106\t[example: missing](../examples/missing.ts)\n   107\t\n   108\tHere are the values an application sends by mistake, all at once. The note under the chart is what the reader sees; the console has one line per code.\n   109\t\n   110\t[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\n   111\t\n   112\t## Numbers sent as strings\n   113\t\n   114\tSome database drivers return counts and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). Axylo treats two cases differently:\n   115\t\n   116\t- A whole field of plain decimal strings (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) is unambiguous: it is parsed and drawn, and hint [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104) asks you to say so with `parse: 'number'`. With that, there is no hint, and TypeScript accepts the string field on a value axis.\n   117\t- A string among numbers, or a string that is not a plain decimal (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`), is a bug upstream. It is not drawn, and it is warning [AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106).\n   118\t\n   119\t[example: numeric-strings](../examples/numeric-strings.ts)\n   120\t\n   121\t## Order and duplicates\n   122\t\n   123\tRows can come in any order. Line, area and scatter charts are drawn in x order; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n   124\t\n   125\t[example: unsorted](../examples/unsorted.ts)\n   126\t\n   127\tOn a line, area or bar chart one x has one value per series. Two rows with the same x in the same series are error [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108), and that series is not drawn: Axylo does not know whether you meant the sum, the last or the average. The same applies to two donut slices with the same label. On a scatter chart repeated x values, and repeated points, are ordinary data.\n   128\t\n   129\t## Units, formats and percentages\n   130\t\n   131\t| Option on a value field | What it does |\n   132\t|---|---|\n   133\t| `label` | The axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. Default: the field name. |\n   134\t| `unit` | Follows every value in tooltip, table and announcements, and is part of the axis title. |\n   135\t| `format` | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements: `Intl.NumberFormat` options, or a function. Default: the page locale's grouping, the full integer part and up to six significant fraction digits (`123,456.75`). |\n   136\t| `tickFormat` | How axis ticks are written; may be shorter than `format`. Default: compact when it saves space (`50K`). |\n   137\t| `scale: 'percent'` | The values are percentages from 0 to 100. |\n   138\t| `scale: 'ratio'` | The values are fractions from 0 to 1, shown as percentages. |\n   139\t\n   140\tA percent or ratio scale is declared, never guessed from a label. Its axis ends at full scale (100%) only when the largest value is at least half of full scale and not above it; otherwise it ends at the first tick at or above the data, so 4% is not drawn as a sliver of 100%. Numbers and dates are formatted for the `locale` option if given, else the document's language (`<html lang>`), else the browser's. A formatter function that throws is warning [AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160), and the default format is used for that value.\n   141\t\n   142\t[example: units](../examples/units.ts)\n   143\t\n   144\tBar and area value axes always include zero, since a bar's length is its value. A `domain` that leaves zero out is widened, with warning [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). Line and scatter axes fit the data.\n   145\t\n   146\t## What Axylo reads, keeps and copies\n   147\t\n   148\t- **Rows** are read when the chart is created or updated and never changed. Their values are copied into Axylo's own typed arrays. Events hand back your own row object (`point.row`), and every diagnostic names rows by their index in your array.\n   149\t- **Typed columns** of values (`Float64Array`, `Float32Array`) are borrowed: Axylo reads them in place and does not copy them, unless it must change a value (`Infinity`, or `missing: 'zero'`). Do not change a borrowed array in place afterwards; to change the data, pass new arrays with `update({ data })`.\n   150\t- **Appended rows** are read once and added to the chart's own storage; `keep` limits how much is kept. See [Updating, streaming and zoom](updating.md#append-and-keep).\n   151\t\n   152\t## Checking data before you draw\n   153\t\n   154\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as a chart, without a page, and returns the diagnostics. Use it in Node, in a unit test, or in a coding agent's loop before rendering. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in theming accessibility conformance options; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in theming accessibility conformance options; do echo $f; nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"theming\n     1\t# Theming\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can change a chart's colours and type with CSS custom properties, give a series the same colour on every chart, and know what Axylo does in dark mode, in forced-colours mode and in print, and why its default palette looks the way it does.\n     4\t\n     5\tThere is no theme object and no palette option. A chart takes its font and text colour from the page, as any element does, and everything else from CSS custom properties that you can set on the chart, on any ancestor, or on `:root`.\n     6\t\n     7\t## The custom properties\n     8\t\n     9\tEach colour property falls back to a default from Axylo's stylesheet, with one value for light charts and one for dark. The defaults below are read from the built stylesheet when this site is built.\n    10\t\n    11\t[table: theme tokens](../data/tokens.mjs)\n    12\t\n    13\tValues are any CSS colour, including `var()` references; a chart follows a variable when it changes, without a call. Colours of marks should keep at least 3:1 against the background behind the chart, and text 4.5:1. When the series colours fall below 3:1 against the background the chart actually sits on, Axylo raises [AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141).\n    14\t\n    15\t[example: theme-tokens](../examples/theme-tokens.html)\n    16\t\n    17\t## One colour per series name\n    18\t\n    19\tThe palette assigns colours by position: the first series gets the first colour. On a dashboard where charts show different subsets, \"Referral\" would change colour from chart to chart. `colors` pins a colour to a series name, so the same series looks the same everywhere:\n    20\t\n    21\t```ts\n    22\tconst colors = { Direct: 'var(--brand-direct)', Referral: 'var(--brand-referral)' };\n    23\tline('#a', { ...options, colors });\n    24\t```\n    25\t\n    26\tSeries not named in `colors` take palette colours. A value the browser does not accept as a colour is replaced by the series' palette colour.\n    27\t\n    28\t[example: theme-colors](../examples/theme-colors.html)\n    29\t\n    30\t## Dark mode\n    31\t\n    32\tEach chart decides on its own whether it is light or dark, from the page around it:\n    33\t\n    34\t1. If `--axylo-background` is set, from that colour's lightness.\n    35\t2. Otherwise from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`: `light` or `dark`; for `light dark`, the scheme the browser is using there.\n    36\t3. Otherwise from the inherited text colour: light text means a dark page.\n    37\t\n    38\tSo a page that sets `color-scheme`, a page whose theme switch only changes classes and colours, and a light-only page on a device set to dark mode (which stays light) all get the right defaults. The chart repaints within a frame when the colour scheme, forced colours or the contrast preference change, and when the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes.\n    39\t\n    40\t[example: theme-toggle](../examples/theme-toggle.html)\n    41\t\n    42\t## Forced colours, high contrast and print\n    43\t\n    44\tIn forced-colours mode (Windows High Contrast and similar) every paint becomes a system colour, the colours you set included, and they come back when the mode ends. Series are then told apart by their markers and dashes. Under `prefers-contrast: more`, and in forced colours, lines also differ by dash.\n    45\t\n    46\tMarks are drawn as SVG by default, so they stay sharp at any zoom and in print. There is no print stylesheet in 1.0, and printing has not been tested; the toolbar prints as it appears on screen.\n    47\t\n    48\t## The default palette\n    49\t\n    50\t[table: palette](../../../packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts)\n    51\t\n    52\tEight colours, in the order blue, orange, green, purple, red, cyan, magenta, slate, with the same family in the same slot in light and dark charts. They were fitted to measurable limits, not chosen by eye:\n    53\t\n    54\t- each colour is at least 3:1 against the default light and dark backgrounds;\n    55\t- every pair stays apart (OKLab difference of at least 0.10) under normal vision and under simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia, and the first four, which most charts use, further apart still;\n    56\t- they stay clear of the focus, reference and muted colours;\n    57\t- neighbouring colours stay apart as translucent and solid fills.\n    58\t\n    59\tColour is never the only cue: lines and areas also carry marker shapes, adjacent fills are separated by background, and the legend and tooltip repeat the cues. The palette is a proposal that meets those limits; whether it is pleasant to look at is still open. The reasoning, the measurements and what was tried are in [decision 0003](../../../docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md).\n    60\t\n    61\t## Internal properties\n    62\t\n    63\tThe stylesheet also uses a few custom properties that are not for authors. They are listed so that you can recognise them in the developer tools; setting them is unsupported.\n    64\t\n    65\t[table: internal tokens](../data/tokens.mjs)\naccessibility\n     1\t# Accessibility\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know what a reader using a screen reader, a keyboard, magnification, high contrast or with a colour-vision deficiency gets from an Axylo chart, which keys do what, what you as the author must supply, and what has not been verified.\n     4\t\n     5\tAccessibility is on by default and cannot be forgotten: there is no module to import and no option to turn on.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What each reader gets\n     8\t\n     9\t| Reader | What the chart provides |\n    10\t|---|---|\n    11\t| Screen reader | The chart is a named group (or, when compact, a named image) with a generated summary: chart type, series, the x range with its time zone, the value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any values left out. The plot is one native slider-like control whose value text reads the point in focus: its x, every visible series' value with its unit, and \"Point 4 of 120\". \"View as table\" opens the data as an HTML table with a caption, column headers with units, and row headers. Changes of state (loading, empty, error) are announced once, politely; arriving data is not announced. |\n    12\t| Keyboard | Two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot and the toolbar. Every point is reachable in order, the series can be switched, the ends are one key away, and Tab always leaves the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1. |\n    13\t| Low vision | Text is real HTML at 12 px or more, with 4.5:1 contrast; marks, axes and focus indicators 3:1. Text follows browser zoom and user text-spacing settings. Labels never overlap; when one cannot be shown, the chart says so. The tooltip can be hovered, stays inside the window, and Escape closes it. |\n    14\t| Colour-vision deficiency | Eight colours kept apart under simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia, and never colour alone: each line has its own marker shape, repeated in the legend and tooltip; adjacent fills are separated by background. |\n    15\t| High contrast | Forced-colours mode uses system colours, and lines also differ by dash; so they do under `prefers-contrast: more`. |\n    16\t| Reduced motion | Nothing is animated on first render. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions at all. |\n    17\t| Touch and single pointer | Every control is at least 24 × 24 px. Zoom works by single clicks on buttons as well as by dragging. A one-finger drag scrolls the page. |\n    18\t\n    19\tThe legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state. When a series is hidden, its entry keeps full-contrast text, struck through.\n    20\t\n    21\t## The keyboard map\n    22\t\n    23\tThe plot is a single native range input laid over the chart. Its keys:\n    24\t\n    25\t| Key | In the plot |\n    26\t|---|---|\n    27\t| Left, Right | Previous or next x, reading every visible series there. Scatter: the previous or next point in x, then y order. Donut: the previous or next slice. |\n    28\t| Home, End | The first or last point. |\n    29\t| Page Up, Page Down | A larger step. |\n    30\t| Up, Down | Narrow to one series and move to the previous or next series. Donut: the next or previous slice. |\n    31\t| Escape | Close the tooltip; pressed again, return from one series to all. Otherwise left to the page, so a chart in a dialog does not swallow it. |\n    32\t| Enter, Space | Select the point: the chart fires its `select` event. |\n    33\t| `+`, `-`, `0` | Zoom in, zoom out, show everything (date, time and number x). |\n    34\t| Shift with Left or Right | Pan the zoomed view. |\n    35\t| Wheel | Zoom, only while the plot has focus and never with Ctrl or Meta held. |\n    36\t\n    37\tThe toolbar (legend toggles, Zoom in, Zoom out, Pan left, Pan right, Reset zoom, Pause, View as table; each appears when it applies) is one tab stop. Inside it the arrow keys move between controls, Home and End go to the first and last, and Enter or Space presses the control. The open table adds the table's scroll region and its pager (previous page, next page and a \"Jump to\" field) to the tab order.\n    38\t\n    39\tThis pattern was chosen by reasoning and by inspecting Chrome's accessibility tree, not by testing with screen readers; see [decision 0001](../../../docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md) and the limits below. The plot's accessible description gives a short version of this map.\n    40\t\n    41\t## What you must supply\n    42\t\n    43\tAxylo can describe the data; only you know what it means.\n    44\t\n    45\t- **A meaningful name.** Give every chart a `title`, or, when the card around it already has a visible heading, `labelledBy` with that heading's id. A generated name (\"Line chart of sessions by day\") is a fallback and raises [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140). A title can be hidden visually with `title: { text, visible: false }`, and its heading level set with `level` (2 to 6, default 3) to fit your page's outline.\n    46\t- **Series names.** For wide rows, give each field a `label`; for long rows, the series field's values are the names. They are read for every point.\n    47\t- **Units.** Set `unit` (and `label`) on value fields, so \"412\" is read as \"412 ms\".\n    48\t- **Context.** `description` adds what the data cannot say (\"The spike on 12 March was the payment provider outage.\") to the generated summary. It never removes the time zone or the statement of values left out.\n    49\t- **The page's structure.** Headings, landmarks and the order of content around the chart are yours.\n    50\t- **Text in other languages.** Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. When the page's language is not English, built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"`.\n    51\t\n    52\t[example: a11y-card](../examples/a11y-card.html)\n    53\t\n    54\t## Limits\n    55\t\n    56\tAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast and text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence planned for that in 1.0 is a rehearsal by the developer on five screen-reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made.\n    57\t\n    58\tAs of this documentation:\n    59\t\n    60\t- No screen reader has been run against these charts yet. The plot's pattern ([decision 0001](../../../docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md)) and the rules for the tooltip and the focused point's text ([decision 0004](../../../docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md)) are unrehearsed.\n    61\t- Only Chrome on desktop has been tested. Firefox, Safari (macOS and iOS) and phones have not been verified.\n    62\t- The tooltip's placement, the pan buttons, zoom on category axes and the table's overlay are being finished, and may differ from this page until they are.\n    63\t- Right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored.\n    64\t- Built-in text ships in English only; other languages go through `messages`.\n    65\t\n    66\t## Conformance\n    67\t\n    68\tThe WCAG 2.2 AA conformance table is produced separately, from test evidence: see [Conformance table](conformance.md).\nconformance\n     1\t# Conformance table\n     2\t\n     3\t**Placeholder. This page is not the conformance table.**\n     4\t\n     5\tThe table that lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion, the test that covers it and what the author must supply (criterion A9 of the specification) is produced separately from the acceptance tests' evidence, and published with each release. It has not been produced yet, and nothing on this page may be read as a claim of conformance.\n     6\t\n     7\tWhen it exists, it will also state the limits of that evidence in the specification's own terms: automated checks and the developer's rehearsal only, no usability testing with disabled users, and the browsers, screen readers and devices that have not been verified.\n     8\t\n     9\tUntil then, [Accessibility](accessibility.md) describes what the charts do and what has not been verified.\noptions\n     1\t# Options reference\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can look up any option of any chart type, any field option, and every member of the chart handle, with its type, its default and what it does. The groups follow the declarations in `packages/axylo/src/public.ts`, which are the source of truth. A test checks that every key in those declarations is on this page and that nothing on this page is missing from them.\n     4\t\n     5\t`Row` is the type of one of your rows. For typed columns it is inferred from the columns.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Which options apply where\n     8\t\n     9\t| Group | line | area | bar | scatter | donut |\n    10\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    11\t| [Common options](#common-options) | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes |\n    12\t| [Encoding](#encoding-line-area-bar) (`x`, `y`, `series`) | yes | yes | yes | – | – |\n    13\t| [Cartesian options](#cartesian-options) (`zoom`, `reference`) | yes | yes | yes | yes | – |\n    14\t| [`compact`](#compact) | yes | yes | yes | – | – |\n    15\t| [`stacked`](#area-options) | – | yes | – | – | – |\n    16\t| [`layout`, `horizontal`](#bar-options) | – | – | yes | – | – |\n    17\t| [Scatter encoding](#scatter-options) | – | – | – | yes | – |\n    18\t| [Donut encoding](#donut-options) (`label`, `value`) | – | – | – | – | yes |\n    19\t\n    20\tAn option that does not apply to a chart type does not compile, and at run time is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150).\n    21\t\n    22\t## Common options\n    23\t\n    24\tOn every chart type.\n    25\t\n    26\t<!-- options: CommonOptions -->\n    27\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    28\t|---|---|---|---|\n    29\t| `data` | `readonly Row[] \\| { columns }` | required | The rows, or typed columns of equal length. See [Your data](data.md). |\n    30\t| `title` | `string \\| Title` | none | Visible heading and accessible name. Without it (and without `labelledBy`) the name is generated, with AX140. |\n    31\t| `labelledBy` | `string` | none | Id of an existing heading that names the chart, for cards that already show a title. |\n    32\t| `subtitle` | `string` | none | A line under the title. |\n    33\t| `description` | `string` | none | Added to the generated summary. The time zone and any omitted values are always stated. |\n    34\t| `state` | `'ready' \\| 'loading' \\| { error: string } \\| { empty: string }` | `'ready'` | Shows a loading, error or empty message inside the plot instead of the data. |\n    35\t| `legend` | `'auto' \\| false \\| 'top' \\| 'right' \\| 'bottom'` | `'auto'` | Where the legend goes, or `false` for none. `'auto'` places it under the plot. A chart with one series has no legend entries. |\n    36\t| `tooltip` | `boolean` | `true` | The pointer tooltip. Keyboard reading is not affected. |\n    37\t| `table` | `boolean` | `true` | The \"View as table\" control. |\n    38\t| `colors` | `Record<string, string>` | none | A CSS colour per series name (per slice label on a donut), the same on every chart. |\n    39\t| `messages` | `Partial<Messages>` | English | Replacements for built-in text. See [Messages](#messages). |\n    40\t| `locale` | `string` | the document's language | Locale for numbers and dates. |\n    41\t| `strict` | `boolean` | `false` | Throw on any error or warning found while checking a call, from that call. |\n    42\t| `onDiagnostic` | `(diagnostic: Diagnostic) => void` | none | Called once for each diagnostic the chart newly finds, in every build. |\n    43\t\n    44\t### Title\n    45\t\n    46\tWhen `title` is an object:\n    47\t\n    48\t<!-- options: Title -->\n    49\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    50\t|---|---|---|---|\n    51\t| `text` | `string` | required | The title. |\n    52\t| `visible` | `boolean` | `true` | `false` keeps it as the accessible name but hides it visually. |\n    53\t| `level` | `2 \\| 3 \\| 4 \\| 5 \\| 6` | `3` | The heading level, to fit the page's outline. |\n    54\t\n    55\t## Encoding (line, area, bar)\n    56\t\n    57\tLong rows name one `y` and a `series`; wide rows give `y` as a list and no `series`. The two forms are exclusive.\n    58\t\n    59\t<!-- options: Encoding -->\n    60\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    61\t|---|---|---|---|\n    62\t| `x` | `XKey<Row> \\| XField` | required | The x field. See [X fields](#x-fields). |\n    63\t| `y` | `ValueField \\| readonly ValueField[]` | required | The value field, or one field per series. See [Value fields](#value-fields). |\n    64\t| `series` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | none | The field whose value names each row's series (long rows only). |\n    65\t\n    66\t## Cartesian options\n    67\t\n    68\tOn line, area, bar and scatter charts.\n    69\t\n    70\t<!-- options: CartesianOptions -->\n    71\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    72\t|---|---|---|---|\n    73\t| `zoom` | `boolean \\| 'touch'` | `true` on date, time and number x | Zoom and pan. `'touch'` also takes pinch and two-finger drag on touch screens. Category x is not zoomable. |\n    74\t| `reference` | `readonly Reference[]` | none | Reference lines and bands. See [Reference](#reference). |\n    75\t\n    76\t### Reference\n    77\t\n    78\t<!-- options: Reference -->\n    79\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    80\t|---|---|---|---|\n    81\t| `x` | `XValue \\| readonly [XValue, XValue]` | none | A vertical line at an x value, or a band between two. |\n    82\t| `y` | `number \\| readonly [number, number]` | none | A horizontal line at a value, or a band between two. |\n    83\t| `label` | `string` | required | Shown beside the line or band, and listed in the summary and the table caption. |\n    84\t\n    85\t## Compact\n    86\t\n    87\t<!-- options: CompactOption -->\n    88\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    89\t|---|---|---|---|\n    90\t| `compact` | `boolean` | `false` | Line, area and bar only. Marks only, as a static image with a text alternative. See [Compact](compact.md). |\n    91\t\n    92\t## Area options\n    93\t\n    94\t<!-- options: AreaOptions -->\n    95\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n    96\t|---|---|---|---|\n    97\t| `stacked` | `boolean` | `false` | Stack the series; the tooltip adds the total. |\n    98\t\n    99\t## Bar options\n   100\t\n   101\t<!-- options: BarOptions -->\n   102\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   103\t|---|---|---|---|\n   104\t| `layout` | `'grouped' \\| 'stacked'` | `'grouped'` | Several series side by side, or stacked. |\n   105\t| `horizontal` | `boolean` | `false` | Bars from the left, categories down the side. |\n   106\t\n   107\t## Scatter options\n   108\t\n   109\tScatter takes one `y` (never a list) and an x that is a number, an instant or a date.\n   110\t\n   111\t<!-- options: ScatterOptions -->\n   112\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   113\t|---|---|---|---|\n   114\t| `x` | `XKey<Row> \\| ScatterXField` | required | The x field: like an [X field](#x-fields), with `type` limited to `'number'`, `'time'` or `'date'`, and no `ordered`. |\n   115\t| `y` | `ValueField` | required | The value field. |\n   116\t| `series` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | none | The field that groups points into series. |\n   117\t\n   118\t## Donut options\n   119\t\n   120\t<!-- options: DonutOptions -->\n   121\t| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   122\t|---|---|---|---|\n   123\t| `label` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | required | The field that names each slice. |\n   124\t| `value` | `ValueField` | required | The value of each slice. Not negative. |\n   125\t\n   126\t## Value fields\n   127\t\n   128\tA value field is a key of `Row` whose values are numbers (or `null`), or an object. A key whose values are strings is accepted only in the object form with `parse: 'number'`.\n   129\t\n   130\t<!-- options: ValueField -->\n   131\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   132\t|---|---|---|---|\n   133\t| `field` | `NumberKey<Row>`, or `StringKey<Row>` with `parse` | required | The key of the row. |\n   134\t| `parse` | `'number'` | none | The field holds numbers as strings (Postgres `bigint`, BigQuery); parse them. |\n   135\t| `label` | `string` | the field name | Axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n   136\t| `unit` | `string` | none | Follows values in tooltip, table and announcements: `'ms'`, `'USD'`. |\n   137\t| `scale` | `'percent' \\| 'ratio'` | none | Percentage data: `'percent'` is 0–100, `'ratio'` is 0–1. Never inferred. |\n   138\t| `format` | `Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| (value: number) => string` | locale grouping, up to 6 significant fraction digits | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n   139\t| `tickFormat` | `Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| (value: number) => string` | compact when it saves space | How axis ticks are written. |\n   140\t| `missing` | `'gap' \\| 'zero'` | `'gap'` | What a missing value means. `'zero'` is for counts where absence means none. |\n   141\t| `domain` | `readonly [number, number]` | from the data | Author bounds. On bar and area, widened to include zero (AX110). |\n   142\t\n   143\t## X fields\n   144\t\n   145\tAn x field is a key of `Row` whose values are numbers, strings or `Date`s, or an object.\n   146\t\n   147\t<!-- options: XField -->\n   148\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   149\t|---|---|---|---|\n   150\t| `field` | `XKey<Row>` | required | The key of the row. |\n   151\t| `type` | `'number' \\| 'time' \\| 'date' \\| 'category'` | inferred | `'date'`: calendar dates; `'time'`: instants; numbers are never inferred to be instants. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time). |\n   152\t| `ordered` | `boolean` | `false` | Category axes: the categories have a natural order, so labels may be thinned when they do not fit. |\n   153\t| `timeZone` | `'local' \\| 'UTC' \\| string` | `'local'` | For instants only: the zone they are shown in. `'local'` is the viewer's. |\n   154\t| `label` | `string` | the field name | Axis title (a date, time or category axis is titled only when you give one). |\n   155\t| `unit` | `string` | none | Follows x values in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n   156\t| `format` | `Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions \\| Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| (value: XValue) => string` | from the data | How x values are written in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n   157\t| `tickFormat` | same as `format` | from the data | How x ticks are written. |\n   158\t| `domain` | `readonly [XValue, XValue]` | from the data | Author bounds for x. |\n   159\t\n   160\tFor scatter charts the x field takes the same keys except `ordered`:\n   161\t\n   162\t<!-- options: ScatterXField -->\n   163\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   164\t|---|---|---|---|\n   165\t| `field` | `XKey<Row>` | required | As above. |\n   166\t| `type` | `'number' \\| 'time' \\| 'date'` | inferred | No `'category'`: a scatter relates two measures. |\n   167\t| `timeZone`, `label`, `unit`, `format`, `tickFormat`, `domain` | as above | as above | As on an x field. |\n   168\t\n   169\t## The chart handle\n   170\t\n   171\tEvery chart function returns a `Chart<Row, Options>`.\n   172\t\n   173\t<!-- options: Chart -->\n   174\t| Member | Type | Meaning |\n   175\t|---|---|---|\n   176\t| `update` | `(patch: Partial<Options>) => void` | Change options: a present key replaces, `undefined` restores the default, an absent key stays. See [Updating](updating.md#update-change-options). |\n   177\t| `append` | `(rows: readonly Row[], options?: { keep?: number }) => void` | Add rows at the end. |\n   178\t| `zoom` | `(domain: readonly [XValue, XValue] \\| null) => void` | Set the visible x range; `null` shows everything. |\n   179\t| `on` | `(event, listener) => () => void` | Listen to `hover`, `select` or `zoom`; returns a function that removes the listener. |\n   180\t| `diagnostics` | `readonly Diagnostic[]` | Diagnostics for the current options and data, current as soon as a call returns. |\n   181\t| `destroy` | `() => void` | Remove the chart and everything it attached. Later calls do nothing. |\n   182\t\n   183\t### append options\n   184\t\n   185\t<!-- options: AppendOptions -->\n   186\t| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n   187\t|---|---|---|---|\n   188\t| `keep` | `number` | all rows | The number of distinct x values to retain; older ones are dropped. |\n   189\t\n   190\t### Events\n   191\t\n   192\t<!-- options: ChartEvents -->\n   193\t| Event | Payload | Meaning |\n   194\t|---|---|---|\n   195\t| `hover` | `Point<Row> \\| null` | The datum under the pointer or the keyboard's point; `null` when it leaves. |\n   196\t| `select` | `Point<Row>` | The reader clicked a point, or pressed Enter or Space on it. |\n   197\t| `zoom` | `readonly [XValue, XValue] \\| null` | The visible range changed; `null` for the whole range. |\n   198\t\n   199\t<!-- options: Point -->\n   200\t| Point key | Type | Meaning |\n   201\t|---|---|---|\n   202\t| `series` | `string` | The series name; on a donut, the slice label. |\n   203\t| `x` | `XValue` | The x value. |\n   204\t| `y` | `number \\| null` | The value; `null` for no data. |\n   205\t| `row` | `Row \\| null` | Your row. With typed columns, an object built from the columns at that index. |\n   206\t| `index` | `number` | The row's index in your data. |\n   207\t\n   208\t### Diagnostic\n   209\t\n   210\t<!-- options: Diagnostic -->\n   211\t| Key | Type | Meaning |\n   212\t|---|---|---|\n   213\t| `code` | `` `AX${number}` `` | Stable code, with a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). |\n   214\t| `level` | `'error' \\| 'warning' \\| 'hint'` | See [levels](diagnostics.md#levels-and-who-is-told). |\n   215\t| `message` | `string` | What is wrong, where, and how to fix it. |\n   216\t| `chart` | `string` | The chart's title, else its element id. |\n   217\t| `field` | `string` | The field, when it is about one. |\n   218\t| `rows` | `readonly number[]` | The first offending rows in your data, at most ten. |\n   219\t| `count` | `number` | How many values or rows in all. |\n   220\t\n   221\t## validate\n   222\t\n   223\t`validate(type, options)` takes `'line'`, `'area'`, `'bar'`, `'scatter'` or `'donut'` and that type's options, runs the checks a created chart runs from its call without a DOM, and returns `Diagnostic[]`. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n   224\t\n   225\t## Messages\n   226\t\n   227\tEvery built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Placeholders in braces are filled in by the chart; an override should keep the ones it needs. The defaults below are read from the library when this site is built.\n   228\t\n   229\t[table: messages](../../../packages/axylo/src/messages/index.ts)\n   230\t\n   231\t## React components\n   232\t\n   233\t`Line`, `Area`, `Bar`, `Scatter` and `Donut` from `@axylo/react` take the chart type's options as props, plus these. See [React](react.md).\n   234\t\n   235\t<!-- options: ReactProps -->\n   236\t| Prop | Type | Meaning |\n   237\t|---|---|---|\n   238\t| `id` | `string` | Attribute of the container. |\n   239\t| `className` | `string` | Added to the container's class `axylo`. |\n   240\t| `style` | `CSSProperties` | Style of the container. |\n   241\t| `data-*` | `string \\| number \\| boolean` | Data attributes of the container. |\n   242\t| `ref` | `Ref<ChartRef>` | Receives `{ chart }`. |\n   243\t| `onHover` | `(point: Point<Row> \\| null) => void` | The `hover` event. |\n   244\t| `onSelect` | `(point: Point<Row>) => void` | The `select` event. |\n   245\t| `onZoom` | `(domain: readonly [XValue, XValue] \\| null) => void` | The `zoom` event. Not on `Donut`. |\n   246\t\n   247\t<!-- options: ChartRef -->\n   248\t| `ChartRef` key | Type | Meaning |\n   249\t|---|---|---|\n   250\t| `chart` | `Chart<Row, Options> \\| null` | The chart's handle; `null` before mount, after unmount and on the server. |\n   251\t\n   252\t## Options that do not exist\n   253\t\n   254\tThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colours from [CSS](theming.md), and motion from the reader's settings: the first render is not animated, and nothing moves under reduced motion. Each of these names, like any unknown key, is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150), which suggests the closest known option.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
