{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba apps/docs/data/diagnostics.mjs'","aggregated_output":"     1\t// One entry per diagnostic code, in registry order: the build turns each into dist/diagnostics/<code>.html.\n     2\t//\n     3\t// - `condition` and `drawn` follow the registry table in docs/02-product-spec.md, section 3.5.\n     4\t// - `see`, `why` and `fix` are Markdown.\n     5\t// - `input` is a minimal input that raises the code. With `run: 'node'` it is an expression calling\n     6\t//   `validate()`, evaluated against the built library when the site is built (the page shows its output) and\n     7\t//   again by apps/docs/test/diagnostics.test.ts. With `run: 'browser'` it is a statement list run in a page\n     8\t//   with the chart functions in scope and `target` an empty element; apps/docs/test/diagnostics.browser.test.ts\n     9\t//   checks that the code is printed. `sample` gives the registry details the page uses to show the message.\n    10\t\n    11\texport const diagnostics = [\n    12\t  {\n    13\t    code: 'AX100', level: 'error', title: 'Columns of different lengths',\n    14\t    condition: 'Typed columns have different lengths.', drawn: 'Error state.',\n    15\t    see: 'The chart shows its error state (\"The chart could not be drawn.\") and the console names the column and every column\\'s length.',\n    16\t    why: 'With typed columns, row *i* is element *i* of every column. When the lengths differ there is no way to tell which values belong together, so Axylo draws nothing rather than pair them wrongly.',\n    17\t    fix: 'Give every column the same length. When columns come from different queries, join them on x before building the arrays, and use `NaN` (in a float array) for a value that is missing.',\n    18\t    run: 'node',\n    19\t    input: `validate('line', {\n    20\t  title: 'Latency',\n    21\t  data: { columns: { t: new Float64Array([0, 60000, 120000]), ms: new Float64Array([12, 15]) } },\n    22\t  x: { field: 't', type: 'time' },\n    23\t  y: 'ms',\n    24\t})`,\n    25\t  },\n    26\t  {\n    27\t    code: 'AX101', level: 'error', title: 'A field that is not in the data',\n    28\t    condition: 'A named field is not a key of any row.', drawn: 'Error state; the message lists the fields that exist and the closest match.',\n    29\t    see: 'The chart shows its error state. The console lists the fields the rows do have and, when one is close, asks whether you meant it.',\n    30\t    why: 'Usually a typo, or rows from an API whose field names changed. With typed rows in TypeScript the same mistake does not compile; with untyped rows this is how you find it.',\n    31\t    fix: 'Use the field name the rows really have, as suggested in the message. If the field is optional in your data, make sure at least one row carries it.',\n    32\t    run: 'node',\n    33\t    input: `validate('line', {\n    34\t  title: 'Sessions',\n    35\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n    36\t  x: 'day',\n    37\t  y: 'sesions',\n    38\t})`,\n    39\t  },\n    40\t  {\n    41\t    code: 'AX102', level: 'warning', title: 'A field absent from some rows',\n    42\t    condition: 'A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows.', drawn: 'Gaps.',\n    43\t    see: 'The line has a gap where the value is absent, and the chart shows the reader a note such as \"Values not shown: 1. See table.\" The console gives the field and the first rows.',\n    44\t    why: 'An explicit `null` means \"no data\" and is drawn as a gap without comment. An absent key may mean the same thing, or a bug that dropped the field, so Axylo draws the gap and says so.',\n    45\t    fix: 'Send `null` where the value is known to be missing. If the absence is a bug, fix the code that builds the rows.',\n    46\t    run: 'node',\n    47\t    input: `validate('line', {\n    48\t  title: 'Sessions',\n    49\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02' }, { day: '2026-01-03', sessions: 140 }],\n    50\t  x: 'day',\n    51\t  y: 'sessions',\n    52\t})`,\n    53\t  },\n    54\t  {\n    55\t    code: 'AX103', level: 'warning', title: 'A value that is not a finite number',\n    56\t    condition: 'A value is `NaN` or infinite.', drawn: 'A gap.',\n    57\t    see: 'A gap where the value is, the reader\\'s note (\"Values not shown: 1. See table.\"), and in the console the field, the row and the value.',\n    58\t    why: '`NaN` and `Infinity` are almost always the result of a calculation (a division by zero, a parse that failed), not a measurement. Drawing them is impossible and treating them as zero would be wrong.',\n    59\t    fix: 'Fix the calculation, or send `null` when there is no value. In typed columns `NaN` is the normal way to say \"missing\" and does not raise this code.',\n    60\t    run: 'node',\n    61\t    input: `validate('line', {\n    62\t  title: 'Conversion',\n    63\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', rate: 0.4 }, { day: '2026-01-02', rate: 0 / 0 }, { day: '2026-01-03', rate: 0.5 }],\n    64\t  x: 'day',\n    65\t  y: 'rate',\n    66\t})`,\n    67\t  },\n    68\t  {\n    69\t    code: 'AX104', level: 'hint', title: 'Numbers sent as strings',\n    70\t    condition: 'Every value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`\\'40\\'`, `\\'-1.5\\'`).', drawn: 'Parsed and drawn; the hint suggests `parse: \\'number\\'`.',\n    71\t    see: 'The chart is drawn in full. In development, the console suggests `parse: \\'number\\'`.',\n    72\t    why: 'Many database drivers return big integers and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). A whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous, so Axylo parses it, and asks you to say so. An empty string counts as missing.',\n    73\t    fix: 'Declare the parse: `y: { field: \\'count\\', parse: \\'number\\' }`. In TypeScript a string field on a value axis does not compile without it.',\n    74\t    run: 'node',\n    75\t    input: `validate('bar', {\n    76\t  title: 'Orders',\n    77\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', count: '40' }, { day: '2026-01-02', count: '52' }],\n    78\t  x: 'day',\n    79\t  y: 'count',\n    80\t})`,\n    81\t  },\n    82\t  {\n    83\t    code: 'AX105', level: 'hint', title: 'Steps missing on a regular axis',\n    84\t    condition: 'Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis.', drawn: 'Gaps. When a series would need more than twice its observations in absent steps (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular: no gaps, and the hint says so.',\n    85\t    see: 'The line breaks where a step (a day, here) has no row, exactly as if the row were there with `null`. In development, the console names the field and the missing positions.',\n    86\t    why: 'Daily data with a missing day usually means that day had no data, not that the line should run straight from the day before to the day after. Axylo finds the step from the data and does not bridge an absent one.',\n    87\t    fix: 'If an absent step means zero (counts of events, say), declare it: `y: { field: \\'orders\\', missing: \\'zero\\' }`. If it means no data, nothing needs fixing; add explicit `null` rows to silence the hint.',\n    88\t    run: 'node',\n    89\t    input: `validate('line', {\n    90\t  title: 'Sessions',\n    91\t  data: [\n    92\t    { day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 },\n    93\t    { day: '2026-01-04', sessions: 150 }, { day: '2026-01-05', sessions: 155 },\n    94\t  ],\n    95\t  x: 'day',\n    96\t  y: 'sessions',\n    97\t})`,\n    98\t  },\n    99\t  {\n   100\t    code: 'AX106', level: 'warning', title: 'Strings among numbers',\n   101\t    condition: 'A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`\\'1,234\\'`, `\\'n/a\\'`).', drawn: 'The strings are not drawn.',\n   102\t    see: 'The string values are left out (gaps), the reader sees \"Values not shown: 1. See table.\", and the console lists the rows and the strings.',\n   103\t    why: 'A stray string in a column of numbers is a bug somewhere upstream. Strings such as `\\'1,234\\'` or `\\'n/a\\'` depend on a locale or a convention Axylo cannot know, so it does not guess.',\n   104\t    fix: 'Convert the values to numbers where the data is produced. For a column that is entirely plain decimal strings, use `parse: \\'number\\'` (see [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104)).',\n   105\t    run: 'node',\n   106\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   107\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   108\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: '1,300' }, { day: '2026-01-03', sessions: 140 }],\n   109\t  x: 'day',\n   110\t  y: 'sessions',\n   111\t})`,\n   112\t  },\n   113\t  {\n   114\t    code: 'AX107', level: 'warning', title: 'A date or time that cannot be read',\n   115\t    condition: 'A date or time cannot be parsed.', drawn: 'That row is not drawn.',\n   116\t    see: 'The row is left out, the reader sees the note, and the console shows the value and the row.',\n   117\t    why: 'The x field was recognised as dates (most values are `YYYY-MM-DD`), and this value is not a valid one: a day that does not exist, or another format.',\n   118\t    fix: 'Send calendar dates as `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM` or `YYYY`; instants as epoch milliseconds with `type: \\'time\\'`, `Date` objects, or ISO strings with an offset. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time).',\n   119\t    run: 'node',\n   120\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   121\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   122\t  data: [{ day: '2026-02-27', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-02-28', sessions: 130 }, { day: '2026-02-30', sessions: 140 }],\n   123\t  x: 'day',\n   124\t  y: 'sessions',\n   125\t})`,\n   126\t  },\n   127\t  {\n   128\t    code: 'AX108', level: 'error', title: 'Two rows for the same x',\n   129\t    condition: 'Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels.', drawn: 'That series, or the donut, is not drawn.',\n   130\t    see: 'The series with the duplicate is not drawn and the chart tells the reader it could not be drawn; a donut shows its error state. The console gives the series, the rows and the value.',\n   131\t    why: 'On a line, area or bar chart one x has one value per series. Two rows for the same x would need a rule (sum, last, average) that only you know.',\n   132\t    fix: 'Aggregate before charting, or check the query: duplicates often come from a join. On a scatter chart repeated x values are ordinary data and do not raise this.',\n   133\t    run: 'node',\n   134\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   135\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   136\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 125 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n   137\t  x: 'day',\n   138\t  y: 'sessions',\n   139\t})`,\n   140\t  },\n   141\t  {\n   142\t    code: 'AX109', level: 'hint', title: 'Numbers that look like timestamps, or like seconds',\n   143\t    condition: 'Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: \\'time\\'`.', drawn: 'As declared.',\n   144\t    see: 'The chart is drawn as declared: plain numbers on a numeric axis, or instants near 1970. In development, the console says what the numbers look like.',\n   145\t    why: 'Axylo never assumes a bare number is an instant (F4), because a wrong guess moves every point. It does notice when numbers look like epoch milliseconds or seconds, and tells you.',\n   146\t    fix: 'For epoch milliseconds, declare `x: { field: \\'ts\\', type: \\'time\\' }`. For epoch seconds, multiply by 1,000 first.',\n   147\t    run: 'node',\n   148\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   149\t  title: 'Requests',\n   150\t  data: [{ ts: 1767225600000, n: 3 }, { ts: 1767225660000, n: 5 }],\n   151\t  x: 'ts',\n   152\t  y: 'n',\n   153\t})`,\n   154\t  },\n   155\t  {\n   156\t    code: 'AX110', level: 'warning', title: 'A bar or area domain without zero',\n   157\t    condition: 'An author `domain` on bar or area excludes zero.', drawn: 'The domain is widened to zero.',\n   158\t    see: 'The value axis starts at zero anyway, and the console says the domain was widened.',\n   159\t    why: 'The length of a bar or the height of an area is its value. A baseline other than zero makes 82 look like a fraction of 97 (F1).',\n   160\t    fix: 'Remove the `domain`, or include zero in it. To show small differences, use a line chart, whose axis may start anywhere.',\n   161\t    run: 'node',\n   162\t    input: `validate('bar', {\n   163\t  title: 'Satisfaction',\n   164\t  data: [{ team: 'Billing', satisfied: 94 }, { team: 'Mobile', satisfied: 82 }],\n   165\t  x: 'team',\n   166\t  y: { field: 'satisfied', domain: [80, 100] },\n   167\t})`,\n   168\t  },\n   169\t  {\n   170\t    code: 'AX111', level: 'hint', title: 'Instants that look like calendar dates',\n   171\t    condition: 'Instants look like calendar dates: all at the same time of day, whole days apart.', drawn: 'As instants, with the time zone shown.',\n   172\t    see: 'The chart shows instants in the viewer\\'s time zone and names the zone. West of UTC, midnight UTC is the previous evening, so each daily value appears one day early. In development, the console suggests date strings.',\n   173\t    why: 'A daily metric is usually a calendar date. Sent as `2026-02-24T00:00:00Z` (or epoch milliseconds of a UTC midnight) it is an instant, which is a different thing: the same moment is a different day in different places.',\n   174\t    fix: 'Send `\\'2026-02-24\\'` (a calendar date), or keep the instants and declare `x: { field: \\'day\\', type: \\'date\\' }`, which reads each instant as its UTC calendar day. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time).',\n   175\t    run: 'node',\n   176\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   177\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   178\t  data: [\n   179\t    { day: '2026-02-24T00:00:00Z', sessions: 120 },\n   180\t    { day: '2026-02-25T00:00:00Z', sessions: 130 },\n   181\t    { day: '2026-02-26T00:00:00Z', sessions: 125 },\n   182\t  ],\n   183\t  x: 'day',\n   184\t  y: 'sessions',\n   185\t})`,\n   186\t  },\n   187\t  {\n   188\t    code: 'AX112', level: 'hint', title: 'Date-times without an offset',\n   189\t    condition: 'Date-time strings have no offset.', drawn: 'As written.',\n   190\t    see: 'The times are shown as written, in every viewer\\'s time zone alike. In development, the console asks whether these are instants.',\n   191\t    why: '`2026-02-24T09:30` without `Z` or `+01:00` is a wall-clock time: it says what a clock showed, not when. Axylo shows it as written rather than guess a zone.',\n   192\t    fix: 'If the times are instants, include the offset (`2026-02-24T09:30:00Z`). If they are local readings (opening hours, a timetable), nothing needs fixing.',\n   193\t    run: 'node',\n   194\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   195\t  title: 'Queue length',\n   196\t  data: [{ at: '2026-02-24T09:00', n: 3 }, { at: '2026-02-24T09:30', n: 7 }],\n   197\t  x: 'at',\n   198\t  y: 'n',\n   199\t})`,\n   200\t  },\n   201\t  {\n   202\t    code: 'AX120', level: 'error', title: 'A negative donut value',\n   203\t    condition: 'A donut value is negative.', drawn: 'Error state.',\n   204\t    see: 'The donut shows its error state; the console names the rows.',\n   205\t    why: 'A slice is a share of a whole. A negative share cannot be drawn as an arc, and dropping it would change every other slice\\'s percentage.',\n   206\t    fix: 'Check the data. To show gains and losses, use a bar chart, which draws negative values below zero.',\n   207\t    run: 'node',\n   208\t    input: `validate('donut', {\n   209\t  title: 'Budget',\n   210\t  data: [{ item: 'Salaries', amount: 70 }, { item: 'Refunds', amount: -5 }],\n   211\t  label: 'item',\n   212\t  value: 'amount',\n   213\t})`,\n   214\t  },\n   215\t  {\n   216\t    code: 'AX121', level: 'warning', title: 'More than eight donut slices',\n   217\t    condition: 'A donut has more than eight slices.', drawn: 'The seven largest, and the rest grouped as \"Other (n)\".',\n   218\t    see: 'Seven slices and an \"Other (n)\" slice; the reader sees \"Values shown differently from the data: n. See table.\", and the table lists every category.',\n   219\t    why: 'Beyond about eight slices, small arcs cannot be told apart or labelled, and the palette has eight colours.',\n   220\t    fix: 'Group small categories yourself (so you choose what \"Other\" means), or use a horizontal bar chart, which shows every category.',\n   221\t    run: 'node',\n   222\t    input: `validate('donut', {\n   223\t  title: 'Sessions by country',\n   224\t  data: ['US', 'DE', 'FR', 'JP', 'BR', 'IN', 'GB', 'CA', 'ES'].map((country, i) => ({ country, sessions: 90 - i * 8 })),\n   225\t  label: 'country',\n   226\t  value: 'sessions',\n   227\t})`,\n   228\t  },\n   229\t  {\n   230\t    code: 'AX130', level: 'warning', title: 'More than eight series',\n   231\t    condition: 'A chart has more than eight series.', drawn: 'All series, told apart by colour and dash or marker.',\n   232\t    see: 'Every series is drawn. Beyond eight, colours repeat, and each series still has its own combination of colour and dash or marker.',\n   233\t    why: 'Eight colours is about as many as a reader can keep apart, also with a colour-vision deficiency (F9).',\n   234\t    fix: 'Filter to the series that matter, group the rest, or split the chart into small multiples.',\n   235\t    run: 'node',\n   236\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   237\t  title: 'Sessions by region',\n   238\t  data: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E', 'F', 'G', 'H', 'I'].flatMap(region =>\n   239\t    ['2026-01-01', '2026-01-02'].map(day => ({ day, region, sessions: 100 }))),\n   240\t  x: 'day',\n   241\t  y: 'sessions',\n   242\t  series: 'region',\n   243\t})`,\n   244\t  },\n   245\t  {\n   246\t    code: 'AX131', level: 'hint', title: 'Several series in a compact chart',\n   247\t    condition: 'A compact chart has more than one series.', drawn: 'Drawn, with dash differences.',\n   248\t    see: 'The lines are drawn with different dashes, since a compact chart has no legend. In development, the console suggests one series per compact chart.',\n   249\t    why: 'A compact chart has no legend, axes or tooltip, so nothing tells the reader which line is which.',\n   250\t    fix: 'Use one compact chart per series, or a full chart with a legend.',\n   251\t    run: 'node',\n   252\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   253\t  labelledBy: 'kpi-heading',\n   254\t  compact: true,\n   255\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', a: 1, b: 2 }, { day: '2026-01-02', a: 2, b: 1 }],\n   256\t  x: 'day',\n   257\t  y: ['a', 'b'],\n   258\t})`,\n   259\t  },\n   260\t  {\n   261\t    code: 'AX132', level: 'hint', title: 'More bars than pixels',\n   262\t    condition: 'More bars than pixels along the category axis.', drawn: 'Drawn; the hint suggests aggregating.',\n   263\t    see: 'Every bar is drawn, thinner than a pixel. In development, the console suggests aggregating.',\n   264\t    why: 'Bars narrower than a pixel blur into an area and cannot be picked or compared.',\n   265\t    fix: 'Aggregate (weeks instead of days), show fewer categories, or use a line or area chart.',\n   266\t    run: 'browser',\n   267\t    sample: { chart: 'Requests per minute', details: { field: 'minute' } },\n   268\t    input: `target.style.width = '300px';\n   269\tbar(target, {\n   270\t  title: 'Requests per minute',\n   271\t  data: Array.from({ length: 2000 }, (_, i) => ({ minute: i, requests: i % 7 })),\n   272\t  x: { field: 'minute', type: 'category' },\n   273\t  y: 'requests',\n   274\t});`,\n   275\t  },\n   276\t  {\n   277\t    code: 'AX133', level: 'hint', title: 'A very large scatter chart',\n   278\t    condition: 'A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points.', drawn: 'Drawn; the hint says drawing may be slow.',\n   279\t    see: 'All points are drawn. In development, the console says drawing may be slow.',\n   280\t    why: 'Scatter points are not simplified in 1.0, so every point is drawn on every frame.',\n   281\t    fix: 'Sample or bin the data, or filter it to the range the reader needs.',\n   282\t    run: 'node',\n   283\t    input: `validate('scatter', {\n   284\t  title: 'Requests',\n   285\t  data: { columns: { size: new Float64Array(50001).map((_, i) => i), ms: new Float64Array(50001).fill(10) } },\n   286\t  x: 'size',\n   287\t  y: 'ms',\n   288\t})`,\n   289\t  },\n   290\t  {\n   291\t    code: 'AX134', level: 'error', title: 'Too many series × x positions',\n   292\t    condition: 'Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000.', drawn: 'Error state; the message gives both counts.',\n   293\t    see: 'The chart shows its error state. The console gives the number of series and of x positions.',\n   294\t    why: 'Line, area and bar charts align every series on every x. Long rows with many series that share few x values (for example one series per user) multiply into more slots than a chart can hold.',\n   295\t    fix: 'Use fewer series (the top ten and \"the rest\"), or a scatter chart, which does not align series on x.',\n   296\t    run: 'node',\n   297\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   298\t  title: 'Sessions per user',\n   299\t  data: Array.from({ length: 4500 }, (_, i) => ({ t: i, user: 'u' + i, sessions: 1 })),\n   300\t  x: 't',\n   301\t  y: 'sessions',\n   302\t  series: 'user',\n   303\t})`,\n   304\t  },\n   305\t  {\n   306\t    code: 'AX140', level: 'warning', title: 'A chart without a name',\n   307\t    condition: 'No `title` and no `labelledBy`.', drawn: 'A generated name.',\n   308\t    see: 'The chart is drawn and named for assistive technology from its type and fields (\"Line chart of sessions by day\"). The console says it has no name. The same code is raised when `labelledBy` names an element that does not exist.',\n   309\t    why: 'A screen-reader user moving through a dashboard hears each chart\\'s name first. A generated name says what is plotted, not what it means.',\n   310\t    fix: 'Give a `title`, or, when the card around the chart already has a heading, its id: `labelledBy: \\'card-heading\\'`. A title can be hidden visually with `title: { text: \\'…\\', visible: false }`.',\n   311\t    run: 'node',\n   312\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   313\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n   314\t  x: 'day',\n   315\t  y: 'sessions',\n   316\t})`,\n   317\t  },\n   318\t  {\n   319\t    code: 'AX141', level: 'warning', title: 'Series colours too close to the background',\n   320\t    condition: 'The palette is below 3:1 against the resolved background.', drawn: 'As themed.',\n   321\t    see: 'The chart is drawn with your colours. The console says that the palette is below 3:1 against the background behind the chart; the diagnostic\\'s `count` says how many series colours are.',\n   322\t    why: 'Marks need 3:1 against what is behind them to be seen (WCAG 1.4.11). Axylo measures the colours the browser actually resolved against the colour actually behind the chart, so a dark card on a light page is measured as dark.',\n   323\t    fix: 'Choose series colours (`--axylo-series-1` … or `colors`) with at least 3:1 against your background, or set `--axylo-background` to the colour your card really has.',\n   324\t    run: 'browser',\n   325\t    sample: { chart: 'Sessions', details: { count: 1 } },\n   326\t    input: `target.style.setProperty('--axylo-series-1', '#eeeeee');\n   327\tline(target, {\n   328\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   329\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n   330\t  x: 'day',\n   331\t  y: 'sessions',\n   332\t});`,\n   333\t  },\n   334\t  {\n   335\t    code: 'AX142', level: 'hint', title: 'Not enough room for a plot',\n   336\t    condition: 'The plot would be smaller than 120 × 80 CSS px.', drawn: 'The title, the summary and the table control instead of the plot.',\n   337\t    see: 'The chart shows its title, a one-line summary and the \"View as table\" control, so the data stays reachable. In development, the console says the plot is too small.',\n   338\t    why: 'A plot that small cannot hold readable axes (L8). Drawing it anyway would produce overlapping or clipped labels.',\n   339\t    fix: 'Give the container more room, or use `compact: true` for a sparkline in a small tile.',\n   340\t    run: 'browser',\n   341\t    sample: { chart: 'Sessions' },\n   342\t    input: `target.style.width = '110px';\n   343\ttarget.style.height = '90px';\n   344\tline(target, {\n   345\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   346\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n   347\t  x: 'day',\n   348\t  y: 'sessions',\n   349\t});`,\n   350\t  },\n   351\t  {\n   352\t    code: 'AX143', level: 'hint', title: 'Labels hidden after every remedy',\n   353\t    condition: 'Labels still do not fit after thinning, wrapping, rotating and truncating.', drawn: 'A note tells the reader.',\n   354\t    see: 'Some category labels are left out and the chart shows \"Some labels hidden. See table.\" In development, the console suggests horizontal bars.',\n   355\t    why: 'Axylo tries, in order: thinning (ordered axes only), wrapping to three lines, rotating up to 45°, and truncating in the middle. It never overlaps labels and never hides one without telling the reader (L1).',\n   356\t    fix: 'Give the chart more width, shorten the labels, or use `horizontal: true` on a bar chart, where long labels read naturally. Axylo does not switch orientation for you: that would change what the chart says at a glance.',\n   357\t    run: 'browser',\n   358\t    sample: { chart: 'Tickets by team', details: { field: 'team', count: 12 } },\n   359\t    input: `target.style.width = '320px';\n   360\tbar(target, {\n   361\t  title: 'Tickets by team',\n   362\t  data: Array.from({ length: 30 }, (_, i) => ({ team: 'Customer Success and Onboarding ' + (i + 1), tickets: 10 + i })),\n   363\t  x: 'team',\n   364\t  y: 'tickets',\n   365\t});`,\n   366\t  },\n   367\t  {\n   368\t    code: 'AX150', level: 'warning', title: 'An option Axylo does not know',\n   369\t    condition: 'An option key is unknown, or does not apply to the chart type.', drawn: 'The option is ignored; the message suggests the closest known key.',\n   370\t    see: 'The chart is drawn without the option. The console names it and, when one is close, the option you probably meant.',\n   371\t    why: 'A misspelled option would otherwise be ignored in silence. In TypeScript the same mistake does not compile.',\n   372\t    fix: 'Use the name from the message, or check the [options reference](options.md) for the chart type. Options that do not exist on purpose (`width`, `height`, `theme`, `palette`, `animation`) are explained there.',\n   373\t    run: 'node',\n   374\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   375\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   376\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }],\n   377\t  x: 'day',\n   378\t  y: 'sessions',\n   379\t  tooltips: false,\n   380\t})`,\n   381\t  },\n   382\t  {\n   383\t    code: 'AX151', level: 'warning', title: 'Appended rows out of order',\n   384\t    condition: '`append` rows are before the last x.', drawn: 'Accepted through the slower full update.',\n   385\t    see: 'The rows are added where they belong in x order. The console says they arrived out of order.',\n   386\t    why: '`append` is the fast path for rows that extend the series. A row before the last x needs the whole frame rebuilt, which is correct but slower, and usually means the feed is not ordered.',\n   387\t    fix: 'Append rows in x order, at or after the last x. To replace data in bulk, use `update({ data })`.',\n   388\t    run: 'browser',\n   389\t    sample: { chart: 'Sessions', details: { field: 'day', rows: [2] } },\n   390\t    input: `const chart = line(target, {\n   391\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   392\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-03', sessions: 130 }],\n   393\t  x: 'day',\n   394\t  y: 'sessions',\n   395\t});\n   396\tchart.append([{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 110 }]);`,\n   397\t  },\n   398\t  {\n   399\t    code: 'AX152', level: 'warning', title: 'An unknown time zone',\n   400\t    condition: '`timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows.', drawn: 'The viewer\\'s zone is used.',\n   401\t    see: 'Instants are shown in the viewer\\'s own zone, which the chart names. The console shows the zone it did not recognise.',\n   402\t    why: 'Time zones come from the browser\\'s `Intl` data. A misspelled or retired zone name cannot be resolved.',\n   403\t    fix: 'Use an IANA zone name such as `\\'Europe/Paris\\'`, or `\\'UTC\\'`, or leave `timeZone` out for the viewer\\'s zone.',\n   404\t    run: 'node',\n   405\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   406\t  title: 'Requests',\n   407\t  data: [{ ts: 1767225600000, n: 3 }, { ts: 1767229200000, n: 5 }],\n   408\t  x: { field: 'ts', type: 'time', timeZone: 'Europe/Pariss' },\n   409\t  y: 'n',\n   410\t})`,\n   411\t  },\n   412\t  {\n   413\t    code: 'AX160', level: 'warning', title: 'A custom formatter threw',\n   414\t    condition: 'A custom `format` or `tickFormat` function threw.', drawn: 'The default format for that value.',\n   415\t    see: 'The affected text uses Axylo\\'s default format; the reader sees a note that values are shown differently. The console names the field.',\n   416\t    why: 'A formatter is your code running inside the chart. One that throws for an unexpected value (a `null`, a negative) must not take the chart down.',\n   417\t    fix: 'Make the formatter handle every value of the field, or use an `Intl.NumberFormat` or `Intl.DateTimeFormat` options object instead of a function.',\n   418\t    run: 'browser',\n   419\t    sample: { chart: 'Sessions', details: { field: 'sessions' } },\n   420\t    input: `line(target, {\n   421\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   422\t  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n   423\t  x: 'day',\n   424\t  y: { field: 'sessions', tickFormat: () => { throw new Error('not ready'); } },\n   425\t});`,\n   426\t  },\n   427\t  {\n   428\t    code: 'AX199', level: 'error', title: 'Axylo failed',\n   429\t    condition: 'Axylo itself failed while building the chart.', drawn: 'Error state with a generic message.',\n   430\t    see: 'The chart shows \"Something went wrong while drawing the chart.\" The error is printed with `console.error` and passed to `onDiagnostic`.',\n   431\t    why: 'Something inside Axylo threw. The chart catches it so that the rest of the page keeps working.',\n   432\t    fix: 'If your data or options are ordinary, this is a defect in Axylo: please report it with the error printed in the console. The minimal input below is not a defect: it makes reading a row throw, which Axylo reports the same way because it cannot tell the two apart.',\n   433\t    run: 'node',\n   434\t    input: `validate('line', {\n   435\t  title: 'Sessions',\n   436\t  data: [{ get day() { throw new Error('not loaded'); }, sessions: 1 }],\n   437\t  x: 'day',\n   438\t  y: 'sessions',\n   439\t})`,\n   440\t  },\n   441\t];\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
