{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg --files | rg -i 'conformance|dx12|agent.rehears|csp|a9-|screen-reader|accessibility-matrix'\nrg -n 'AX199|console\\\\.|emit\\\\(' packages/axylo/src/core/delivery.ts\nnl -ba docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md | sed -n '8,90p'\ncat research/harness/src/libs/{chartjs.js,chartjs-tuned.js,recharts.jsx}\ncat research/harness/src/data.js | head -140\"","aggregated_output":"apps/docs/pages/conformance.md\ntests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts\n4: * Errors and warnings print with `console.warn` once per code per page in every build; hints print with\n5: * `console.info`, also once per code per page, and only in development builds. `onDiagnostic` receives every\n     8\t\n     9\tSlot order: blue, orange, green, purple, red, cyan, magenta, slate. That is the first version's order. Hue families are kept per slot.\n    10\t\n    11\t| Slot | Family | Light | OKLCH | Contrast on #ffffff | Dark | OKLCH | Contrast on #16181d | Hue shift |\n    12\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    13\t| 1 | blue | `#4a94eb` | 0.66 0.150 254 | 3.12 | `#64acfa` | 0.73 0.135 252 | 7.46 | 2° |\n    14\t| 2 | orange | `#e97200` | 0.68 0.175 52 | 3.06 | `#de7b2e` | 0.68 0.150 54 | 5.90 | 2° |\n    15\t| 3 | green | `#136102` | 0.43 0.140 141 | 7.67 | `#b2e390` | 0.86 0.121 133 | 12.08 | 8° |\n    16\t| 4 | purple | `#7314a2` | 0.43 0.205 310 | 8.93 | `#9149b9` | 0.54 0.177 312 | 3.26 | 2° |\n    17\t| 5 | red | `#bd585e` | 0.59 0.130 18 | 4.45 | `#c3513d` | 0.58 0.151 32 | 3.86 | 14° |\n    18\t| 6 | cyan | `#007baa` | 0.55 0.115 234 | 4.76 | `#71d8ff` | 0.83 0.110 225 | 10.97 | 9° |\n    19\t| 7 | magenta | `#a52265` | 0.49 0.175 355 | 6.96 | `#f898b5` | 0.79 0.119 1 | 8.58 | 6° |\n    20\t| 8 | slate | `#9293a5` | 0.67 0.026 283 | 3.03 | `#7a7f93` | 0.60 0.031 274 | 4.47 | 9° |\n    21\t\n    22\t**One token changes.** The dark focus token goes from `#79a8ff` to a near-white `#e3e8f0`, which is 14.4:1 on #16181d. The old focus blue sat at OKLab L 0.735, exactly where a dark-mode series blue belongs. With it kept, no dark set I found had a first blue above L 0.55. The exact search with the blue asked for at 0.62 ended at `#3970c2`, L 0.55 (`configs/dark-n2.json`, best within 300 s). The light focus `#1f5fcc` is unchanged; `keyboard.browser.test.ts` hardcodes it. Every other token is unchanged.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Before and after.**\n    25\t\n    26\t| | 51e1be5 | Now |\n    27\t|---|---|---|\n    28\t| Light OKLab L, lowest to highest | 0.39 to 0.68 | 0.43 to 0.68 |\n    29\t| Light, spread across the first three | 0.26 | 0.25 |\n    30\t| Light, largest chroma | 0.17 | 0.205 (purple) |\n    31\t| Dark first blue | `#1c73c5`, L 0.55, 3.64:1 | `#64acfa`, L 0.73, 7.46:1 |\n    32\t| Dark, largest chroma | 0.15 | 0.177 (purple) |\n    33\t| Largest light/dark hue shift | 18.5° (slate), 8° chromatic | 13.6° (red) |\n    34\t| Byte-rounded simulation, worst pair | 0.098 (light protan 6–8) | 0.102 |\n    35\t| 25% fills on an 8-bit raster, worst neighbours | 0.0590 (review's raster) | 0.063 (Chrome, canvas and SVG) |\n    36\t\n    37\tThe dark blue now has ordinary dark-mode lightness. The light set did not get much more even. Slot 3's green is still the heaviest of the first three lines on white (L 0.43 against 0.66 and 0.68). \"Tried and failed\" below says what I searched for that and why I stopped.\n    38\t\n    39\t![Light scheme preview](../../research/palette/preview-light.png)\n    40\t\n    41\t![Dark scheme preview](../../research/palette/preview-dark.png)\n    42\t\n    43\tEach preview shows:\n    44\t- the swatches, with hex, contrast and nearest neighbour;\n    45\t- lines with 3, 5 and 8 series at 2 px, with Axylo's end markers;\n    46\t- three overlapping areas at 25%;\n    47\t- eight stacked areas at 80%;\n    48\t- an eight-slice donut;\n    49\t- grouped bars.\n    50\t\n    51\tThe same charts are repeated under the three simulations (the Machado matrices as SVG color matrices in linear RGB) and in grayscale. Source: `research/palette/preview.html`.\n    52\t\n    53\t## What is measured, and on what\n    54\t\n    55\tThere are three forms of measurement:\n    56\t\n    57\t- **Analytic.** The 8-bit hex is decoded, then simulated or composited in floating point, then converted to OKLab.\n    58\t- **Bytes.** Values are rounded where an 8-bit image would round them. A simulated color is encoded and rounded to the nearest byte before the OKLab conversion; that is what the simulated rows of the preview PNGs hold. A translucent fill is quantised as a raster would quantise it: alpha at round(α×255), each channel rounded. That reproduces Chrome 157's canvas and SVG fills on white exactly and is within one byte on #16181d. The second variant, alpha at floor(α×255), is what the review's CPU Skia raster did.\n    59\t- **Chrome.** The fill pixels Chrome actually drew, canvas and SVG (`research/palette/raster.mjs`).\n    60\t\n    61\tThe unit test requires the analytic and the byte forms. It checks the recorded Chrome pixels against the 0.06 limit, and checks that each recorded pixel is within one byte of the model. A stricter margin is the worst case over every byte color within one byte per channel of both fills. It is recorded but not required, because I found no palette that meets it together with the rest (0.054–0.056 is the best I saw; see below).\n    62\t\n    63\t## Constraints, measured\n    64\t\n    65\tThese come from `research/palette/palette.json`, which `score.mjs` writes. The unit test recomputes each value with its own code and compares it at the written precision, including which pair is closest.\n    66\t\n    67\t| Constraint | Check | Limit | Light | Dark |\n    68\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    69\t| 1 | lowest contrast on background | ≥ 3 | 3.03 (slate) | 3.26 (purple) |\n    70\t| 2 | min pairwise ΔE, all 8, normal / protan / deutan / tritan | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.104 / 0.103 / 0.102 |\n    71\t| 2, bytes | the same after the simulation is rounded to bytes | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 |\n    72\t| 3 | first 4, worst vision (analytic; bytes) | ≥ 0.14 | 0.156 protan; 0.155 | 0.158 tritan; 0.159 |\n    73\t| 3 | first 3, 5, 6, worst vision (analytic) | ≥ 0.10 | 0.156 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.158 / 0.103 / 0.103 |\n    74\t| 4 | largest light/dark OKLCH hue shift | ≤ 30° | 13.6° (red) | same pairs |\n    75\t| 5 | nearest series color to focus / reference / muted | ≥ 0.10 | 0.101 (cyan) / 0.152 (green) / 0.110 (cyan) | 0.138 (cyan) / 0.104 (cyan) / 0.113 (slate) |\n    76\t| 6 | neighbour fills at 25%: ideal / byte models / Chrome canvas / Chrome SVG | ≥ 0.06 | 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 / 0.063 | 0.064 / 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 |\n    77\t| 6 | neighbour fills at 80%: ideal / byte models / Chrome | ≥ 0.06 | 0.201 / 0.202 / 0.202 | 0.179 / 0.179 / 0.179 |\n    78\t| 6, recorded only | 25% fills, worst case within one byte | (0.06) | 0.056 | 0.056 |\n    79\t\n    80\t\"Muted\" is my addition to constraint 5: the donut draws its \"Other\" slice in the muted token, next to series slices.\n    81\t\n    82\t### Prefix minima (constraint 3)\n    83\t\n    84\tEach cell is analytic / bytes, with the closest slots.\n    85\t\n    86\t| Prefix | Vision | Light | Dark |\n    87\t|---|---|---|---|\n    88\t| first 3 | normal | 0.319 / 0.319 (1–2) | 0.251 / 0.251 (1–3) |\n    89\t| first 3 | protan | 0.156 / 0.155 (2–3) | 0.247 / 0.246 (1–3) |\n    90\t| first 3 | deutan | 0.270 / 0.271 (2–3) | 0.178 / 0.177 (2–3) |\nimport { Chart } from 'chart.js/auto';\nimport 'chartjs-adapter-date-fns'; // a date adapter is required before a time scale works\n\nexport const meta = { name: 'Chart.js', pkg: 'chart.js' };\nconst canvas = el => el.appendChild(document.createElement('canvas'));\n// maintainAspectRatio:false is needed for the chart to fill a fixed-height container\nconst base = d => ({ maintainAspectRatio: false, plugins: { title: { display: true, text: d.title } } });\n\nexport function trend(el, d) {\n  const chart = new Chart(canvas(el), {\n    type: 'line',\n    data: { labels: d.t, datasets: d.series.map(s => ({ label: s.name, data: s.values })) },\n    options: { ...base(d), scales: { x: { type: 'time' }, y: { title: { display: true, text: d.yTitle } } } },\n  });\n  return { chart, destroy: () => chart.destroy() };\n}\n\nexport function bars(el, d) {\n  const chart = new Chart(canvas(el), {\n    type: 'bar',\n    data: { labels: d.categories, datasets: [{ label: d.name, data: d.values }] },\n    options: { ...base(d), scales: { y: { title: { display: true, text: d.yTitle } } } },\n  });\n  return { chart, destroy: () => chart.destroy() };\n}\n\nexport function parts(el, d) {\n  const chart = new Chart(canvas(el), {\n    type: 'doughnut',\n    data: { labels: d.labels, datasets: [{ label: d.name, data: d.values }] },\n    options: base(d),\n  });\n  return { chart, destroy: () => chart.destroy() };\n}\n\nexport function dirty(el, d) {\n  const chart = new Chart(canvas(el), {\n    type: 'line',\n    data: { labels: d.categories, datasets: [{ label: d.name, data: d.values }] },\n    options: base(d),\n  });\n  return { chart, destroy: () => chart.destroy() };\n}\n\nexport function scale(el, d) {\n  const chart = new Chart(canvas(el), {\n    type: 'line',\n    data: { labels: d.t, datasets: [{ label: d.name, data: d.y }] },\n    options: { ...base(d), scales: { x: { type: 'time' } } },\n  });\n  return { chart, destroy: () => chart.destroy() };\n}\n\nexport function stream(el, d) {\n  const h = scale(el, d), { chart } = h;\n  h.push = (x, y) => {\n    chart.data.labels.push(x); chart.data.labels.shift();\n    chart.data.datasets[0].data.push(y); chart.data.datasets[0].data.shift();\n    chart.update();\n  };\n  return h;\n}\n\nexport function dates(el, d) {\n  const chart = new Chart(canvas(el), {\n    type: 'line',\n    data: { labels: d.days, datasets: d.series.map(s => ({ label: s.name, data: s.values })) },\n    options: { ...base(d), scales: { x: { type: 'time' } } },\n  });\n  return { chart, destroy: () => chart.destroy() };\n}\n// Chart.js with its documented performance options: no parsing, decimation plugin, no animation, no point markers.\nimport { Chart } from 'chart.js/auto';\nimport 'chartjs-adapter-date-fns';\nexport const meta = { name: 'Chart.js (tuned)', pkg: 'chart.js' };\nexport function scale(el, d) {\n  const data = d.t.map((x, i) => ({ x, y: d.y[i] }));\n  const chart = new Chart(el.appendChild(document.createElement('canvas')), {\n    type: 'line',\n    data: { datasets: [{ label: d.name, data, pointRadius: 0, borderWidth: 1 }] },\n    options: { maintainAspectRatio: false, animation: false, parsing: false, normalized: true,\n      plugins: { title: { display: true, text: d.title }, decimation: { enabled: true, algorithm: 'min-max' } },\n      scales: { x: { type: 'time' } } },\n  });\n  return { chart, data, destroy: () => chart.destroy() };\n}\nexport function stream(el, d) {\n  const h = scale(el, d);\n  h.push = (x, y) => { h.data.push({ x, y }); h.data.shift(); h.chart.update('none'); };\n  return h;\n}\nimport { LineChart, Line, BarChart, Bar, PieChart, Pie, Cell, XAxis, YAxis, CartesianGrid, Tooltip, Legend, ResponsiveContainer } from 'recharts';\nimport { mount } from './_react.jsx';\n\nexport const meta = { name: 'Recharts', pkg: 'recharts' };\n// Recharts has no default palette (every Line is #3182bd, every Pie sector #808080) and no title component.\n// Colors below are the ones used throughout the official examples.\nconst colors = ['#8884d8', '#82ca9d', '#ffc658'];\nconst pieColors = ['#0088FE', '#00C49F', '#FFBB28', '#FF8042'];\nconst day = t => new Date(t).toLocaleDateString(); // without a tickFormatter a time axis prints raw epoch numbers\n\nconst TimeLines = ({ rows, names, yTitle }) => (\n  <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\">\n    <LineChart data={rows}>\n      <CartesianGrid strokeDasharray=\"3 3\" />\n      <XAxis dataKey=\"t\" type=\"number\" scale=\"time\" domain={['dataMin', 'dataMax']} tickFormatter={day} />\n      <YAxis label={yTitle && { value: yTitle, angle: -90, position: 'insideLeft' }} />\n      <Tooltip labelFormatter={day} />\n      <Legend />\n      {names.map((n, i) => <Line key={n} dataKey={n} stroke={colors[i]} />)}\n    </LineChart>\n  </ResponsiveContainer>\n);\n\nexport function trend(el, d) {\n  const rows = d.t.map((t, i) => Object.fromEntries([['t', t], ...d.series.map(s => [s.name, s.values[i]])]));\n  const r = mount(el, p => <TimeLines {...p} />);\n  r.draw({ rows, names: d.series.map(s => s.name), yTitle: d.yTitle });\n  return r;\n}\n\nexport function bars(el, d) {\n  const rows = d.categories.map((name, i) => ({ name, [d.name]: d.values[i] }));\n  const r = mount(el, () => (\n    <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\">\n      <BarChart data={rows}>\n        <CartesianGrid strokeDasharray=\"3 3\" />\n        <XAxis dataKey=\"name\" />\n        <YAxis label={{ value: d.yTitle, angle: -90, position: 'insideLeft' }} />\n        <Tooltip />\n        <Bar dataKey={d.name} fill={colors[0]} />\n      </BarChart>\n    </ResponsiveContainer>\n  ));\n  r.draw();\n  return r;\n}\n\nexport function parts(el, d) {\n  const rows = d.labels.map((name, i) => ({ name, value: d.values[i] }));\n  const r = mount(el, () => (\n    <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\">\n      <PieChart>\n        <Pie data={rows} dataKey=\"value\" nameKey=\"name\" innerRadius=\"50%\" label>\n          {rows.map((row, i) => <Cell key={row.name} fill={pieColors[i % pieColors.length]} />)}\n        </Pie>\n        <Tooltip />\n        <Legend />\n      </PieChart>\n    </ResponsiveContainer>\n  ));\n  r.draw();\n  return r;\n}\n\nexport function dirty(el, d) {\n  const rows = d.categories.map((name, i) => ({ name, [d.name]: d.values[i] }));\n  const r = mount(el, () => (\n    <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\">\n      <LineChart data={rows}>\n        <XAxis dataKey=\"name\" />\n        <YAxis />\n        <Tooltip />\n        <Line dataKey={d.name} stroke={colors[0]} />\n      </LineChart>\n    </ResponsiveContainer>\n  ));\n  r.draw();\n  return r;\n}\n\nexport function scale(el, d) {\n  const rows = d.t.map((t, i) => ({ t, [d.name]: d.y[i] }));\n  const r = mount(el, p => <TimeLines {...p} />);\n  r.rows = rows; r.names = [d.name];\n  r.draw({ rows, names: r.names });\n  return r;\n}\n\nexport function stream(el, d) {\n  const r = scale(el, d);\n  r.push = (x, y) => { r.rows = [...r.rows.slice(1), { t: x, [d.name]: y }]; r.draw({ rows: r.rows, names: r.names }); };\n  return r;\n}\n\n// Recharts has no date parsing: date strings go on a category axis and are printed as written.\nexport function dates(el, d) {\n  const rows = d.days.map((day, i) => Object.fromEntries([['day', day], ...d.series.map(s => [s.name, s.values[i]])]));\n  const r = mount(el, () => (\n    <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\">\n      <LineChart data={rows}>\n        <XAxis dataKey=\"day\" />\n        <YAxis />\n        <Tooltip />\n        <Legend />\n        {d.series.map((s, i) => <Line key={s.name} dataKey={s.name} stroke={colors[i]} />)}\n      </LineChart>\n    </ResponsiveContainer>\n  ));\n  r.draw();\n  return r;\n}\n// Shared, deterministic datasets. Every library receives exactly these values.\nexport function rng(seed) {\n  let s = seed >>> 0;\n  return () => { s = (s + 0x6D2B79F5) | 0; let t = Math.imul(s ^ (s >>> 15), 1 | s); t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t; return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };\n}\nconst DAY = 864e5;\nconst START = Date.UTC(2026, 0, 1);\n\n// Task \"trend\": 3 series x 120 daily points (UTC midnights). \"Referral\" has a 7-day outage (nulls).\nexport const trend = (() => {\n  const r = rng(7), n = 120;\n  const t = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => START + i * DAY);\n  const mk = (base, growth, noise, season) => t.map((_, i) => Math.round(base + growth * i + season * Math.sin((i / 7) * 2 * Math.PI) + (r() - 0.5) * noise));\n  const series = [\n    { name: 'Direct', values: mk(31000, 120, 4000, 3000) },\n    { name: 'Organic search', values: mk(18000, 60, 3000, 2500) },\n    { name: 'Referral', values: mk(4200, -8, 1200, 400) },\n  ];\n  for (let i = 60; i < 67; i++) series[2].values[i] = null;\n  return { title: 'Daily sessions by channel', yTitle: 'Sessions', t, series };\n})();\n\n// Task \"bars\": one series, all values between 82 and 97 (tests zero baseline), one long label.\nexport const bars = {\n  title: 'Customer satisfaction by team', yTitle: 'Satisfied (%)', name: 'Satisfied (%)',\n  categories: ['Billing', 'Customer Success & Onboarding', 'Platform', 'Mobile', 'Data', 'Security', 'Sales Eng.'],\n  values: [94, 88, 97, 82, 91, 85, 90],\n};\n\n// Task \"parts\": six shares, two of them small.\nexport const parts = {\n  title: 'Sessions by browser', name: 'Share (%)',\n  labels: ['Chrome', 'Safari', 'Edge', 'Firefox', 'Samsung Internet', 'Other'],\n  values: [46.2, 27.5, 14.1, 8.9, 2.1, 1.2],\n};\n\n// Task \"dirty\": values a real app sends by mistake. Correct reading: Wed, Thu, Sat are missing; Fri is ambiguous (a string).\nexport const dirty = {\n  title: 'Dirty input', name: 'Value',\n  categories: ['Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri', 'Sat', 'Sun'],\n  values: [10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70],\n};\n\n// Task \"scale\": one series, n points, one per minute, random walk.\nexport function scale(n) {\n  const r = rng(1), t = new Array(n), y = new Array(n);\n  let v = 100;\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { v += (r() - 0.5) * 2; t[i] = START + i * 60000; y[i] = Math.round(v * 100) / 100; }\n  return { title: `${n.toLocaleString('en-US')} points`, name: 'Value', t, y };\n}\n\n// Task \"stream\": 600-point window; the harness then pushes one new point per frame.\nexport function stream() {\n  const d = scale(600);\n  const r = rng(2);\n  let v = d.y[d.y.length - 1], i = d.t.length;\n  return { ...d, title: 'Streaming window', next: () => { v += (r() - 0.5) * 2; return [START + i++ * 60000, Math.round(v * 100) / 100]; } };\n}\n\n// Task \"dates\": the trend series keyed by calendar-date strings (no time, no zone), to separate\n// \"this library shifts instants into the viewer's zone\" from \"this library cannot show a date as a date\".\nexport const dates = { ...trend, days: trend.t.map(t => new Date(t).toISOString().slice(0, 10)) };\n\n// Tasks \"dirty-null\", \"dirty-nan\", \"dirty-string\", \"dirty-undefined\": one bad value at a time, in a line of\n// seven otherwise valid points, so each outcome can be attributed to one kind of input.\nexport function dirtyOne(kind) {\n  const bad = { null: null, nan: NaN, string: '40', undefined: undefined }[kind];\n  return { ...dirty, title: `One bad value: ${kind}`, values: [10, 20, 30, bad, 50, 60, 70] };\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
