0 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n222:    <p id=\"ax7-s\" class=\"axylo-summary\">Line chart, 3 series, …</p>\n228:  <p class=\"axylo-note\" role=\"status\">Values not shown: 2. See table.</p>\n235:Ids are generated per chart. The plot's role, and how a focused point is conveyed, are not fixed here: M1 runs a spike with three candidate patterns and M4 confirms the choice (section 4).\n247:research/              Phase 1 evidence and the ten-library harness\n257:Each milestone ends with outcomes checked by a command, an example that runs in the docs site, and the documentation page for what was built. Durations are ceilings for one owner working with coding agents, not targets. Every milestone from M1 on re-runs the acceptance matrix, the size report and the benchmark smoke set, and records the results.\n263:- The acceptance matrix as data, every row \"not yet\", with applicability decided per chart type.\n273:- **Screen-reader pattern spike:** three candidates on the `trend` chart (application region with a live region; a roving labelled proxy; an adjustable control with value text), rehearsed on the five reader and browser pairs.\n282:Example: \"Line chart from an API response\". Docs: Getting started, Data and fields.\n284:### M2. Faithful data, on the line chart (ceiling: 8 days)\n296:### M3. Reading the chart (ceiling: 10 days)\n302:- L1's property test in three engines, including text-spacing overrides and a late-loading font, on a category-axis line chart with long labels.\n309:### M4. Accessibility, complete on the line chart (ceiling: 10 days)\n316:- A 24-chart page: unique ids, no layout shift when one table opens, total tab stops recorded.\n320:Example: \"Exploring a chart with the keyboard\". Docs: Accessibility: what is automatic, what the author supplies, the rehearsal record and its limits.\n344:- Budgets P1–P5 and P7 measured with the Phase 3 method, recalibrated once 