{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_75","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -i 'dropped|rotated|rotates|remov.*label|unlabel|40.*40|numeric string' docs/01-landscape.md | head -35\ncat research/harness/node_modules/recharts/es6/theme/useBackwardsCompatibleTheme.js | head -60\nrg -n 'marker|12|axis' packages/axylo/src/scene/lines.ts | head -32\nrg -n 'ordered|label|three|axes' packages/axylo/src/layout/labels.ts | tail -15\nrg -n 'locale|UTC|zone' apps/docs/data/diagnostics.mjs | head -15\nrg -n 'css:' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/dist.report.md | head -6\nrg -n 'strong|frame|page' apps/docs/site/site.css | tail -17\nls -l apps/docs/dist/diagnostics | head -3\nrg --files packages/axylo/test | rg 'csp|ax1|i1|a11y' | head -28\"","aggregated_output":"9:1. **Nobody is told when the data is wrong.** Across five dirty-input fixtures, no library produced a message naming the field, the row or the problem. Nine of ten plotted the string `'40'` as a number without comment. A single `NaN` or `undefined` among valid numbers blanked the whole series in ApexCharts, broke Nivo, and broke either MUI X (`NaN`) or Highcharts (`undefined`, with an exception). Three libraries dropped category labels that did not fit, without saying so.\n153:| Long category label | all labels rotated | **label dropped** | overlaps | **5 of 7 dropped** | wrapped | rotated, cut at the container edge | rotated, cut at the container edge | overlaps | **label dropped** | overlaps |\n154:| Time tick labels at 720 px | rotated 45° | crowded | fit | overlap | fit | fit | fit | overlap | fit | fit |\n166:**R2. Labels that do not fit are the most common visible failure.** Only Highcharts wrapped \"Customer Success & Onboarding\". Recharts and MUI X removed that label and left a bar with no name; ECharts kept two labels of seven. Nivo, uPlot and the authored D3 chart overlapped text. ApexCharts and Plotly rotated the labels and they were cut at the container edge. Chart.js rotated all labels, and they stayed complete and readable.\n214:Every library handled `null` as a gap. Nine plotted a numeric string as a number, and Highcharts silently left it out; none said so either way. The only messages were one exception from minified code and browser SVG attribute errors. No library named the field, the row or the problem. Whether plotting `'40'` is wrong depends on the data (database drivers return large counts as strings); the finding is that the author is not told that a guess was made.\n257:| Tab stops in the chart | 0 | 8, of which 7 land on unlabeled groups | 0 | 0 | 2 | 10 | 9: eight toolbar buttons and a link | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n270:**R11. Half of the ten give a keyboard or assistive-technology user nothing to work with by default.** Chart.js and ECharts put one unlabeled canvas on the page. uPlot adds a title and a legend table that reads `--` until a mouse moves. Nivo is an unnamed image; the authored D3 chart is an image whose only content is its tick text. Chart.js' documentation leaves making the canvas accessible to the author (**Docs**, page dated 2021).\n297:| ECharts | `aria: { enabled: true }` (no extra import with the full build) | One unlabeled canvas | `role=\"img\"` with a generated label that lists the first ten items of each series, timestamps as raw numbers; still 0 tab stops |\n371:- The streaming producer waits for an animation frame before each update, so a slow library receives a slower feed, and \"later than 20 ms\" counts late callbacks, not dropped frames.\n389:| Fit every label, or say what is hidden | R2 | Highcharts (wraps); Chart.js (rotates legibly) |\nimport { resolvePartialDefaultProps } from '../util/resolveDefaultProps';\nimport { useRechartsTheme } from './RechartsThemeContext';\n\n/**\n * Backwards compatible hook for theming.\n *\n * - If the selector returns a theme slice, it is merged with `explicitProps` and acts as defaults.\n * - If no matching theme slice is present, the explicit props are returned without applying legacy defaults.\n *   This allows an empty theme to leave unspecified styles unstyled while preserving user props.\n *\n * `themeSelector` selects the subset of theme which this component is interested in.\n * The selector is not called in case the theme is undefined.\n *\n * @deprecated this hook only exists for backwards compatibility and we will remove it in 4.x. Use {@link useRechartsTheme} instead.\n * @experimental\n */\nexport var useBackwardsCompatibleTheme = (themeSelector, explicitProps, legacyDefaults) => {\n  var theme = useRechartsTheme();\n  if (theme == null) {\n    if (legacyDefaults == null) {\n      /*\n       * Theme is not set at all, and the parent component has no defaults.\n       * We have nothing else to do other than returning the props as-is.\n       */\n      return explicitProps;\n    }\n    /*\n     * Theme is not set at all - this means lack of any `RechartsThemeProvider` (or it was given null/undefined).\n     * This is the 2.x path - each component sets its own defaults. Let them!\n     */\n    return resolvePartialDefaultProps(explicitProps, legacyDefaults);\n  }\n  var slice = themeSelector(theme);\n  if (slice == null) {\n    /*\n     * In this case the theme exists but the particular slice does not.\n     * This could be that a chunk of the theme is not defined,\n     * or it could be the emptyTheme.\n     * Here we see the new theme so let's not mix it with old defaults, but preserve explicit props.\n     */\n    return explicitProps;\n  }\n  /*\n   * And finally a mix of the theme and explicit props.\n   * Old default styles don't apply here either.\n   */\n  return resolvePartialDefaultProps(explicitProps, slice);\n};21:function markerIndexes(\n36:      showAll = i === first || Math.abs(pixel - previousPixel) >= 12;\n63:  return { type: 'points', series, xy, shape: cue.marker, size: 6, fill: seriesPaint(series) };\n79:  const markers = markerIndexes(input, visible, singlePoint ? selection.indexes : selection.isolated,