{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_78","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba apps/docs/pages/migrate-chartjs.md && nl -ba apps/docs/pages/migrate-recharts.md && nl -ba apps/docs/pages/react.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Migrating from Chart.js\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can translate a Chart.js configuration into Axylo for the three everyday tasks below, map Chart.js concepts to Axylo's, and know what Axylo deliberately does differently or does not have.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe Chart.js code on this page is quoted, not retyped, from the configurations used in this project's comparison of ten charting libraries (`research/harness/src/libs/chartjs.js`). They were written against **Chart.js 4.6.1** with **chartjs-adapter-date-fns 3.0.0** and **date-fns 4.4.0**, and each is the least configuration that library needed for the task; comments mark what was required beyond the data. The Axylo versions run live on this page, on the same data.\n     6\t\n     7\t## A multi-series time line\n     8\t\n     9\tChart.js takes parallel arrays (`labels` and one `data` array per dataset), needs a date adapter before a time axis works, and needs `maintainAspectRatio: false` to fill a container of fixed height.\n    10\t\n    11\t[source: Chart.js, task \"trend\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/chartjs.js#L1-L16)\n    12\t\n    13\tIn Axylo the same table is rows and field names. The fixture's x values are epoch milliseconds of UTC midnights, which are calendar days: `type: 'date'` reads them that way, so each value shows on its own day in every time zone. Chart.js shows the same values as local instants, which west of UTC puts every point on the day before. The gap in \"Referral\" (seven `null`s) is a gap in both.\n    14\t\n    15\t[example: migrate-trend](../examples/migrate-trend.ts)\n    16\t\n    17\t## A bar chart with a target line\n    18\t\n    19\tChart.js has no reference line of its own. The usual ways are the separate `chartjs-plugin-annotation` package, or a second dataset of `type: 'line'` repeating the target. The comparison's configuration has neither, so only its bars are quoted here:\n    20\t\n    21\t[source: Chart.js, task \"bars\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/chartjs.js#L18-L25)\n    22\t\n    23\tIn Axylo the target is one option, `reference`, and it is labelled, included in the value axis, and listed in the chart's summary and table caption. Both libraries start these bars at zero; in Axylo a bar or area axis cannot be made to start elsewhere. The long label \"Customer Success & Onboarding\" is fitted by Axylo's remedies (wrapping, then rotating, then truncating with the full text in the tooltip and table), where Chart.js rotates all labels.\n    24\t\n    25\t[example: migrate-target](../examples/migrate-target.ts)\n    26\t\n    27\t## A live-updating series\n    28\t\n    29\tChart.js updates by mutating its arrays and calling `update()`. The comparison's stream task pushes one point and drops the oldest:\n    30\t\n    31\t[source: Chart.js, tasks \"scale\" and \"stream\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/chartjs.js#L45-L62)\n    32\t\n    33\tIn Axylo, `append` adds rows and `keep` bounds the window. A Pause control appears for the reader, the view follows the newest data while it is zoomed to the latest edge, and arriving data is not announced to screen readers.\n    34\t\n    35\t[example: migrate-stream](../examples/migrate-stream.ts)\n    36\t\n    37\tThe comparison also has a tuned Chart.js configuration for large data (`chartjs-tuned.js`: no parsing, the decimation plugin, no animation, no point markers). Axylo has no equivalent options: typed columns and the simplified drawing of dense lines are the defaults.\n    38\t\n    39\t## Concepts side by side\n    40\t\n    41\t| Chart.js | Axylo |\n    42\t|---|---|\n    43\t| `new Chart(canvas, { type: 'line', … })` | `line(target, options)`: one function per chart type; `target` is any element or a selector, not a canvas. |\n    44\t| `data.labels` and `datasets[].data` | `data` as rows, wide rows or typed columns, and field names: `x`, `y`, `series`. See [Your data](data.md). |\n    45\t| `datasets[].label` | The `series` field's values, or `label` on each `y` field. |\n    46\t| `options.plugins.title` | `title` (also the accessible name). |\n    47\t| `scales.x.type: 'time'` and a date adapter | Nothing for date strings; `type: 'time'` for epoch milliseconds, `type: 'date'` for calendar days. No adapter. |\n    48\t| `scales.y.title` | `label` and `unit` on the `y` field. |\n    49\t| `maintainAspectRatio`, `responsive`, canvas size | Nothing: the chart takes its container's size. |\n    50\t| `scales.y.beginAtZero` | Bars and areas always start at zero; lines fit the data. |\n    51\t| `spanGaps` | No equivalent: gaps are never bridged. `missing: 'zero'` for counts. |\n    52\t| `backgroundColor`, `borderColor` | The default palette, CSS custom properties, or `colors` by series name. See [Theming](theming.md). |\n    53\t| `plugins.legend.position` | `legend: 'top' \\| 'right' \\| 'bottom' \\| false`. |\n    54\t| `plugins.tooltip` (`mode`, `intersect`) | On by default, nearest x wherever the pointer is; `tooltip: false` turns it off. |\n    55\t| `chartjs-plugin-annotation` | `reference: [{ y, label }]` or `{ x: [from, to], label }`. |\n    56\t| Mutate `chart.data`, then `chart.update()` | `chart.update(patch)` for options and data, `chart.append(rows, { keep })` for new rows. |\n    57\t| `onClick`, `onHover` | `chart.on('select', …)`, `chart.on('hover', …)`. |\n    58\t| `animation` | No option: the first render is never animated. |\n    59\t| `parsing: false`, decimation | No options: typed columns and simplified drawing are the defaults. |\n    60\t| `chart.destroy()` | `chart.destroy()`. |\n    61\t\n    62\t## What is deliberately different\n    63\t\n    64\t- **Accessible without work.** A Chart.js chart is a canvas, and making it accessible is left to you. An Axylo chart has a name, a summary, keyboard access and a table view, with no option.\n    65\t- **Bad data is reported.** Chart.js plots `'40'` among numbers as 40 and says nothing. Axylo leaves it out, tells the reader, and prints a diagnostic with the field and row ([AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106)).\n    66\t- **Time zones are named.** Instants are shown in a zone that the tooltip heading and the summary name, and calendar dates are not shifted.\n    67\t- **Fewer chart types.** Line, area, bar, scatter, donut and the compact variant. No radar, polar area, bubble or mixed charts in 1.0.\n    68\t- **Less to configure.** No plugin API, no per-point styles, no custom tooltip renderer, no animation settings, no log scale or second y axis in 1.0.\n    69\t- **Your data is not watched.** Changing an array in place is not seen; call `update` or `append`. Typed columns are borrowed and must not be changed in place.\n     1\t# Migrating from Recharts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can translate a Recharts chart into an `@axylo/react` component for the three everyday tasks below, map Recharts concepts to Axylo's, and know what Axylo deliberately does differently or does not have.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe Recharts code on this page is quoted, not retyped, from the configurations used in this project's comparison of ten charting libraries (`research/harness/src/libs/recharts.jsx`). They were written against **Recharts 3.11.0** with **React 19.3.1**, and each is the least configuration that library needed for the task. Recharts has no default palette and no title component, so the comparison used the colours of Recharts' own examples. The `mount` helper renders the element into the harness's container. The Axylo versions run live on this page, on the same data.\n     6\t\n     7\t## A multi-series time line\n     8\t\n     9\tRecharts takes one row object per x with one key per series, one `<Line>` per series with its colour, and a tick formatter, because a numeric time axis prints raw epoch numbers without one.\n    10\t\n    11\t[source: Recharts, task \"trend\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/recharts.jsx#L1-L29)\n    12\t\n    13\tIn Axylo one component holds the whole chart. Long rows with a `series` field replace one `<Line>` per series; wide rows would work too, with `y` as a list. The palette, the title, the axis titles, the date formatting, the legend and the tooltip need no code. `type: 'date'` reads the fixture's UTC-midnight milliseconds as calendar days.\n    14\t\n    15\t[example: recharts-trend](../examples/recharts-trend.tsx)\n    16\t\n    17\t## A bar chart with a target line\n    18\t\n    19\tRecharts draws a target with its `ReferenceLine` component; the comparison's configuration did not use it, so only its bars are quoted here:\n    20\t\n    21\t[source: Recharts, task \"bars\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/recharts.jsx#L31-L46)\n    22\t\n    23\tIn Axylo the target is the `reference` prop, labelled, included in the value axis and listed in the summary and table caption. The long label is fitted instead of dropped (in the comparison, Recharts removed that label and left a bar with no name), and the chart is titled.\n    24\t\n    25\t[example: recharts-target](../examples/recharts-target.tsx)\n    26\t\n    27\t## A live-updating series\n    28\t\n    29\tThe comparison's Recharts stream re-renders with a new array for every point:\n    30\t\n    31\t[source: Recharts, tasks \"scale\" and \"stream\"](../../../research/harness/src/libs/recharts.jsx#L81-L93)\n    32\t\n    33\tWith `@axylo/react` the first rows are state, created once, and new points go through the handle with `append`, so earlier rows are not read again. The reader gets a Pause control.\n    34\t\n    35\t[example: recharts-stream](../examples/recharts-stream.tsx)\n    36\t\n    37\tRe-rendering with a new `data` array works too, and is the right thing when the whole data changes; it is the slower path for a stream.\n    38\t\n    39\t## Concepts side by side\n    40\t\n    41\t| Recharts | `@axylo/react` |\n    42\t|---|---|\n    43\t| `<LineChart data>` with `<XAxis>`, `<YAxis>`, `<CartesianGrid>`, `<Tooltip>`, `<Legend>` and one `<Line>` per series | One `<Line data x y series />`. Axes, grid, tooltip and legend are on by default. |\n    44\t| `<ResponsiveContainer>` | Nothing: the chart takes its container's size. |\n    45\t| `dataKey` per series | Long rows with `series`, or `y={['direct', 'organic']}` for wide rows. |\n    46\t| `stroke`, `fill`, `<Cell fill>` | The default palette, CSS custom properties, or `colors` by series name. See [Theming](theming.md). |\n    47\t| `<XAxis type=\"number\" scale=\"time\" tickFormatter>` | Nothing for date strings; `type: 'time'` or `type: 'date'` on `x` for numbers. Dates and numbers are formatted for the page's locale. |\n    48\t| `<YAxis label>` | `label` and `unit` on the `y` field. |\n    49\t| No title component | `title`, which is also the accessible name. |\n    50\t| `<ReferenceLine y>`, `<ReferenceArea>` | `reference={[{ y, label }]}`, or `{ x: [from, to], label }`. |\n    51\t| `<Bar>` with `stackId` | `layout=\"stacked\"` on `<Bar>`; `stacked` on `<Area>`. |\n    52\t| `layout=\"vertical\"` on `<BarChart>` | `horizontal` on `<Bar>`. |\n    53\t| `<Pie innerRadius>` | `<Donut label value />`. |\n    54\t| `isAnimationActive={false}` | Nothing: the first render is never animated. |\n    55\t| `dot={false}` | Nothing: markers appear only when points are sparse. |\n    56\t| `onClick`, `onMouseMove` on the chart | `onSelect`, `onHover`. |\n    57\t| Re-render with a new array to stream | `ref` and `chart.append(rows, { keep })`. |\n    58\t\n    59\t## What is deliberately different\n    60\t\n    61\t- **One component, one options object.** The same options work outside React with `line()`, so a chart can move between a React page and any other page unchanged.\n    62\t- **Defaults that are finished.** A palette that works in light and dark, a title, labels that fit, formatted numbers and dates, all without code.\n    63\t- **Accessible by default, differently.** Recharts' keyboard navigation (on by default in the version tested) uses `role=\"application\"`. Axylo's plot is one native range control with a text value, plus a table view; see [Accessibility](accessibility.md).\n    64\t- **Bad data is reported.** A string among numbers is left out and reported with its row ([AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106)), where Recharts plots `'40'` as 40.\n    65\t- **No composition API.** No custom shapes, custom tooltip content, label render props or `syncId` for linked charts in 1.0. A chart is configured through options, not assembled from components.\n    66\t- **Fewer chart types.** Line, area, bar, scatter, donut and the compact variant. No radar, radial bar, treemap, funnel or composed charts in 1.0.\n     1\t# React\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can use Axylo's React components, choose between props and the imperative handle, keep re-renders cheap, handle events and errors, and render on the server.\n     4\t\n     5\t`@axylo/react` works with React 18 and 19. It is a thin layer: each component creates an ordinary Axylo chart in an empty `<div>` and turns prop changes into `update` calls.\n     6\t\n     7\t```sh\n     8\tnpm install axylo @axylo/react\n     9\t```\n    10\t\n    11\t## The components\n    12\t\n    13\t`Line`, `Area`, `Bar`, `Scatter` and `Donut` take the same options as `line()`, `area()`, `bar()`, `scatter()` and `donut()`, as props. A few props belong to the component itself:\n    14\t\n    15\t| Prop | What it does |\n    16\t|---|---|\n    17\t| `id`, `className`, `style`, `data-*` | Attributes of the container `<div>`. The container always has the class `axylo`. |\n    18\t| `ref` | Receives `{ chart }`: the chart's handle, or `null` before mount, after unmount and on the server. |\n    19\t| `onHover`, `onSelect` | The `hover` and `select` events. |\n    20\t| `onZoom` | The `zoom` event. Not on `Donut`. |\n    21\t\n    22\t`aria-*` props are not passed to the container: the chart names itself through `title` or `labelledBy`. An `aria-label` prop is reported as an option the chart does not know ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)).\n    23\t\n    24\t[example: react-line](../examples/react-line.tsx)\n    25\t\n    26\t## Props and what they cost\n    27\t\n    28\tOn each render the component compares the new props with the ones it last applied, and calls `chart.update` with only what changed.\n    29\t\n    30\t- `data` is compared by identity. A new array, even with the same rows, is read again. Keep data in state, memoise it with `useMemo`, or build it outside the component. In development, the same content arriving as a new array three renders running prints a hint, `[axylo/react] … memoize it`, to the console.\n    31\t- Every other prop is compared by content, so an inline `x={{ field: 'day', type: 'date' }}` or `reference={[{ y: 90, label: 'Target' }]}` costs nothing when it has not changed. Do not change an option object in place: a value changed under the same reference is not seen.\n    32\t- A prop you remove is sent as `undefined`, which returns that option to its default.\n    33\t- Event handlers are replaced without recreating the chart, so inline arrow functions are fine. `onDiagnostic` too.\n    34\t\n    35\t## Props or the handle\n    36\t\n    37\tProps describe what the chart shows. The handle is for what props cannot express: `append` for live data, `zoom` from your own controls, and reading `diagnostics`.\n    38\t\n    39\t[example: react-stream](../examples/react-stream.tsx)\n    40\t\n    41\tWhen `data` is a prop, a new `data` replaces whatever was appended, so a streaming component keeps its first rows stable (in state, as above) and appends through the ref.\n    42\t\n    43\t## Lifecycle\n    44\t\n    45\tThe chart is created when the component mounts and destroyed when it unmounts. It is correct under StrictMode's setup, cleanup and setup again. A chart hidden by Suspense is not destroyed: when it is shown again it is the same chart, with its appended rows and zoom.\n    46\t\n    47\tThe component creates the chart during React's layout phase, so a parent's layout effect or callback ref already finds `ref.current.chart` with the committed options, and calls made there apply in the order React committed them.\n    48\t\n    49\t## Errors and error boundaries\n    50\t\n    51\tA chart does not throw for bad data: it draws what it can, shows the reader what it could not, and reports diagnostics. Only a `strict` chart throws, when a create or an update finds an error or a warning. In a component that happens during React's commit, so the nearest error boundary catches it like any render error, with the `AxyloStrictError` and its `diagnostics`.\n    52\t\n    53\tTo log diagnostics without throwing, pass `onDiagnostic`.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Server rendering\n    56\t\n    57\tThe component files are marked `'use client'`. On the server a component renders only its empty container, `<div class=\"axylo\"></div>` with your `id`, `className`, `style` and `data-*` attributes, and never reaches the chart code, which needs a browser. The chart is created after hydration. Give the container its size with CSS, so the page does not move when the chart appears: the chart fills the container's width, and with no height set its plot is 16:9 of the width, between 160 and 480 px.\n    58\t\n    59\tTo check data on the server, use `validate()` from `axylo`, which runs without a DOM. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_79","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba apps/docs/data/diagnostics.mjs | rg 'code:|why:|AX[0-9]+:' && rg -n 'function |const .*=>' apps/docs/lib/site.mjs apps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_79","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba apps/docs/data/diagnostics.mjs | rg 'code:|why:|AX[0-9]+:' && rg -n 'function |const .*=>' apps/docs/lib/site.mjs apps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs\"","aggregated_output":"    13\t    code: 'AX100', level: 'error', title: 'Columns of different lengths',\n    16\t    why: 'With typed columns, row *i* is element *i* of every column. When the lengths differ there is no way to tell which values belong together, so Axylo draws nothing rather than pair them wrongly.',\n    27\t    code: 'AX101', level: 'error', title: 'A field that is not in the data',\n    30\t    why: 'Usually a typo, or rows from an API whose field names changed. With typed rows in TypeScript the same mistake does not compile; with untyped rows this is how you find it.',\n    41\t    code: 'AX102', level: 'warning', title: 'A field absent from some rows',\n    44\t    why: 'An explicit `null` means \"no data\" and is drawn as a gap without comment. An absent key may mean the same thing, or a bug that dropped the field, so Axylo draws the gap and says so.',\n    55\t    code: 'AX103', level: 'warning', title: 'A value that is not a finite number',\n    58\t    why: '`NaN` and `Infinity` are almost always the result of a calculation (a division by zero, a parse that failed), not a measurement. Drawing them is impossible and treating them as zero would be wrong.',\n    69\t    code: 'AX104', level: 'hint', title: 'Numbers sent as strings',\n    72\t    why: 'Many database drivers return big integers and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). A whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous, so Axylo parses it, and asks you to say so. An empty string counts as missing.',\n    83\t    code: 'AX105', level: 'hint', title: 'Steps missing on a regular axis',\n    86\t    why: 'Daily data with a missing day usually means that day had no data, not that the line should run straight from the day before to the day after. Axylo finds the step from the data and does not bridge an absent one.',\n   100\t    code: 'AX106', level: 'warning', title: 'Strings among numbers',\n   103\t    why: 'A stray string in a column of numbers is a bug somewhere upstream. Strings such as `\\'1,234\\'` or `\\'n/a\\'` depend on a locale or a convention Axylo cannot know, so it does not guess.',\n   114\t    code: 'AX107', level: 'warning', title: 'A date or time that cannot be read',\n   117\t    why: 'The x field was recognised as dates (most values are `YYYY-MM-DD`), and this value is not a valid one: a day that does not exist, or another format.',\n   128\t    code: 'AX108', level: 'error', title: 'Two rows for the same x',\n   131\t    why: 'On a line, area or bar chart one x has one value per series. Two rows for the same x would need a rule (sum, last, average) that only you know.',\n   142\t    code: 'AX109', level: 'hint', title: 'Numbers that look like timestamps, or like seconds',\n   145\t    why: 'Axylo never assumes a bare number is an instant (F4), because a wrong guess moves every point. It does notice when numbers look like epoch milliseconds or seconds, and tells you.',\n   156\t    code: 'AX110', level: 'warning', title: 'A bar or area domain without zero',\n   159\t    why: 'The length of a bar or the height of an area is its value. A baseline other than zero makes 82 look like a fraction of 97 (F1).',\n   170\t    code: 'AX111', level: 'hint', title: 'Instants that look like calendar dates',\n   173\t    why: 'A daily metric is usually a calendar date. Sent as `2026-02-24T00:00:00Z` (or epoch milliseconds of a UTC midnight) it is an instant, which is a different thing: the same moment is a different day in different places.',\n   188\t    code: 'AX112', level: 'hint', title: 'Date-times without an offset',\n   191\t    why: '`2026-02-24T09:30` without `Z` or `+01:00` is a wall-clock time: it says what a clock showed, not when. Axylo shows it as written rather than guess a zone.',\n   202\t    code: 'AX120', level: 'error', title: 'A negative donut value',\n   205\t    why: 'A slice is a share of a whole. A negative share cannot be drawn as an arc, and dropping it would change every other slice\\'s percentage.',\n   216\t    code: 'AX121', level: 'warning', title: 'More than eight donut slices',\n   219\t    why: 'Beyond about eight slices, small arcs cannot be told apart or labelled, and the palette has eight colours.',\n   230\t    code: 'AX130', level: 'warning', title: 'More than eight series',\n   233\t    why: 'Eight colours is about as many as a reader can keep apart, also with a colour-vision deficiency (F9).',\n   246\t    code: 'AX131', level: 'hint', title: 'Several series in a compact chart',\n   249\t    why: 'A compact chart has no legend, axes or tooltip, so nothing tells the reader which line is which.',\n   261\t    code: 'AX132', level: 'hint', title: 'More bars than pixels',\n   264\t    why: 'Bars narrower than a pixel blur into an area and cannot be picked or compared.',\n   277\t    code: 'AX133', level: 'hint', title: 'A very large scatter chart',\n   280\t    why: 'Scatter points are not simplified in 1.0, so every point is drawn on every frame.',\n   291\t    code: 'AX134', level: 'error', title: 'Too many series × x positions',\n   294\t    why: 'Line, area and bar charts align every series on every x. Long rows with many series that share few x values (for example one series per user) multiply into more slots than a chart can hold.',\n   306\t    code: 'AX140', level: 'warning', title: 'A chart without a name',\n   309\t    why: 'A screen-reader user moving through a dashboard hears each chart\\'s name first. A generated name says what is plotted, not what it means.',\n   319\t    code: 'AX141', level: 'warning', title: 'Series colours too close to the background',\n   322\t    why: 'Marks need 3:1 against what is behind them to be seen (WCAG 1.4.11). Axylo measures the colours the browser actually resolved against the colour actually behind the chart, so a dark card on a light page is measured as dark.',\n   335\t    code: 'AX142', level: 'hint', title: 'Not enough room for a plot',\n   338\t    why: 'A plot that small cannot hold readable axes (L8). Drawing it anyway would produce overlapping or clipped labels.',\n   352\t    code: 'AX143', level: 'hint', title: 'Labels hidden after every remedy',\n   355\t    why: 'Axylo tries, in order: thinning (ordered axes only), wrapping to three lines, rotating up to 45°, and truncating in the middle. It never overlaps labels and never hides one without telling the reader (L1).',\n   368\t    code: 'AX150', level: 'warning', title: 'An option Axylo does not know',\n   371\t    why: 'A misspelled option would otherwise be ignored in silence. In TypeScript the same mistake does not compile.',\n   383\t    code: 'AX151', level: 'warning', title: 'Appended rows out of order',\n   386\t    why: '`append` is the fast path for rows that extend the series. A row before the last x needs the whole frame rebuilt, which is correct but slower, and usually means the feed is not ordered.',\n   399\t    code: 'AX152', level: 'warning', title: 'An unknown time zone',\n   402\t    why: 'Time zones come from the browser\\'s `Intl` data. A misspelled or retired zone name cannot be resolved.',\n   413\t    code: 'AX160', level: 'warning', title: 'A custom formatter threw',\n   416\t    why: 'A formatter is your code running inside the chart. One that throws for an unexpected value (a `null`, a negative) must not take the chart down.',\n   428\t    code: 'AX199', level: 'error', title: 'Axylo failed',\n   431\t    why: 'Something inside Axylo threw. The chart catches it so that the rest of the page keeps working.',\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:14:export function escapeHtml(text) {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:22:export function slug(text) {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:27:export function plainText(text) {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:50:export function markdownToHtml(source, { link = url => url, directive, headings = [] } = {}) {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:54:  const inline = text => renderInline(text, link);\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:58:  const tableName = () => {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:96:      const align = splitRow(lines[i + 1]).map(cell => cell.endsWith(':') ? (cell.startsWith(':') ? 'center' : 'right') : null);\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:145:function startsBlock(lines, i) {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:152:export function splitRow(line) {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:166:function table(header, rows, align, inline, name = 'Table') {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:167:  const style = index => align[index] ? ` style=\"text-align: ${align[index]}\"` : '';\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:168:  const head = header.map((cell, index) => `<th scope=\"col\"${style(index)}>${inline(cell)}</th>`).join('');\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:169:  const body = rows.map(row => `<tr>${header.map((_, index) => `<td${style(index)}>${inline(row[index] ?? '')}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`).join('');\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:174:export function codeBlock(code, language = '') {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:180:export function renderInline(text, link = url => url) {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:238:function matchBracket(text, open) {\napps/docs/lib/markdown.mjs:253:export function wordCount(source) {\napps/docs/lib/site.mjs:30:export function checkLibraryBuilt() {\napps/docs/lib/site.mjs:31:  const missing = REQUIRED.flatMap(([dir, files]) => files.map(f => path.join(dir, f)).filter(f => !fs.existsSync(f)));\napps/docs/lib/site.mjs:38:export async function importSource(relative) {\napps/docs/lib/site.mjs:50:export function readThemeDefaults(css = fs.readFileSync(path.join(libDist, 'axylo.css'), 'utf8')) {\napps/docs/lib/site.mjs:51:  const block = selector => {\napps/docs/lib/site.mjs:62:export async function buildSite({ outDir = path.join(docsRoot, 'dist'), log = () => {} } = {}) {\napps/docs/lib/site.mjs:98:func