{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in area bar scatter donut compact; do nl -ba apps/docs/pages/$f.md; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Area charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when to use an area chart, how stacking works, and what Axylo does with mixed signs and missing parts.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tAn area shows a quantity over an ordered x where the filled height is the point: a total and how its parts changed. Stacked, the top edge is the total and each band is one part. A single unstacked area is a line with its volume filled in.\n     8\t\n     9\tUse a [line chart](line.md) to compare trends that do not add up, or when the parts cross over and must be read one by one. Overlapping unstacked areas hide each other; stack them or use lines.\n    10\t\n    11\t[example: area-stacked](../examples/area-stacked.ts)\n    12\t\n    13\t## Options\n    14\t\n    15\t```ts\n    16\timport { area } from 'axylo';\n    17\tarea(target, { data, x, y, series, stacked: true, ...options });\n    18\t```\n    19\t\n    20\t| Option | On an area chart |\n    21\t|---|---|\n    22\t| `x`, `y`, `series` | As on a [line chart](line.md#options): long rows with `series`, or wide rows with a list of `y` fields. |\n    23\t| `stacked` | `true` stacks the series. Default: `false`. |\n    24\t| `zoom`, `reference`, `compact` | As on a line chart. |\n    25\t| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110): an area's height is its value. |\n    26\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    27\t\n    28\tNot on an area chart: `layout` and `horizontal` (bars only), `label` and `value` (donut only).\n    29\t\n    30\t## How an area chart behaves\n    31\t\n    32\t| Behaviour | Area |\n    33\t|---|---|\n    34\t| Pointer | The nearest x, as on a line. The tooltip lists every visible series at that x and, when stacked, the stack total. |\n    35\t| Keyboard | By x, then series, as on a line. |\n    36\t| Zoom | As on a line: date, time and number x. |\n    37\t| Baseline | The value axis always includes zero. |\n    38\t| Stacking | Positive values stack upwards from zero and negative values downwards, each on its own side, and the axis covers both totals. Adjacent bands are separated by at least 1 px of background, so they can be told apart without colour. |\n    39\t| Many points | Simplified as a line is, on the cumulative sums, so the drawn top edge keeps every column's extremes. Values come from the data. |\n    40\t| Limit | Eight series, then [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n    41\t| Reference lines and bands | As on a line. |\n    42\t\n    43\t## Edge case: a missing part of a stack\n    44\t\n    45\tWhen one series has no value at an x, its band there has zero height, so the bands above it do not fall into the gap. The stack at that x is marked \"incomplete\" in the tooltip, the table and announcements, because the total there is not the whole.\n    46\t\n    47\t[example: area-incomplete](../examples/area-incomplete.ts)\n     1\t# Bar charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when to use a bar chart, how to group, stack and turn bars horizontal, and what Axylo does with long labels, missing bars and negative values.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tBars compare categories: satisfaction by team, tickets by product area, orders per region. A bar's length is its value, so the value axis always starts at zero. Bars over a date axis compare counts per period (orders per day).\n     8\t\n     9\tUse a [line chart](line.md) to show a trend over many points, and a [donut](donut.md) only for the shares of one whole.\n    10\t\n    11\t[example: bar-target](../examples/bar-target.ts)\n    12\t\n    13\t## Options\n    14\t\n    15\t```ts\n    16\timport { bar } from 'axylo';\n    17\tbar(target, { data, x, y, series, layout: 'grouped', horizontal: false, ...options });\n    18\t```\n    19\t\n    20\t| Option | On a bar chart |\n    21\t|---|---|\n    22\t| `x` | The categories (or dates, numbers). Categories keep the order of the data. `ordered: true` says they have a natural order (months, age bands), which allows thinning their labels. |\n    23\t| `y`, `series` | One value field with `series`, or a list of fields. Several series make groups. |\n    24\t| `layout` | `'grouped'` (default) puts the series side by side; `'stacked'` stacks them. |\n    25\t| `horizontal` | `true` draws the bars from the left, with the categories down the side. Default: `false`. |\n    26\t| `zoom` | On date, time and number x only. A category axis is not zoomable. |\n    27\t| `reference`, `compact` | As on a line chart. |\n    28\t| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). |\n    29\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    30\t\n    31\tNot on a bar chart: `stacked` (that is `layout: 'stacked'` here), `label` and `value` (donut only).\n    32\t\n    33\t## How a bar chart behaves\n    34\t\n    35\t| Behaviour | Bar |\n    36\t|---|---|\n    37\t| Pointer | The band under the pointer: the whole category, not only the bar. The tooltip lists every series in that category. |\n    38\t| Keyboard | By category, then series. |\n    39\t| Zoom | Only on date, time and number x. |\n    40\t| Baseline | Always zero. An author `domain` without zero is widened ([AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110)). |\n    41\t| Missing values | A missing bar gets a small \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. |\n    42\t| Stacking | Positive parts stack up from zero, negative parts down from it; the axis covers both totals. A missing part has zero height and marks its stack \"incomplete\". |\n    43\t| Long labels | Remedies in order: thinning (date, number and `ordered` category axes only), wrapping to three lines, rotating up to 45°, truncating in the middle with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, the chart says \"Some labels hidden. See table.\" and [AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143) suggests `horizontal: true`. Axylo never switches orientation by itself: that would change what the chart says at a glance. |\n    44\t| Many bars | Not simplified. More bars than pixels along the axis are drawn, with hint [AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132). |\n    45\t| Limit | Eight series, then [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n    46\t| Reference lines | Drawn and labelled, included in the domain, listed in the summary and table caption. |\n    47\t\n    48\t## Horizontal and grouped bars\n    49\t\n    50\tLong category names read best down the side. With two value fields the bars are grouped, and a missing value is a \"no data\" mark, not an empty slot that looks like zero.\n    51\t\n    52\t[example: bar-horizontal](../examples/bar-horizontal.ts)\n    53\t\n    54\t## Edge case: stacks with both signs\n    55\t\n    56\t[example: bar-stacked](../examples/bar-stacked.ts)\n     1\t# Scatter charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when to use a scatter chart, which options it takes, and how its points are picked and read by keyboard.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tA scatter chart relates two measures: latency against payload size, rating against price. Each row is a point. An optional `series` groups the points by colour and marker.\n     8\t\n     9\tIts x is a number, an instant or a date, never a category. To compare categories, use a [bar chart](bar.md); to follow one measure over time, a [line](line.md).\n    10\t\n    11\t[example: scatter-basic](../examples/scatter-basic.ts)\n    12\t\n    13\t## Options\n    14\t\n    15\t```ts\n    16\timport { scatter } from 'axylo';\n    17\tscatter(target, { data, x, y, series, ...options });\n    18\t```\n    19\t\n    20\t| Option | On a scatter chart |\n    21\t|---|---|\n    22\t| `x` | A number, time or date field. `type` takes `'number'`, `'time'` or `'date'`; `ordered` does not apply. |\n    23\t| `y` | One value field. A list of fields is not accepted ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150) at run time). |\n    24\t| `series` | Optional. The field that names each point's group. |\n    25\t| `zoom` | Along x only in 1.0. |\n    26\t| `reference` | Lines and bands, as on a line chart. |\n    27\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    28\t\n    29\tNot on a scatter chart, and a type error if given: `compact` (a sparkline of points says nothing a tile can use), `stacked`, `layout`, `horizontal`, `label` and `value`.\n    30\t\n    31\t## How a scatter chart behaves\n    32\t\n    33\t| Behaviour | Scatter |\n    34\t|---|---|\n    35\t| Pointer | The nearest point within 24 px; when two are equally near, the later row wins. The tooltip shows the point's series, x and y, and lists coincident points, up to eight. |\n    36\t| Keyboard | By x, then y, through every visible point. |\n    37\t| Zoom | x only in 1.0; both axes later. |\n    38\t| Many points | Not simplified in 1.0. Above 50,000 points every point is still drawn, with hint [AX133](diagnostics.md#ax133). |\n    39\t| Duplicates | Points that share an x, or both coordinates, are ordinary data. |\n    40\t| Limit | Eight series, then [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n    41\t| Reference lines and bands | Drawn and included in the domain. |\n    42\t\n    43\t## Edge case: points on top of each other\n    44\t\n    45\tWhole-number ratings put many reviews on the same spot. They are all kept: the tooltip lists the coincident points, and the keyboard visits each one in turn.\n    46\t\n    47\t[example: scatter-ties](../examples/scatter-ties.ts)\n     1\t# Donut charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you know when a donut is the right choice, which options it takes, and what it does with zero, missing, negative and too many slices.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\tA donut shows the shares of one whole: sessions by browser, budget by item. It works for a handful of parts; with more than eight, Axylo groups the smallest. To compare amounts that are not parts of one whole, or many categories, use a [bar chart](bar.md).\n     8\t\n     9\t[example: donut-basic](../examples/donut-basic.ts)\n    10\t\n    11\t## Options\n    12\t\n    13\t```ts\n    14\timport { donut } from 'axylo';\n    15\tdonut(target, { data, label, value, ...options });\n    16\t```\n    17\t\n    18\t| Option | On a donut |\n    19\t|---|---|\n    20\t| `label` | The field that names each slice. Labels must be unique. |\n    21\t| `value` | The value field: a field name or a [value field object](options.md#value-fields) (`label`, `unit`, `scale`, `format`). Values must not be negative. |\n    22\t| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n    23\t\n    24\tNot on a donut, and a type error if given: `x`, `y` and `series` (a donut has a label and a value), `zoom` and `reference` (there is no axis), `compact`, `stacked`, `layout`, `horizontal`.\n    25\t\n    26\t## How a donut behaves\n    27\t\n    28\t| Behaviour | Donut |\n    29\t|---|---|\n    30\t| Order | Slices keep the order of the data, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. |\n    31\t| Legend | Every slice, in slice order, with its value and percentage as text. |\n    32\t| Pointer | The slice under the pointer. The tooltip shows its label, value and percentage. |\n    33\t| Keyboard | Through the slices in slice order. |\n    34\t| Zero and missing | A zero slice is listed in the legend without an arc. A slice with no value is listed as \"No data\". |\n    35\t| Negative | A negative value is error [AX120](diagnostics.md#ax120): the donut shows its error state, since a negative share cannot be drawn. |\n    36\t| More than eight | The seven largest slices are drawn and the rest grouped as \"Other (n)\", with [AX121](diagnostics.md#ax121). The table still lists every category. |\n    37\t| All zero | The empty state: there is no whole to divide. |\n    38\t| Duplicates | Two slices with the same label are [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): the donut is not drawn. |\n    39\t| Separators | Adjacent slices are separated by at least 1 px of background. |\n    40\t\n    41\t## Edge case: many slices, a zero and a gap\n    42\t\n    43\t[example: donut-many](../examples/donut-many.ts)\n     1\t# Compact charts\n     2\t\n     3\tAfter this page you can put a sparkline in a KPI tile with `compact: true`, know what it shows and leaves out, and give it a text alternative that a screen-reader user can rely on.\n     4\t\n     5\t## When to use it\n     6\t\n     7\t`compact: true` on a line, area or bar chart draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and its label, and the chart only says which way it is going.\n     8\t\n     9\t[example: compact-tiles](../examples/compact-tiles.html)\n    10\t\n    11\tA compact chart does not give access to every value; its summary is the alternative. When the reader needs every value, use the full chart.\n    12\t\n    13\t## What a compact chart is\n    14\t\n    15\t| Behaviour | Compact |\n    16\t|---|---|\n    17\t| For assistive technology | A static image (`role=\"img\"`) with an accessible name and the generated summary as its description: series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, and any values left out. |\n    18\t| Name | `labelledBy` with the tile heading's id (best: the heading is already there), or `title`. In a compact chart the title is not shown; it is the accessible name. Without either, the name is generated and [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140) is raised. |\n    19\t| Keyboard and pointer | No tab stop and no pointer behaviour, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. A page of forty tiles adds no tab stops. |\n    20\t| Size | Fills its container from 24 × 16 px up. With no height set, it is 32 px tall. |\n    21\t| Several series | Allowed, with lines told apart by dash as well as colour, since there is no legend; hint [AX131](diagnostics.md#ax131) suggests one series per tile. |\n    22\t| Fidelity | The rules of the full charts still hold: zero baseline for bar and area, gaps for missing values, diagnostics, simplified drawing of dense lines, sorting by x, no motion, and light or dark from the page. |\n    23\t| Reference lines | Not drawn. |\n    24\t\n    25\t## Options\n    26\t\n    27\t`compact` takes the options of its chart type ([line](line.md#options), [area](area.md#options), [bar](bar.md#options)). The ones that need axes or controls (`zoom`, `reference`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`) have no effect on a compact chart. Scatter and donut have no compact form: on them `compact` is a type error.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Edge case: a value left out\n    30\t\n    31\tWhen values were left out, the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner of the chart.\n    32\t\n    33\t[example: compact-dirty](../examples/compact-dirty.html)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
