{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_91","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'repeat|0.14|0.10|0.06|decision|Status' docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md; rg -n 'significant|compact|1e6' packages/axylo/src/format/number.ts; rg -n 'series|bright|total|incomplete|domain|No data' apps/docs/pages/area.md apps/docs/pages/bar.md; ls packages/axylo/src/diagnostics; ls packages/axylo/src/data\"","aggregated_output":"3:Status: **proposed, refitted after review.** The first version (commit 51e1be5) was reviewed by Codex gpt-6.2-astra (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/palette.review.md`). The review confirmed the arithmetic and showed four things wrong: the uneven lightness was claimed to be forced and is not; simulated screenshots and 8-bit fills fell below the limits the record promised; the order explorer could prune a feasible order after a timeout; and the record overclaimed in several places. This version is a refit with byte-level checks and corrected text. The hard constraints are measured and tested. Whether the result is good to look at is the owner's call (see \"For the owner\"). The refit itself has not been reviewed.\n15:| 3 | green | `#136102` | 0.43 0.140 141 | 7.67 | `#b2e390` | 0.86 0.121 133 | 12.08 | 8° |\n34:| Byte-rounded simulation, worst pair | 0.098 (light protan 6–8) | 0.102 |\n35:| 25% fills on an 8-bit raster, worst neighbours | 0.0590 (review's raster) | 0.063 (Chrome, canvas and SVG) |\n51:The same charts are repeated under the three simulations (the Machado matrices as SVG color matrices in linear RGB) and in grayscale. Source: `research/palette/preview.html`.\n61:The unit test requires the analytic and the byte forms. It checks the recorded Chrome pixels against the 0.06 limit, and checks that each recorded pixel is within one byte of the model. A stricter margin is the worst case over every byte color within one byte per channel of both fills. It is recorded but not required, because I found no palette that meets it together with the rest (0.054–0.056 is the best I saw; see below).\n70:| 2 | min pairwise ΔE, all 8, normal / protan / deutan / tritan | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.104 / 0.103 / 0.102 |\n71:| 2, bytes | the same after the simulation is rounded to bytes | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 |\n72:| 3 | first 4, worst vision (analytic; bytes) | ≥ 0.14 | 0.156 protan; 0.155 | 0.158 tritan; 0.159 |\n73:| 3 | first 3, 5, 6, worst vision (analytic) | ≥ 0.10 | 0.156 / 0.102 / 0.102 | 0.158 / 0.103 / 0.103 |\n75:| 5 | nearest series color to focus / reference / muted | ≥ 0.10 | 0.101 (cyan) / 0.152 (green) / 0.110 (cyan) | 0.138 (cyan) / 0.104 (cyan) / 0.113 (slate) |\n76:| 6 | neighbour fills at 25%: ideal / byte models / Chrome canvas / Chrome SVG | ≥ 0.06 | 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 / 0.063 | 0.064 / 0.064 / 0.063 / 0.063 |\n77:| 6 | neighbour fills at 80%: ideal / byte models / Chrome | ≥ 0.06 | 0.201 / 0.202 / 0.202 | 0.179 / 0.179 / 0.179 |\n78:| 6, recorded only | 25% fills, worst case within one byte | (0.06) | 0.056 | 0.056 |\n96:| first 5 | normal / protan / deutan / tritan | 0.135 / 0.103 / 0.136 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.126 / 0.109 / 0.103 |\n97:| first 6 | normal / protan / deutan / tritan | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.117 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.124 / 0.109 / 0.103 |\n107:  - all-eight pairwise 0.102–0.103 under the three deficiencies, analytic and in bytes;\n108:  - first five and six 0.102–0.103;\n109:  - focus distance 0.101 (cyan against `#1f5fcc`).\n111:  - all-eight pairwise 0.102–0.104;\n112:  - first five and six 0.102–0.103;\n113:  - reference distance 0.104 (cyan against `#bcc0c9`).\n118:The solver kept margins above the tested limits: pairwise 0.102, fills 0.063, contrast 3.02, hue shift 15° for chromatic slots (`configs/fit-final.json`, `solverLimits`). In the final boxes it proved 0.065/0.103 and 0.066/0.102 infeasible, so the margins cannot be much larger near this palette.\n181:- **Byte-level checks** (tested): byte-rounded simulation; fills on both 8-bit raster models; Chrome's drawn pixels within one byte of the model and 0.06 apart.\n183:- **Solver margins** as listed above; the tests use the decision's limits.\n198:| schemeTableau10 | 5 of 10 | 0.129 / 0.016 / 0.007 (3–5, red–green) / 0.064 |\n202:| schemeSet1 | 5 of 9 | 0.130 / 0.031 / 0.035 / 0.066 |\n3: * readouts keep the full integer part and the fraction to six significant digits; ticks are as short as\n81: * Fraction digits a readout shows for a shown value: enough for six significant digits of the fraction,\n107:    // Below about 1e-94 fixed notation cannot reach the significant digits.\n202: * tick of a set, so they line up); compact notation when the largest tick is at least a million; scientific\n204: * every notation each tick gets enough significant digits for the step, so neighbours never read the same.\n223:  const significant = Math.min(Math.max(valueExp - stepExp + 1, 1), 21);\n224:  const notation: Intl.NumberFormatOptions['notation'] = maxExp >= 15 || fractionDigits > MAX_TICK_FRACTION_DIGITS ? 'scientific' : 'compact';\n225:  return formatExact(numberFormat(locale, { ...style, notation, maximumSignificantDigits: significant }), exact);\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:17:bar(target, { data, x, y, series, layout: 'grouped', horizontal: false, ...options });\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:23:| `y`, `series` | One value field with `series`, or a list of fields. Several series make groups. |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:24:| `layout` | `'grouped'` (default) puts the series side by side; `'stacked'` stacks them. |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:28:| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:37:| Pointer | The band under the pointer: the whole category, not only the bar. The tooltip lists every series in that category. |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:38:| Keyboard | By category, then series. |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:40:| Baseline | Always zero. An author `domain` without zero is widened ([AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110)). |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:42:| Stacking | Positive parts stack up from zero, negative parts down from it; the axis covers both totals. A missing part has zero height and marks its stack \"incomplete\". |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:45:| Limit | Eight series, then [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\napps/docs/pages/bar.md:46:| Reference lines | Drawn and labelled, included in the domain, listed in the summary and table caption. |\napps/docs/pages/area.md:7:An area shows a quantity over an ordered x where the filled height is the point: a total and how its parts changed. Stacked, the top edge is the total and each band is one part. A single unstacked area is a line with its volume filled in.\napps/docs/pages/area.md:17:area(target, { data, x, y, series, stacked: true, ...options });\napps/docs/pages/area.md:22:| `x`, `y`, `series` | As on a [line chart](line.md#options): long rows with `series`, or wide rows with a list of `y` fields. |\napps/docs/pages/area.md:23:| `stacked` | `true` stacks the series. Default: `false`. |\napps/docs/pages/area.md:25:| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110): an area's height is its value. |\napps/docs/pages/area.md:34:| Pointer | The nearest x, as on a line. The tooltip lists every visible series at that x and, when stacked, the stack total. |\napps/docs/pages/area.md:35:| Keyboard | By x, then series, as on a line. |\napps/docs/pages/area.md:38:| Stacking | Positive values stack upwards from zero and negative values downwards, each on its own side, and the axis covers both totals. Adjacent bands are separated by at least 1 px of background, so they can be told apart without colour. |\napps/docs/pages/area.md:40:| Limit | Eight series, then [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\napps/docs/pages/area.md:45:When one series has no value at an x, its band there has zero height, so the bands above it do not fall into the gap. The stack at that x is marked \"incomplete\" in the tooltip, the table and announcements, because the total there is not the whole.\napps/docs/pages/area.md:47:[example: area-incomplete](../examples/area-incomplete.ts)\ncollector.ts\nindex.ts\nregistry.ts\nindex.ts\nnormalize.ts\noptions.ts\nreports.ts\nsteps.ts\nstore-buffers.ts\nstore-calendar.ts\nstore-diagnostics.ts\nstore-map.ts\nstore-rows.ts\nstore-unaligned.ts\nstore-window.ts\nstore.ts\ntable.ts\ntransaction.ts\nvalidate.ts\nvalues.ts\nx.ts\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_92","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat apps/docs/examples/node/tsconfig.json apps/docs/examples/tsconfig.html.json; nl -ba apps/docs/test/examples.browser.test.ts | sed -n '20,150p'; cat apps/docs/examples/compact-tiles.html; cat apps/docs/examples/update.html; rg -n 'memoiz|churn' packages | rg -v 'dist/' | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"extends\": \"../../../../tsconfig.base.json\",\n  \"compilerOptions\": { \"noEmit\": true, \"types\": [\"node\"], \"lib\": [\"ES2022\"] },\n  \"include\": [\"*.ts\"]\n}\n{\n  \"extends\": \"./tsconfig.json\",\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    // The scripts of the .html examples are plain JavaScript, written as a page would write them, so\n    // they are checked with checkJs. Everything is strict except null checks: `querySelector` on an\n    // element the example's own markup contains is not written with a guard.\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"checkJs\": true,\n    \"strictNullChecks\": false\n  },\n  \"include\": [\"../.cache/html-scripts/*.js\", \"../.cache/html-scripts/*.d.ts\"]\n}\n    20\timport path from 'node:path';\n    21\timport { chromium, type Browser } from 'playwright-core';\n    22\timport { buildSite } from '../lib/site.mjs';\n    23\timport { docsRoot, listExamples, repoRoot, writeTypecheckInputs } from '../lib/examples.mjs';\n    24\timport { serve } from '../lib/serve.mjs';\n    25\t\n    26\tinterface Example { name: string; file: string; ext: string; raises: string[]; noMarks: boolean }\n    27\t\n    28\t// Through node itself, so the same call works on Windows, where node_modules/.bin/tsc is a shell script.\n    29\tconst tsc = path.join(repoRoot, 'node_modules/typescript/bin/tsc');\n    30\t// Read from the sources at collection time, so each example is its own test.\n    31\tconst examples: Example[] = listExamples();\n    32\tlet server: Awaited<ReturnType<typeof serve>>;\n    33\tlet browser: Browser;\n    34\t\n    35\tfunction typecheck(config: string): string {\n    36\t  try {\n    37\t    execFileSync(process.execPath, [tsc, '-p', path.join(docsRoot, 'examples', config)], { cwd: repoRoot, encoding: 'utf8', stdio: 'pipe' });\n    38\t    return '';\n    39\t  } catch (error) {\n    40\t    const e = error as { stdout?: string; stderr?: string };\n    41\t    return `${e.stdout ?? ''}${e.stderr ?? ''}` || String(error);\n    42\t  }\n    43\t}\n    44\t\n    45\tbeforeAll(async () => {\n    46\t  writeTypecheckInputs();\n    47\t  await buildSite();\n    48\t  server = await serve();\n    49\t  browser = await chromium.launch({ channel: 'chrome', headless: true });\n    50\t}, 120_000);\n    51\t\n    52\tafterAll(async () => {\n    53\t  await browser?.close();\n    54\t  await server?.close();\n    55\t});\n    56\t\n    57\tdescribe('examples type-check against the package declarations', () => {\n    58\t  it('TypeScript and React examples (strict)', () => {\n    59\t    expect(typecheck('tsconfig.json')).toBe('');\n    60\t  });\n    61\t  it('scripts of the HTML examples (checkJs)', () => {\n    62\t    expect(typecheck('tsconfig.html.json')).toBe('');\n    63\t  });\n    64\t});\n    65\t\n    66\tdescribe('examples render in Chrome from the built library', () => {\n    67\t  it('there are examples', () => {\n    68\t    expect(examples.length).toBeGreaterThan(20);\n    69\t  });\n    70\t\n    71\t  it.each(examples.map(example => [example.file, example] as const))('%s', async (_file, example) => {\n    72\t    const context = await browser.newContext({\n    73\t      viewport: { width: 760, height: 900 }, locale: 'en-US', timezoneId: 'America/Los_Angeles',\n    74\t    });\n    75\t    const page = await context.newPage();\n    76\t    const errors: string[] = [];\n    77\t    const codes = new Set<string>();\n    78\t    page.on('pageerror', error => errors.push(`page error: ${error.message}`));\n    79\t    page.on('console', message => {\n    80\t      const text = message.text();\n    81\t      if (message.type() === 'error') errors.push(`console.error: ${text}`);\n    82\t      for (const m of text.matchAll(/^\\[axylo (AX\\d{3})\\]/g)) codes.add(m[1]!);\n    83\t      if (message.type() === 'warning' && !text.startsWith('[axylo ')) errors.push(`console.warn: ${text}`);\n    84\t    });\n    85\t    page.on('requestfailed', request => errors.push(`request failed: ${request.url()}`));\n    86\t    page.on('response', response => { if (response.status() >= 400) errors.push(`HTTP ${response.status()}: ${response.url()}`); });\n    87\t    await page.goto(server.url(`apps/docs/dist/examples/${example.name}.html`));\n    88\t\n    89\t    // Ready when every chart has drawn marks or shows a state message, and nothing changes for 3 frames.\n    90\t    await page.waitForFunction(() => {\n    91\t      const figures = [...document.querySelectorAll('figure.axylo-figure')];\n    92\t      return figures.length > 0 && figures.every(f =>\n    93\t        f.querySelector('svg [style*=\"--axylo-series-color\"], canvas') !== null ||\n    94\t        [...f.querySelectorAll('.axylo-message')].some(m => !(m as HTMLElement).hidden && m.textContent));\n    95\t    }, null, { timeout: 15_000 });\n    96\t    await page.evaluate(() => new Promise(resolve => {\n    97\t      let n = 0;\n    98\t      const tick = () => (++n < 3 ? requestAnimationFrame(tick) : resolve(null));\n    99\t      requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n   100\t    }));\n   101\t\n   102\t    const charts = await page.evaluate(() => [...document.querySelectorAll('figure.axylo-figure')].map(f => {\n   103\t      const labelled = f.getAttribute('aria-labelledby');\n   104\t      const name = (f.getAttribute('aria-label') ?? '') ||\n   105\t        (labelled ?? '').split(/\\s+/).map(id => document.getElementById(id)?.textContent ?? '').join(' ').trim();\n   106\t      const marks = [...f.querySelectorAll('svg [style*=\"--axylo-series-color\"]')].filter(el => {\n   107\t        const box = (el as SVGGraphicsElement).getBBox();\n   108\t        return box.width > 0 || box.height > 0;\n   109\t      }).length;\n   110\t      const message = [...f.querySelectorAll('.axylo-message')].find(m => !(m as HTMLElement).hidden)?.textContent ?? '';\n   111\t      return { role: f.getAttribute('role'), name, marks, message };\n   112\t    }));\n   113\t\n   114\t    expect(charts.length, `${example.file}: charts`).toBeGreaterThan(0);\n   115\t    for (const chart of charts) {\n   116\t      expect(['group', 'img'], `${example.file}: role`).toContain(chart.role);\n   117\t      expect(chart.name, `${example.file}: accessible name`).not.toBe('');\n   118\t      if (example.noMarks) expect(chart.message, `${example.file}: a state message`).not.toBe('');\n   119\t      else expect(chart.marks, `${example.file}: \"${chart.name}\" draws marks (message: \"${chart.message}\")`).toBeGreaterThan(0);\n   120\t    }\n   121\t    expect(errors, `${example.file}: errors`).toEqual([]);\n   122\t    expect([...codes].sort(), `${example.file}: diagnostics printed`).toEqual([...example.raises].sort());\n   123\t    await context.close();\n   124\t  }, 60_000);\n   125\t});\n<style>\n  .tiles { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(170px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; }\n  .tile { padding: 12px; border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 25%, transparent); border-radius: 8px; }\n  .tile h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 500; }\n  .tile p { margin: 2px 0 8px; font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 600; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"tiles\">\n  <section class=\"tile\">\n    <h3 id=\"sessions-heading\">Sessions, last 30 days</h3>\n    <p>48,210</p>\n    <div id=\"sessions-spark\"></div>\n  </section>\n  <section class=\"tile\">\n    <h3 id=\"revenue-heading\">Revenue, last 30 days</h3>\n    <p>$182,400</p>\n    <div id=\"revenue-spark\"></div>\n  </section>\n  <section class=\"tile\">\n    <h3 id=\"errors-heading\">Errors per day</h3>\n    <p>37</p>\n    <div id=\"errors-spark\"></div>\n  </section>\n</div>\n\n<script type=\"module\">\n  import { line, area, bar } from 'axylo';\n\n  const days = Array.from({ length: 30 }, (_, i) => `2026-03-${String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}`);\n  const rows = days.map((day, i) => ({\n    day,\n    sessions: Math.round(1500 + 12 * i + 180 * Math.sin(i / 2)),\n    revenue: Math.round(5600 + 40 * i + 700 * Math.cos(i / 3)),\n    errors: Math.max(0, Math.round(30 + 10 * Math.sin(i * 1.3))),\n  }));\n\n  // Marks only. Each tile's heading names its chart; the chart describes itself to assistive\n  // technology with its generated summary (range, minimum, maximum, last value).\n  line('#sessions-spark', { labelledBy: 'sessions-heading', compact: true, data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'sessions' });\n  area('#revenue-spark', { labelledBy: 'revenue-heading', compact: true, data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'revenue' });\n  bar('#errors-spark', { labelledBy: 'errors-heading', compact: true, data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'errors' });\n</script>\n<div id=\"chart\"></div>\n<p>\n  <button type=\"button\" id=\"metric\">Show revenue</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" id=\"subtitle\">Add a subtitle</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" id=\"next\">Next week's data</button>\n</p>\n\n<script type=\"module\">\n  import { line } from 'axylo';\n\n  /** @param {number} start the first day of the week in March */\n  const week = start => Array.from({ length: 7 }, (_, i) => ({\n    day: `2026-03-${String(start + i).padStart(2, '0')}`,\n    orders: Math.round(180 + 25 * Math.sin(start + i)),\n    revenue: Math.round(9000 + 1400 * Math.cos(start + i)),\n  }));\n\n  const chart = line('#chart', { title: 'Orders', data: week(2), x: 'day', y: 'orders' });\n\n  const metric = document.querySelector('#metric');\n  let revenue = false;\n  metric.addEventListener('click', () => {\n    revenue = !revenue;\n    // A present key replaces its old value whole: `y` is a new field spec, `title` a new title.\n    chart.update(revenue\n      ? { title: 'Revenue', y: { field: 'revenue', label: 'Revenue', unit: 'USD' } }\n      : { title: 'Orders', y: 'orders' });\n    metric.textContent = revenue ? 'Show orders' : 'Show revenue';\n  });\n\n  const toggle = document.querySelector('#subtitle');\n  let subtitle = false;\n  toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {\n    subtitle = !subtitle;\n    // `undefined` returns the option to its default (no subtitle). Absent keys stay as they are.\n    chart.update({ subtitle: subtitle ? 'Week of 2 March' : undefined });\n    toggle.textContent = subtitle ? 'Remove the subtitle' : 'Add a subtitle';\n  });\n\n  let start = 2;\n  document.querySelector('#next').addEventListener('click', () => {\n    start = start === 2 ? 9 : 2;\n    chart.update({ data: week(start), subtitle: subtitle ? `Week of ${start} March` : undefined });\n  });\n</script>\npackages/react/src/index.ts:14:/** Replaced at build time: the development file keeps the memoization hint, the production file drops it. */\npackages/react/src/index.ts:117:  console.warn(`[axylo/react] <${name}> was given \\`data\\` as a new object three renders running, each time with the same length and the same first and last rows (only those are compared). A new object is normalized again; if the content has not changed, memoize it (useMemo, or keep it in state).`);\npackages/react/build.mjs:4://   index.dev.js  development: with the memoization hint\npackages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:391:  it('the memoization hint: once per chart, on the third endpoint-equal replacement, worded as a sample', async () => {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_93","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md | head -55; cat apps/docs/examples/bar-stacked.ts apps/docs/examples/units.ts; sed -n '315,443p' apps/docs/data/diagnostics.mjs\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_93","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat apps/docs/pages/diagnostics.md | head -55; cat apps/docs/examples/bar-stacked.ts apps/docs/examples/units.ts; sed -n '315,443p' apps/docs/data/diagnostics.mjs\"","aggregated_output":"# Diagnostics\n\nAfter this page you can read an Axylo diagnostic, collect diagnostics in your own code, make a chart throw on them, and check a chart's data in Node or in a test before anything is drawn. Every code has its own page, listed at the end.\n\n## Reading one\n\nWhen Axylo cannot draw something as given, it prints one line to the console:\n\n```text\n[axylo AX103] \"Conversion\": Field \"rate\" has 1 non-finite value at row 1 (NaN). Check the calculation that produced it or send null for no data. Shown as gaps.\n```\n\n| Part | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Code | `AX103`. Stable across releases; each has a page. |\n| Chart | `\"Conversion\"`: the chart's title, or its element id when it has none. |\n| What and where | The field, how many values, and the first rows (up to ten), numbered in your own data as you passed it. |\n| Fix | What to change. |\n| Outcome | What the chart did instead: here, gaps. |\n\n## Levels and who is told\n\n| Level | Means | Console | The reader |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Error | The chart, or a series, cannot be drawn. | Every build, once per code per page | The chart shows its error state, or says which series could not be drawn |\n| Warning | Something is not drawn as given. | Every build, once per code per page | When values were left out or changed, a note in the figure: \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" |\n| Hint | Advice for you; the chart is drawn as given. | Development builds only | Usually nothing. Two hints go with a visible change: a plot too small to draw shows its summary instead ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), and hidden labels get a note ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)) |\n\nErrors and warnings print with `console.warn`, hints with `console.info`. The note to the reader is in every build, inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, so a production chart is never silently wrong.\n\n## Collecting them in your code\n\nEach diagnostic is an object:\n\n```ts\ninterface Diagnostic {\n  code: `AX${number}`;           // 'AX103'\n  level: 'error' | 'warning' | 'hint';\n  message: string;               // what is wrong, where, and how to fix it\n  chart: string;                 // the chart's title, else its element id\n  field?: string;                // when it is about a field\n  rows?: readonly number[];      // first rows, in your data, at most 10\n  count?: number;                // how many values or rows in all\n}\n```\n\n`chart.diagnostics` lists them for the current options and data, up to date as soon as a create, `update` or `append` returns. `onDiagnostic` is called once for each diagnostic the chart newly finds, in every build and at every level, so it is the place to send them to your error tracking. A listener that throws is reported once and otherwise ignored; it cannot break the chart.\n\n[example: diagnostics-listener](../examples/diagnostics-listener.html)\n\n## When they are found\n\nMost diagnostics are found while a call checks its input: the create, `update` or `append` call. Those are in `chart.diagnostics` when the call returns. A few can only be found later, while drawing: low contrast after the page's CSS changes ([AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141)), a plot too small ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), labels that stop fitting after a resize ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)), more bars than pixels ([AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132)), a formatter that throws ([AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160)), and a label element that does not exist. They are reported when found, through the same console line and `onDiagnostic`.\n\n## Strict mode\nimport { bar } from 'axylo';\n\n// Stacked bars with both signs: positive parts stack up from zero, negative parts down from it,\n// and the value axis covers both totals.\nconst rows = [\n  { quarter: '2025 Q1', new: 120, expansion: 45, churn: -60 },\n  { quarter: '2025 Q2', new: 135, expansion: 52, churn: -48 },\n  { quarter: '2025 Q3', new: 110, expansion: 61, churn: -75 },\n  { quarter: '2025 Q4', new: 150, expansion: 58, churn: -52 },\n];\n\nbar('#chart', {\n  title: 'Net revenue change by quarter',\n  data: rows,\n  x: { field: 'quarter', ordered: true },\n  y: [\n    { field: 'new', label: 'New', unit: 'k USD' },\n    { field: 'expansion', label: 'Expansion', unit: 'k USD' },\n    { field: 'churn', label: 'Churn', unit: 'k USD' },\n  ],\n  layout: 'stacked',\n});\nimport { line } from 'axylo';\n\nconst rows = [\n  { month: '2026-01', conversion: 0.031, p95: 412 },\n  { month: '2026-02', conversion: 0.034, p95: 388 },\n  { month: '2026-03', conversion: 0.029, p95: 455 },\n  { month: '2026-04', conversion: 0.038, p95: 1210 },\n];\n\n// A ratio (0–1) is declared, never guessed from the field name, and is written as a percentage.\n// Its axis ends at the first tick above the data, not at 100%, because the data stays far below\n// half of full scale.\nline('#ratio', {\n  title: 'Checkout conversion',\n  data: rows,\n  x: 'month',\n  y: { field: 'conversion', label: 'Conversion', scale: 'ratio' },\n});\n\n// A unit follows each value in tooltip, table and announcements, and titles the axis.\n// Ticks and readouts are formatted apart: ticks may be compact, readouts are exact.\nline('#latency', {\n  title: 'Checkout latency',\n  data: rows,\n  x: 'month',\n  y: { field: 'p95', label: 'Latency (p95)', unit: 'ms', tickFormat: { notation: 'compact' } },\n});\n  y: 'sessions',\n})`,\n  },\n  {\n    code: 'AX141', level: 'warning', title: 'Series colours too close to the background',\n    condition: 'The palette is below 3:1 against the resolved background.', drawn: 'As themed.',\n    see: 'The chart is drawn with your colours. The console says that the palette is below 3:1 against the background behind the chart; the diagnostic\\'s `count` says how many series colours are.',\n    why: 'Marks need 3:1 against what is behind them to be seen (WCAG 1.4.11). Axylo measures the colours the browser actually resolved against the colour actually behind the chart, so a dark card on a light page is measured as dark.',\n    fix: 'Choose series colours (`--axylo-series-1` … or `colors`) with at least 3:1 against your background, or set `--axylo-background` to the colour your card really has.',\n    run: 'browser',\n    sample: { chart: 'Sessions', details: { count: 1 } },\n    input: `target.style.setProperty('--axylo-series-1', '#eeeeee');\nline(target, {\n  title: 'Sessions',\n  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n  x: 'day',\n  y: 'sessions',\n});`,\n  },\n  {\n    code: 'AX142', level: 'hint', title: 'Not enough room for a plot',\n    condition: 'The plot would be smaller than 120 × 80 CSS px.', drawn: 'The title, the summary and the table control instead of the plot.',\n    see: 'The chart shows its title, a one-line summary and the \"View as table\" control, so the data stays reachable. In development, the console says the plot is too small.',\n    why: 'A plot that small cannot hold readable axes (L8). Drawing it anyway would produce overlapping or clipped labels.',\n    fix: 'Give the container more room, or use `compact: true` for a sparkline in a small tile.',\n    run: 'browser',\n    sample: { chart: 'Sessions' },\n    input: `target.style.width = '110px';\ntarget.style.height = '90px';\nline(target, {\n  title: 'Sessions',\n  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n  x: 'day',\n  y: 'sessions',\n});`,\n  },\n  {\n    code: 'AX143', level: 'hint', title: 'Labels hidden after every remedy',\n    condition: 'Labels still do not fit after thinning, wrapping, rotating and truncating.', drawn: 'A note tells the reader.',\n    see: 'Some category labels are left out and the chart shows \"Some labels hidden. See table.\" In development, the console suggests horizontal bars.',\n    why: 'Axylo tries, in order: thinning (ordered axes only), wrapping to three lines, rotating up to 45°, and truncating in the middle. It never overlaps labels and never hides one without telling the reader (L1).',\n    fix: 'Give the chart more width, shorten the labels, or use `horizontal: true` on a bar chart, where long labels read naturally. Axylo does not switch orientation for you: that would change what the chart says at a glance.',\n    run: 'browser',\n    sample: { chart: 'Tickets by team', details: { field: 'team', count: 12 } },\n    input: `target.style.width = '320px';\nbar(target, {\n  title: 'Tickets by team',\n  data: Array.from({ length: 30 }, (_, i) => ({ team: 'Customer Success and Onboarding ' + (i + 1), tickets: 10 + i })),\n  x: 'team',\n  y: 'tickets',\n});`,\n  },\n  {\n    code: 'AX150', level: 'warning', title: 'An option Axylo does not know',\n    condition: 'An option key is unknown, or does not apply to the chart type.', drawn: 'The option is ignored; the message suggests the closest known key.',\n    see: 'The chart is drawn without the option. The console names it and, when one is close, the option you probably meant.',\n    why: 'A misspelled option would otherwise be ignored in silence. In TypeScript the same mistake does not compile.',\n    fix: 'Use the name from the message, or check the [options reference](options.md) for the chart type. Options that do not exist on purpose (`width`, `height`, `theme`, `palette`, `animation`) are explained there.',\n    run: 'node',\n    input: `validate('line', {\n  title: 'Sessions',\n  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }],\n  x: 'day',\n  y: 'sessions',\n  tooltips: false,\n})`,\n  },\n  {\n    code: 'AX151', level: 'warning', title: 'Appended rows out of order',\n    condition: '`append` rows are before the last x.', drawn: 'Accepted through the slower full update.',\n    see: 'The rows are added where they belong in x order. The console says they arrived out of order.',\n    why: '`append` is the fast path for rows that extend the series. A row before the last x needs the whole frame rebuilt, which is correct but slower, and usually means the feed is not ordered.',\n    fix: 'Append rows in x order, at or after the last x. To replace data in bulk, use `update({ data })`.',\n    run: 'browser',\n    sample: { chart: 'Sessions', details: { field: 'day', rows: [2] } },\n    input: `const chart = line(target, {\n  title: 'Sessions',\n  data: [{ day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-03', sessions: 130 }],\n  x: 'day',\n  y: 'sessions',\n});\nchart.append([{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 110 }]);`,\n  },\n  {\n    code: 'AX152', level: 'warning', title: 'An unknown time zone',\n    condition: '`timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows.', drawn: 'The viewer\\'s zone is used.',\n    see: 'Instants are shown in the viewer\\'s own zone, which the chart names. The console shows the zone it did not recognise.',\n    why: 'Time zones come from the browser\\'s `Intl` data. A misspelled or retired zone name cannot be resolved.',\n    fix: 'Use an IANA zone name such as `\\'Europe/Paris\\'`, or `\\'UTC\\'`, or leave `timeZone` out for the viewer\\'s zone.',\n    run: 'node',\n    input: `validate('line', {\n  title: 'Requests',\n  data: [{ ts: 1767225600000, n: 3 }, { ts: 1767229200000, n: 5 }],\n  x: { field: 'ts', type: 'time', timeZone: 'Europe/Pariss' },\n  y: 'n',\n})`,\n  },\n  {\n    code: 'AX160', level: 'warning', title: 'A custom formatter threw',\n    condition: 'A custom `format` or `tickFormat` function threw.', drawn: 'The default format for that value.',\n    see: 'The affected text uses Axylo\\'s default format; the reader sees a note that values are shown differently. The console names the field.',\n    why: 'A formatter is your code running inside the chart. One that throws for an unexpected value (a `null`, a negative) must not take the chart down.',\n    fix: 'Make the formatter handle every value of the field, or use an `Intl.NumberFormat` or `Intl.DateTimeFormat` options object instead of a function.',\n    run: 'browser',\n    sample: { chart: 'Sessions', details: { field: 'sessions' } },\n    input: `line(target, {\n  title: 'Sessions',\n  data: [{ day: '2026-01-01', sessions: 120 }, { day: '2026-01-02', sessions: 130 }],\n  x: 'day',\n  y: { field: 'sessions', tickFormat: () => { throw new Error('not ready'); } },\n});`,\n  },\n  {\n    code: 'AX199', level: 'error', title: 'Axylo failed',\n    condition: 'Axylo itself failed while building the chart.', drawn: 'Error state with a generic message.',\n    see: 'The chart shows \"Something went wrong while drawing the chart.\" The error is printed with `console.error` and passed to `onDiagnostic`.',\n    why: 'Something inside Axylo threw. The chart catches it so that the rest of the page keeps working.',\n    fix: 'If your data or options are ordinary, this is a defect in Axylo: please report it with the error printed in the console. The minimal input below is not a defect: it makes reading a row throw, which Axylo reports the same way because it cannot tell the two apart.',\n    run: 'node',\n    input: `validate('line', {\n  title: 'Sessions',\n  data: [{ get day() { throw new Error('not loaded'); }, sessions: 1 }],\n  x: 'day',\n  y: 'sessions',\n})`,\n  },\n];\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
